window.BMN = {"updated": "2026-11-16T11:30:00+03:00", "sections": [["news", "News"], ["previews", "Meister previews"], ["casino", "Casino"], ["sweepstakes", "Sweepstakes"], ["markets", "Prediction markets"], ["crypto", "Crypto"], ["guides", "Guides"]], "proPicks": {}, "bmLive": {}, "record": {"asOf": "20 Nov 2026, 10:09 EAT", "ncm": {"n": 464, "won": 224, "pts": 111.73}, "tom": {"n": 181, "won": 79, "pts": -10.78}}, "offers": [], "articles": [{"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "id": "acca-insurance-and-boosts-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Betting basics", "title": "Acca insurance and odds boosts explained: what they are worth, and how to check one", "dek": "Bookmakers use acca insurance and price boosts to make accumulators look better value. Here is how each works, what the small print usually says, and a simple way to work out what an offer is really worth.", "date": "2026-11-20T13:10:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>Two offers dominate accumulator betting in the UK: <b>acca insurance</b> (your stake back, usually as a free bet, if one leg lets you down) and <b>odds boosts</b> (a bigger price on a selection or a whole acca). Both are real extras, but neither changes the basic maths of an accumulator. This guide explains how they work and how to put a number on them. 18+.</p>", "<h2>First, why accas are hard to win</h2>", "<p>An accumulator multiplies the prices of its legs together, and it also multiplies the bookmaker's margin. If each leg carries a margin of about 5%, a five-leg acca carries roughly 1.05 multiplied by itself five times, or about <b>28%</b> on top of the fair price. That is why accas pay big when they land but lose money over time for most bettors. Insurance and boosts are designed to take the edge off that margin, not remove it.</p>", "<h2>How acca insurance works</h2>", "<p>A typical acca insurance offer says: if <b>exactly one leg</b> of your accumulator loses, you get your stake back, usually up to a cap and usually as a <b>free bet</b> rather than cash. The details vary by bookmaker, so read them, but these are the terms to look for:</p><ul><li><b>Minimum legs</b>: often five or more.</li><li><b>Minimum odds per leg</b>: for example 1.20 or 1.30, so very short favourites do not count.</li><li><b>The cap</b>: the most you can get back.</li><li><b>Free bet or cash</b>: a free bet is worth less than cash, because with a free bet you normally keep only the winnings, not the stake.</li><li><b>Eligible markets</b>: often only pre-match bets on selected leagues or markets.</li></ul>", "<h2>What acca insurance is worth: a worked example</h2>", "<p>Take a five-leg acca, each leg a fair 50:50 chance priced at 1.90, for a £10 stake. The acca pays about 24.8 times your stake.</p><ul><li>Chance all five win: 0.5 to the power of five, about <b>3.1%</b>.</li><li>Chance exactly one leg loses: five ways for that to happen, each 3.1%, so about <b>15.6%</b>.</li></ul><p>So about one acca in six and a half gives you a £10 free bet back. A free bet typically returns about 70% to 80% of its face value as cash if you use it on sensible prices, so call it £7.50. That is worth about 15.6% × £7.50 = <b>£1.17</b> per £10 acca. Without the insurance, this acca returns on average about £7.75 for every £10 staked (3.1% × £248). With the insurance it is about £8.92. Better, but still below the £10 you put in.</p><p>That is the honest summary: acca insurance is worth something, often around a tenth of your stake on a five-fold, but on its own it rarely turns a bad acca into a good one.</p>", "<h2>How odds boosts work</h2>", "<p>An odds boost raises the price on a selection or an acca: a 'price boost' on one result, or a percentage boost on accumulator winnings (for example 10% extra on a five-fold). The question is always the same: <b>is the boosted price above the fair price?</b></p><ul><li>If a team's fair chance is 50% (fair price 2.00), a boost from 1.85 to 2.10 takes the price <b>above</b> fair. That is a genuinely good bet.</li><li>If the fair chance is 40% (fair price 2.50), the same boost from 1.85 to 2.10 is still <b>below</b> fair. It is a smaller loss, not a winner.</li></ul><p>To find the fair chance, use a sharp bookmaker's or the exchange's price with the margin removed. Our guide to <a href=\"betting-odds-explained.html\">betting odds</a> shows how.</p><p>Percentage acca boosts work the same way as insurance: they add something to every winning acca, but because accas win rarely, a 10% boost on winnings is worth much less than 10% of your stake.</p>", "<h2>A quick checklist</h2>", "<ol><li><b>Read the terms</b>: minimum legs and odds, the cap, and whether you get cash or a free bet.</li><li><b>Price the legs</b>: are they fair on their own? Insurance does not fix legs priced well below fair.</li><li><b>Do the sum</b>: chance of one leg losing × the cash value of the refund, or the boosted price against the fair price.</li><li><b>Keep accas small</b>: fewer legs means less margin stacked up, and insurance or a boost then covers more of it.</li></ol>", "<h2>The rules that protect you</h2>", "<p>In Great Britain, insurance and boosts are promotions, so the Gambling Commission's promotion rules apply: the terms must be clear and fair, and since 19 January 2026 wagering requirements on bonuses have been capped. You can set deposit limits, take a break or self-exclude at any time, including across all licensed sites through GAMSTOP. Our guide to <a href=\"free-bets-explained.html\">free bets</a> covers how free-bet refunds are paid.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["UK Gambling Commission: Gambling promotions to be safer and simpler (wagering cap from 19 January 2026)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/gambling-promotions-to-be-safer-and-simpler"], ["GAMSTOP: free self-exclusion from online gambling in Great Britain", "https://www.gamstop.co.uk/"], ["BetMeisters calculations: acca margin, acca insurance and odds-boost worked examples", "https://betmeisters.news/betting-odds-explained.html"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["American football", "Basketball", "Ice hockey"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "us-sports-thursday-1911", "section": "previews", "kicker": "US sports · Thursday night 19 November", "title": "Thursday night in the US: Texans v Colts, Knicks at Minnesota, a full NHL slate and Houston v Kansas", "dek": "Thursday Night Football, four NBA games, 12 NHL games and 26 college basketball games. Pinnacle's chances for the main ones, with the margin removed, in UK and US Eastern times.", "date": "2026-11-19T21:31:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>A busy Thursday night in American sport: Thursday Night Football, four NBA games, a full NHL slate and 26 men's college basketball games. Every chance below is <b>Pinnacle's price with the margin removed</b>, taken through odds-api.io on Thursday evening. Times are UK first (late Thursday night and early Friday morning), then US Eastern. For basketball and the NFL the main markets are the point spread and the total, so we give those too.</p>", "<h2>NFL: Houston Texans v Indianapolis Colts (1.15am UK, 8.15pm ET)</h2><p>Thursday Night Football is a one-sided game on the numbers. Pinnacle makes the Texans <b>86% to win</b> (fair price 1.16) and the Colts 14%. The point spread is <b>Texans −12.5</b>, which the market treats as close to a coin flip, and the total is <b>44 points</b>, also very close to 50-50. A 12.5-point spread is a big number in the NFL: the market expects the Texans to win, but by how much is the real question.</p>", "<h2>NBA: four games at 1am UK (8pm ET)</h2><h3>Minnesota Timberwolves v New York Knicks</h3><p>The closest game of the night. Pinnacle has the Knicks <b>51%</b> and the Timberwolves 49%, with the Knicks a 1-point favourite on the road. The total is 215.5, the lowest of the four NBA games.</p><h3>Memphis Grizzlies v Golden State Warriors</h3><p>Memphis are <b>69%</b> at home (fair price 1.45), with a spread of Grizzlies −7 and a total of 224.5.</p><h3>San Antonio Spurs v LA Clippers</h3><p>The biggest favourites in the NBA tonight: the Spurs are <b>89%</b> (fair price 1.12) and −15.5 on the spread. The Clippers are about 11%.</p><h3>Milwaukee Bucks v Portland Trail Blazers</h3><p>The number that stands out on the NBA card: Pinnacle makes the visiting Trail Blazers <b>80%</b> to win in Milwaukee, with Portland −11 on the spread. Away favourites at that size are unusual, so this is a price worth checking against the team news before tip-off. The total is 230.5, the highest of the night.</p>", "<h2>NHL: 12 games from midnight UK (7pm ET)</h2><p>Hockey markets are tight, and tonight's card is no exception: ten of the 12 games have no side better than 61%.</p><ul><li><b>Carolina Hurricanes v Montreal Canadiens</b> (midnight UK): Carolina are the night's biggest favourites at <b>74%</b>.</li><li><b>Calgary Flames v Minnesota Wild</b> (1am UK): Minnesota <b>69%</b> away; Calgary 31%.</li><li><b>Seattle Kraken v Vancouver Canucks</b> (2.40am UK): Seattle <b>61%</b>.</li><li><b>Columbus at Chicago</b> (1am UK): Columbus <b>60%</b> on the road, and the total is 5.5 goals, one of only two games below 6.5.</li><li><b>Ottawa v Philadelphia</b> (midnight UK): Ottawa <b>59%</b>.</li><li><b>Tampa Bay v Edmonton</b> (12.30am UK): Tampa Bay <b>57%</b>.</li><li><b>Washington v Buffalo</b> (midnight UK): Washington <b>56%</b>.</li><li><b>Colorado v Anaheim</b> (2am UK): Colorado <b>55%</b>.</li><li><b>Vegas v Dallas</b> (3am UK): Vegas <b>53%</b>, total 5.5.</li><li><b>Toronto v Los Angeles</b> and <b>Winnipeg v the Islanders</b> (both midnight UK): 52% and 53% for the home sides.</li><li><b>Pittsburgh v St. Louis</b> (midnight UK): a dead heat, <b>50% each</b>.</li></ul><p>One betting note: some UK bookmakers' hockey totals are for regular time only (60 minutes), while Pinnacle's main line includes overtime and shootouts. Check which one you are betting before comparing prices.</p>", "<h2>College basketball: the best of 26 games</h2><p><b>Houston v Kansas</b> (4am UK Friday, 11pm ET) is the headline game: Houston <b>59%</b>, Kansas 41%, total 150.5. Earlier, <b>Notre Dame v Auburn</b> (10pm UK, 5pm ET) has Auburn <b>59%</b> away from home. The in-state <b>Oregon v Oregon State</b> game (2am UK) is close to a coin flip at 52% to 48%, as are <b>TCU v Texas A&M</b> and <b>Drake v College of Charleston</b>. <b>Wisconsin</b> are 65% against Seton Hall (1.30am UK).</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, via odds-api.io, at about 6pm UK on Thursday 19 November. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via odds-api.io, 19 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Tennis"], "previewType": "event", "id": "atp-finals-semis-preview-2026", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Tennis · ATP Finals, Turin", "title": "ATP Finals semi-finals: Alcaraz and Zverev are through, and four players chase the last two places", "dek": "Carlos Alcaraz and Alexander Zverev have both won their first two group matches in Turin. Thursday night and Friday decide who joins them in Saturday's semi-finals, and the market has clear favourites for both places.", "date": "2026-11-19T18:17:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>The ATP Finals, the season-ending event for the year's best eight singles players, reach the end of the round-robin stage this week in Turin. Two players are already in Saturday's semi-finals: <b>Carlos Alcaraz</b> and <b>Alexander Zverev</b>, who are both 2-0. The last group matches, on Thursday night and Friday, decide who joins them. The format is simple: the top two in each group of four go through, and each group winner plays the other group's runner-up in the semi-finals.</p><p>The chances below are from <b>Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed</b>, taken through odds-api.io on Thursday afternoon, 19 November. Results and standings are from Tennis Majors and the ATP Tour. Times are UK (Eastern five hours earlier).</p>", "<h2>Group McEnroe: Zverev, Fils, Shelton, Jodar</h2><p><b>Zverev</b> beat Rafael Jodar 6-4, 6-4 and Ben Shelton 6-2, 7-6(4), so he is through. Arthur Fils and Ben Shelton are 1-1: Shelton beat Fils in three sets, and Fils came from a set down to beat Jodar. Jodar is 0-2 but not yet out.</p><p>The group finishes on Thursday: <b>Shelton v Jodar</b>, then <b>Zverev v Fils</b> at 7.30pm UK (2.30pm Eastern). Pinnacle makes Zverev <b>70%</b> to beat Fils (fair price 1.43). According to Tennis Majors' qualification scenarios:</p><ul><li>If <b>Zverev beats Fils</b>, Zverev wins the group, and Shelton takes second place if he has beaten Jodar.</li><li>If <b>Fils beats Zverev</b>, Fils usually qualifies, but he can still go out if he needs three sets and Shelton beats Jodar in straight sets.</li><li><b>Jodar</b> qualifies only if he beats Shelton in straight sets and Zverev beats Fils in straight sets. If the three players are level on matches and sets, games won decide it.</li></ul><p>In short, the likeliest outcome on the numbers is Zverev first and Shelton or Fils second, and Thursday's sets, not just the results, may decide which.</p>", "<h2>Group Lendl: Alcaraz, Tiafoe, Medvedev, Cobolli</h2><p><b>Alcaraz</b> has dropped only ten games in two matches: he beat Frances Tiafoe 6-2, 6-2 and Daniil Medvedev 6-2, 6-4. He is certain to finish first whatever happens against Flavio Cobolli on Friday at 1pm UK (8am Eastern), where Pinnacle makes him <b>82%</b> (fair price 1.22). Italy's Cobolli lost to Medvedev and Tiafoe and is out.</p><p>That leaves a straight shoot-out for second place: <b>Medvedev v Tiafoe</b> on Friday at 7.30pm UK (2.30pm Eastern), and the winner goes through. Pinnacle makes <b>Medvedev 67%</b> (fair price 1.49) and <b>Tiafoe 33%</b> (fair price 3.06). Medvedev beat Cobolli 7-6, 6-3; Tiafoe beat him 7-6(6), 6-0.</p>", "<h2>Who plays whom on Saturday</h2><p>The semi-finals are on Saturday 21 November:</p><ul><li><b>Alcaraz</b> (Group Lendl winner) v the Group McEnroe runner-up: most likely Shelton or Fils.</li><li>The Group McEnroe winner (most likely <b>Zverev</b>, 70% to beat Fils) v the Group Lendl runner-up: Medvedev (67%) or Tiafoe (33%).</li></ul><p>On the market's numbers, the single most likely semi-final line-up is Alcaraz against Shelton or Fils, and Zverev against Medvedev. A Zverev v Medvedev semi-final would be a meeting of two players who have both reached Grand Slam finals and both won this title before. We will publish full previews once the line-up is set on Friday night, with fresh prices.</p>", "<h2>What the numbers say</h2><p>Alcaraz has won both his matches in straight sets and is already sure of first place in his group. The bigger questions are in the other half: whether Zverev can win his group, and whether Medvedev or Tiafoe takes the last place. Both Thursday's and Friday's evening matches could change the shape of Saturday's semi-finals.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, via odds-api.io, at about 3pm UK on Thursday 19 November. Results and qualification scenarios from Tennis Majors (17 November) and the ATP Tour. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Tennis Majors: ATP Finals 2026 qualification scenarios", "https://www.tennismajors.com/atp/nitto-atp-finals-atp/atp-finals-2026-all-the-qualification-scenarios-867176.html"], ["ATP Tour: Nitto ATP Finals", "https://www.atptour.com/en/scores/current/nitto-atp-finals/605/live-scores"], ["Pinnacle prices via odds-api.io, 19 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "daily-football", "id": "football-friday-2011", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Football · Friday 20 November", "title": "Friday's football: Bayern v Köln, Athletic v Espanyol, a Yorkshire derby in League One and Al Ahli v Al Nassr", "dek": "The big leagues return on Friday night. Bayern Munich are 92% to beat Köln, Barnsley host Sheffield Wednesday, and the Saudi Pro League has its biggest game of the round. Every chance is Pinnacle's, with the margin removed.", "date": "2026-11-19T18:16:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>The international break is over and club football is back on Friday 20 November. Most of the games that matter to UK and US viewers kick off between 5.30pm and 8pm UK time (12.30pm to 3pm Eastern). Every chance below is from <b>Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed</b>, taken on Thursday afternoon through odds-api.io. The fair price is one divided by the chance.</p>", "<div class=\"update\"><h3>Updated 10:10 UK: the morning market</h3><p>Pinnacle's prices this morning (margin removed) have moved a little since Thursday. The biggest change is in Saudi Arabia: <b>Al Nassr</b> have eased from 49% to about <b>46%</b> at Al Ahli (Al Ahli about 31%, the draw about 23%). In Serie B, <b>Hellas Verona</b> are now about <b>55%</b> against Cesena (59% on Thursday). <b>Porto</b> have shortened to about <b>89%</b> in the Taça de Portugal.</p><p>The rest are close to Thursday's numbers: <b>Athletic Club</b> about 65% against Espanyol, <b>Troyes</b> about 44% against Le Havre, <b>Sheffield Wednesday</b> about 46% at Barnsley (over 2.5 goals now about 65%), <b>Cercle Brugge</b> about 49%, <b>Heracles</b> about 85% and three or more goals in Bayern v Köln still about 86%.</p><p>We have not added team news: none of it had been confirmed by a source we trust at the time of writing.</p></div>", "<h2>Bundesliga: Bayern Munich v Köln (7.30pm UK)</h2><p>The most one-sided game of the night. Pinnacle makes Bayern <b>92%</b> to win (fair price 1.09), the draw about 6% and Köln about 3%. It also makes three or more goals <b>86%</b> likely, the highest goals expectation of any big game this weekend. At these numbers the question for most bettors is not who wins but by how much.</p>", "<h2>LaLiga: Athletic Club v Espanyol (8pm UK)</h2><p>Athletic are <b>65%</b> favourites (fair price 1.54) at San Mamés, with the draw at 21% and Espanyol at 14%. Three or more goals is close to a coin flip at 53%.</p>", "<h2>Ligue 1: Troyes v Le Havre (7.45pm UK)</h2><p>A tighter game to open the French weekend: Troyes <b>44%</b>, the draw 28%, Le Havre 28%. Over 2.5 goals is 51%.</p>", "<h2>England: Barnsley v Sheffield Wednesday (8pm UK)</h2><p>A South Yorkshire derby in League One under the Friday lights. Pinnacle makes the visitors favourites: <b>Sheffield Wednesday 45%</b>, the draw 24%, Barnsley 32%. In the National League, <b>Boreham Wood</b> are 46% at Southend (7.45pm), with three or more goals 71% likely.</p>", "<h2>Saudi Pro League: Al Ahli v Al Nassr (5.30pm UK)</h2><p>The biggest game of the Saudi round. Al Nassr are <b>49%</b> to win away at Al Ahli, the draw is 22% and Al Ahli 29%. Over 2.5 goals is 65%.</p>", "<h2>The rest of Europe</h2><p><b>Germany:</b> Heidenheim are 49% at Darmstadt and Arminia Bielefeld 44% at home to Magdeburg in 2. Bundesliga (both 5.30pm). <b>Italy:</b> Hellas Verona are 59% at home to Cesena in Serie B (7.30pm). <b>Spain:</b> Andorra are 52% against Leganés in LaLiga 2 (7.30pm). <b>Netherlands:</b> a full Eerste Divisie round at 7pm, with Heracles 85% at home to Jong PSV and three or more goals 82% likely. <b>Belgium:</b> Cercle Brugge are 47% against Westerlo (7.45pm). <b>Scotland:</b> Inverness are 60% at home to Ayr in the Championship (7.30pm). <b>Portugal:</b> Porto are 87% at home to Paredes in the Taça de Portugal (8.45pm).</p>", "<h2>Later on</h2><p>In Argentina's Clausura play-offs, Vélez Sarsfield are 44% at home to Belgrano (11pm UK, 6pm Eastern), and in Mexico Puebla v Atlético San Luis (1am UK Saturday, 8pm Eastern Friday) is close: Puebla 38%, San Luis 35%.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, via odds-api.io, at about 3pm UK on Thursday 19 November. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via odds-api.io, 19 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]], "updated": "2026-11-20T13:10:00+03:00"}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "previewType": "racing-card", "id": "racing-card-2011", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Racing card · Friday 20 November", "title": "Friday's racing: Ascot's jumps card, Chepstow and Catterick, then Wolverhampton and Dundalk under lights", "dek": "Five meetings and 39 races. Ascot's seven-race jumps card carries the best prize money, with Nicky Henderson's Crystal Island a short favourite in the opener, and the all-weather takes over from 4.30pm. Every meeting, the early market and what to watch.", "date": "2026-11-19T18:12:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>Friday's racing in Britain and Ireland runs from 11.20am to 8.30pm UK time (6.20am to 3.30pm Eastern) across five meetings and 39 races. Three are over jumps in the daytime: <b>Chepstow</b> (eight races from 11.20am), <b>Catterick</b> (seven from 12pm, good ground) and <b>Ascot</b> (seven from 12.10pm, good ground). In the evening the action moves to the all-weather at <b>Wolverhampton</b> (nine races, 4.30pm to 8.30pm) and <b>Dundalk</b> in Ireland (eight, 4.45pm to 8.15pm).</p><p>On Thursday afternoon only Ascot had been priced, and only by a single bookmaker, so the chances below are that bookmaker's early prices with its margin removed (its book on these races runs from 108% to 126%). The exchanges had not formed a market on any of Friday's races, so treat these as a first view, not a verdict. Runners, riders, ratings and form are from The Racing API racecards.</p>", "<div class=\"update\"><h3>Updated 10:10 UK: non-runners, going and the morning market</h3><p><b>Ascot 12.10 is now a walkover-style one-horse race.</b> Both <b>Crystal Island</b>, last night's 8/15 favourite, and <b>Gee Force Flyer</b> are non-runners, leaving Joe Tizzard's <b>Etna Bianco</b> as the only declared runner.</p><p><b>Other non-runners:</b> Prometeo (Ascot 1.20, now 5 runners), Gwen's Girl (Catterick 12.00), Jet To London and Moments Away (Catterick 12.33, now 7), Sunset Hill (Catterick 1.43), Auburn Avenue (Chepstow 12.22), Arctic Conditions and Annie Express (Chepstow 2.07), Snowy Hill (Chepstow 3.17), Valor's Call, Scarlett's Spell, Passerine and Dapper Guest (Wolverhampton 5.00, 6.00, 7.00 and 7.30).</p><p><b>Going:</b> Ascot and Catterick are still <b>good</b>. Chepstow's card now lists its hurdle races as <b>soft</b> and its chases as <b>good</b>. Wolverhampton and Dundalk are standard.</p><p><b>The morning market</b> (the median price across about 25 bookmakers this morning, against a single early bookmaker last night):</p><ul><li><b>Ascot 12.45:</b> <b>No Way Jay</b> is now a clear evens favourite; <b>Current Edition</b> has drifted to about 6/4. They were joint 11/8 last night.</li><li><b>Ascot 1.20:</b> the market has turned round. <b>Spanish Stroll</b> is now favourite at about 5/4, with <b>Push The Limit</b> (last night's 6/5 favourite) about 13/8.</li><li><b>Ascot 1.55</b> (the day's most valuable race in Britain): <b>Western Knight</b> is now favourite at about 2/1, just ahead of <b>Jet To Vegas</b> at about 9/4 (last night's favourite) and <b>Crebilly</b> at 4/1.</li><li><b>Ascot 2.30:</b> <b>Wondering Why</b> (Ben Pauling) now heads the betting at about 3/1, with Moveit Like Minnie about 7/2.</li><li><b>Ascot 3.05:</b> <b>Draco Malfoy</b> (Kim Bailey and Mat Nicholls) is favourite at about 7/2.</li><li><b>Ascot 3.40:</b> <b>Power Steering</b> has shortened to about 4/5.</li><li><b>Chepstow 12.57</b> (the day's best race there): <b>Ben Solo</b> is about 3/1, with Wilstar and Largy Go both about 4/1.</li><li><b>Catterick 12.00:</b> Dan Skelton's <b>Sacre Chef</b> is a short favourite at about 8/15.</li><li><b>Wolverhampton 7.30</b> (Class 2 handicap): <b>Asuka</b> (George Boughey, Billy Loughnane) is about 13/8.</li></ul><p>Prices are from The Racing API's racecards at about 10:10 UK. They move all morning; check before you bet.</p></div>", "<h2>Ascot: the day's best card</h2><h3>12.10 · Beginners' chase, 2m5f (3 runners, Class 2, £10,562)</h3><p>Only three go to post, and Nicky Henderson's <b>Crystal Island</b> (Nico de Boinville, rated 133, form 1/11-) is the clear favourite at 8/15 on his first run for 213 days. With the margin removed that is about <b>61%</b>. Olly Murphy's <b>Gee Force Flyer</b> (125, form 32231-) is about 21% and Joe Tizzard's <b>Etna Bianco</b> (126) about 19%. It is a beginners' chase, so none of them has won over fences yet, and all three return from breaks of more than 200 days.</p>", "<h3>12.45 · Maiden hurdle, 2m3f (8 runners, Class 3, £13,615)</h3><p>A two-horse market: Tizzard's <b>No Way Jay</b> (Freddie Gingell, second last time 20 days ago) and the Twiston-Davies yard's <b>Current Edition</b> (rated 119, form 532-22) are both 11/8, about <b>35%</b> each. Current Edition has finished second in each of his last three runs.</p>", "<h3>1.20 · Introductory hurdle, 1m7f (6 runners, Class 2, £15,843)</h3><p>A race for horses starting out over hurdles. Dan Skelton's <b>Push The Limit</b> (Tristan Durrell), third on his only run 29 days ago, is 6/5, about <b>40%</b>. Tizzard's <b>Spanish Stroll</b> (form 90-1) is about 27% and the O'Neills' <b>Without Burlington</b>, a winner on his only start, about 17% on his return from 121 days off.</p>", "<h3>1.55 · Handicap chase, 2m5f (6 runners, Class 2, £18,210)</h3><p>The most valuable race of the day in Britain, and a competitive one: the top four are all rated 135 or 136. <b>Jet To Vegas</b> (Lucinda Russell and Michael Scudamore, Derek Fox, form 86P-12) is the early favourite at 9/4, about <b>27%</b>. Tizzard's <b>Western Knight</b> (about 22%) returns from 253 days off; the O'Neills' <b>Crebilly</b> (form 142131) and Jedd O'Keeffe's <b>Fringill Dike</b> (form -61221) are in-form handicappers at about 20% and 17%.</p>", "<h3>2.30 · Handicap hurdle, 2m (8 runners, Class 3, £15,947)</h3><p>Wide open: no runner is better than about 18%. <b>Moveit Like Minnie</b> (Twiston-Davies, 7lb claimer Amber Jackson-Fennell) heads the betting at 7/2, from Skelton's <b>Gambino</b>, Paul Nicholls' <b>Fasol</b> (who ran six days ago) and Daisy Hitchins' top-rated <b>Intellotto</b> (128, won last time).</p>", "<h3>3.05 and 3.40 · A big-field chase and a bumper</h3><p>The 3.05 is a ten-runner handicap chase over nearly three miles with no favourite shorter than 11/2: the O'Neills' <b>Yes Day</b> (form 324131, ran nine days ago) heads it. The card closes with a bumper in which Harry Fry's <b>Power Steering</b>, second on his only run, is about <b>31%</b>.</p>", "<h2>Chepstow and Catterick</h2><p>Neither card had been priced when we wrote this, so here are the ratings and form. At Chepstow, the most valuable race is the <b>12.57 handicap chase</b> (Class 3, 11 runners), where <b>Authentic Legacy</b> (the Moores) and Rebecca Curtis' <b>Ben Solo</b> top the weights on 126, with Kim Bailey and Mat Nicholls' <b>Sogna In Grande</b> (125) and Olly Murphy's <b>Wilstar</b> (125, form /8211-) just below. Two divisions of a novices' hurdle (11.50 and 12.22) and a maiden hurdle at 11.20 give a first look at a few jumpers for the winter; Nicholls' <b>Exciting Ride</b> and Kim Bailey's <b>Hidden Mountain</b> (form 21-1) are among the runners.</p><p>Catterick's best race is the <b>2.18</b>, a three-mile Class 3 handicap chase for horses who came from point-to-points, with Richard Hobson's <b>Some Scope</b> top-rated on 119. The <b>12.33</b> novices' hurdle includes the O'Neills' <b>Nightflyer</b> (rated 112, form -52122) and Gary Hanmer's <b>I've Got A Friend</b> (form U2-112). The <b>2.53</b> is a handicap chase for female jockeys.</p>", "<h2>Wolverhampton: the All-Weather Championships handicap</h2><p>The evening's feature is the <b>7.30 All-Weather Championship Handicap</b> over seven furlongs (Class 2, £12,988, 10 runners). Ian Williams' <b>Dandy Style</b> (Clifford Lee) tops the ratings on 101 with form 264231 but has been off for 155 days. George Boughey's <b>Asuka</b> (Billy Loughnane, 97, form 211341) ran 22 days ago. Before that, two divisions of a fillies' maiden (5.30 and 6pm) bring out runners from Ralph Beckett (<b>Lady Phillips</b>, second in both her starts) and the Gosdens (<b>Zhuri</b>, rated 75).</p>", "<h2>Dundalk: eight races under lights</h2><p>The best prize money at Dundalk is in the two Irish Stallion Farms EBF maidens: the <b>6.45</b> for colts and geldings and the <b>7.15</b> for fillies (€13,200 each). The fillies' race has several unraced runners from big yards, including Henry de Bromhead's <b>Always Be Mine</b>, Jim Bolger's <b>An Tuath Nua</b> and Joseph O'Brien's <b>Arddin</b>. The <b>6.15</b> rated race (€12,000) has Joseph O'Brien's <b>Celtic Motif</b> top-rated on 88, with Denis Hogan's <b>Apache Outlaw</b> (84, form 396951, Colin Keane) next.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Racecards and early prices from The Racing API, Thursday 19 November about 3pm UK. Chances are one bookmaker's early prices with the margin removed, Ascot only. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["The Racing API: racecards for Friday 20 November 2026", "https://www.theracingapi.com"], ["The Racing API: racecards for Friday 20 November (morning update)", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]], "updated": "2026-11-20T13:10:00+03:00"}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "weekend-football", "id": "weekend-football-gw11-1911", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Weekend football · Premier League Gameweek 11", "title": "Weekend football preview: Newcastle v Arsenal, Liverpool v Man Utd and every Premier League game in numbers", "dek": "The Premier League is back after the international break. Arsenal lead by five points, two of the bottom three go to top-half sides, Coventry host bottom-placed Palace, and Pinnacle's prices put a number on every game. Plus the big matches in Spain, Italy, Germany and France.", "date": "2026-11-19T18:03:00+03:00", "read": 7, "lead": true, "leadUntil": "2026-11-20T18:03:00+03:00", "body": ["<p>The Premier League returns this weekend after the November international break with Gameweek 11: seven games on Saturday 21 November, two on Sunday and Brentford v Everton on Monday night. The headline game is <b>Newcastle v Arsenal</b> at 5.30pm UK on Saturday (12.30pm Eastern), and Sunday brings <b>Liverpool v Manchester United</b> at 4.30pm UK (11.30am Eastern).</p><p>Every chance in this preview comes from <b>Pinnacle's prices with the bookmaker's margin removed</b>, taken through odds-api.io on Thursday afternoon, 19 November. Pinnacle is the sharpest bookmaker in the world and takes big bets from professionals, so its prices are the best public estimate of how likely each result is. Next to each chance we give the <b>fair price</b> (one divided by the chance) and the best price we found at the big UK bookmakers. When the best price is shorter than the fair price, the bookmakers are taking a margin on that result. This weekend that is true of every result on the Premier League card.</p>", "<h2>The table after ten games</h2><p><b>Arsenal</b> lead on 27 points from 10 games: nine wins and one defeat. <b>Brighton</b> are second on 22, with the best goal difference in the league (+19), and <b>Manchester City</b> are third on 21. <b>Liverpool</b> are fourth on 18, and <b>Chelsea</b> and <b>Everton</b> are level on 16.</p><p>At the bottom, <b>Crystal Palace</b> are 20th on 7 points with a goal difference of −14. <b>Sunderland</b> and <b>Manchester United</b> have 8 points each. <b>Coventry</b> and <b>Nottingham Forest</b> are just above them on 9. Only three points separate 15th-placed Fulham (10) from the bottom, so six teams are in the relegation fight. (Table after Gameweek 10, which ended on 8 November.)</p>", "<h2>Every game in numbers</h2><p>Fair chances from Pinnacle, margin removed. 'Over 2.5' is the chance of three or more goals.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Kick-off (UK)</th><th>Match</th><th>Home</th><th>Draw</th><th>Away</th><th>Over 2.5</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sat 12:30</td><td>Man City v Fulham</td><td>69%</td><td>18%</td><td>13%</td><td>65%</td></tr><tr><td>Sat 15:00</td><td>Aston Villa v Sunderland</td><td>54%</td><td>25%</td><td>21%</td><td>55%</td></tr><tr><td>Sat 15:00</td><td>Bournemouth v Nottm Forest</td><td>49%</td><td>26%</td><td>24%</td><td>51%</td></tr><tr><td>Sat 15:00</td><td>Chelsea v Leeds</td><td>51%</td><td>26%</td><td>24%</td><td>53%</td></tr><tr><td>Sat 15:00</td><td>Coventry v Crystal Palace</td><td>39%</td><td>27%</td><td>35%</td><td>56%</td></tr><tr><td>Sat 15:00</td><td>Tottenham v Ipswich</td><td>63%</td><td>21%</td><td>16%</td><td>60%</td></tr><tr><td>Sat 17:30</td><td>Newcastle v Arsenal</td><td>16%</td><td>22%</td><td>62%</td><td>56%</td></tr><tr><td>Sun 14:00</td><td>Hull v Brighton</td><td>15%</td><td>21%</td><td>65%</td><td>58%</td></tr><tr><td>Sun 16:30</td><td>Liverpool v Man Utd</td><td>55%</td><td>23%</td><td>23%</td><td>65%</td></tr><tr><td>Mon 20:00</td><td>Brentford v Everton</td><td>45%</td><td>27%</td><td>28%</td><td>54%</td></tr></tbody></table>", "<h2>The big one: Newcastle v Arsenal (Saturday 5.30pm UK)</h2><p>The leaders go to St James' Park. Newcastle are 12th on 12 points, fifteen behind Arsenal, and the market makes Arsenal clear favourites: <b>62% to win</b> (fair price 1.62), with the draw at 22% and a Newcastle win at 16% (fair price 6.13). The best UK price on Arsenal is 1.60, almost exactly fair, which tells you how closely the bookmakers are watching this game. Pinnacle makes three or more goals 56% likely.</p><p>A 62% favourite still fails to win almost two times in five. Arsenal have dropped only three points all season, so a Newcastle win or a draw would be the biggest shake-up of the weekend. We previewed this game in full earlier in the week.</p>", "<h2>Liverpool v Manchester United (Sunday 4.30pm UK)</h2><p>Liverpool are fourth, United are 18th, and ten points separate them. Pinnacle makes Liverpool <b>55%</b> (fair price 1.83), with the draw and a United win both at about <b>23%</b> (fair price about 4.4). At 65%, Over 2.5 goals is the joint-highest goals expectation of the weekend, level with Manchester City v Fulham. United go into the game in the relegation zone on 8 points; a draw would take them level with Coventry and Forest on 9. Our full preview of this game is in the previews section.</p>", "<h2>The relegation six-pointer: Coventry v Crystal Palace (Saturday 3pm UK)</h2><p>The tightest game on the card. Coventry (17th, 9 points) host bottom-placed Palace (7 points), and Pinnacle barely separates them: <b>Coventry 39%</b>, the draw 27%, <b>Palace 35%</b>. A Palace win would take them to 10 points and above Coventry, who would stay on 9. The best UK prices (2.45, 3.60 and 2.80) are all a little shorter than fair, so there is no generous price in a game the market finds hard to call.</p>", "<h2>The rest of Saturday</h2><h3>Manchester City v Fulham (12.30pm UK)</h3><p>The biggest home favourites of the weekend: City are <b>69%</b> (fair price 1.44) to beat Fulham, and three or more goals is 65% likely. City are third, six points behind Arsenal, so they need to keep pace before the leaders play at Newcastle later in the day. The best UK price, 1.40, is shorter than fair.</p><h3>Tottenham v Ipswich (3pm UK)</h3><p>Spurs (11th, 12 points) are <b>63%</b> favourites (fair price 1.59) against Ipswich, who are 14th on 11. Over 2.5 goals is 60%.</p><h3>Aston Villa v Sunderland (3pm UK)</h3><p>Villa are <b>54%</b> (fair price 1.84) at home to 19th-placed Sunderland, with the draw at 25% and a Sunderland win at 21%. Villa are tenth on 13 points.</p><h3>Chelsea v Leeds (3pm UK)</h3><p>Fifth against ninth, and a closer game than the table suggests: Chelsea <b>51%</b> (fair price 1.96), the draw 26%, Leeds 24%. Leeds have drawn five of their ten games.</p><h3>Bournemouth v Nottingham Forest (3pm UK)</h3><p>Bournemouth (seventh, 15 points) are <b>49%</b> (fair price 2.04) against Forest, who are 16th on 9 points and only a point above the drop zone. The draw is 26% and a Forest win 24%. Goals are close to a coin flip at 51% for three or more.</p>", "<h2>Sunday and Monday</h2><h3>Hull v Brighton (Sunday 2pm UK)</h3><p>Second-placed Brighton are the biggest away favourites of the weekend at <b>65%</b> (fair price 1.55) at Hull, who are 13th. A Hull win is 15% (fair price 6.80). Brighton's +19 goal difference is the best in the league.</p><h3>Brentford v Everton (Monday 8pm UK, 3pm Eastern)</h3><p>Eighth against sixth, two points apart. Brentford are <b>45%</b> (fair price 2.22), the draw 27% and Everton 28%. Brentford have drawn five of their ten games, Everton four.</p>", "<h2>Around Europe</h2><h3>Spain: Sevilla v Real Betis (Saturday 5.30pm UK)</h3><p>The Seville derby. Pinnacle makes <b>Betis 40%</b> and Sevilla 32%, with the draw at 28%. Elsewhere in LaLiga, Real Madrid are 84% to beat Celta Vigo on Saturday night (8pm UK), Barcelona are 81% at home to Villarreal on Sunday lunchtime (1pm UK), and Atlético Madrid are 62% at Getafe on Sunday evening (8pm UK).</p><h3>Italy: Napoli v Torino (Saturday 7.45pm UK)</h3><p>Napoli are 65% favourites at home to Torino. AC Milan (71% v Sassuolo), Lazio (71% v Lecce) and Como (75% v Cagliari) all play at 2pm UK on Saturday, and Juventus are 72% at home to Venezia on Sunday (11.30am UK).</p><h3>Germany: Gladbach v Dortmund and Frankfurt v Hoffenheim (Saturday 2.30pm UK)</h3><p>Bayern Munich start the weekend on Friday night at home to Köln (7.30pm UK) as 92% favourites, with three or more goals 86% likely. On Saturday, Dortmund are 62% at Borussia Mönchengladbach, and Frankfurt v Hoffenheim is the most open of the big games: Frankfurt 41%, Hoffenheim 35%, with three or more goals 71% likely.</p><h3>France: Nice v PSG (Saturday 4.15pm UK)</h3><p>Paris Saint-Germain are 67% favourites at Nice, and Pinnacle makes three or more goals 69% likely. Lille v Lyon at 7.45pm UK is closer: Lille 42%, the draw 29%, Lyon 29%.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, via odds-api.io, at about 3pm UK on Thursday 19 November; best UK prices from the same feed. Table after Gameweek 10. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via odds-api.io, 19 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"], ["Within Nigeria: Premier League table after 10 games (9 Nov 2026)", "https://www.withinnigeria.com/2026/11/09/premier-league-table-after-10-games-arsenal-top-on-27-points-brighton-second-as-man-united-fall-into-the-relegation-zone/"]]}, {"topics": ["Fantasy football", "Football"], "sports": ["Football"], "id": "fpl-for-beginners", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Fantasy Premier League", "title": "FPL for beginners: the squad, the budget, the points and the five decisions that matter most", "dek": "Fantasy Premier League in plain English: how the £100m squad works, how players score, what transfers and chips are for, and the simple habits that separate a good season from a frustrating one.", "date": "2026-11-19T16:35:00+03:00", "read": 8, "body": ["<p>Fantasy Premier League (FPL) is the official, free fantasy game of the Premier League, and millions of people play it every season. You pick a squad of real players, they score points from what they do on the pitch each gameweek, and you make changes as the season goes on. It is not betting: there is no stake and nothing to lose but pride. This guide covers the rules you need for 2026/27 and the decisions that matter most.</p>", "<h2>The squad: 15 players, £100m, three per club</h2>", "<p>Every manager starts with a <b>£100.0m budget</b> to buy a <b>15-man squad</b>: <b>2 goalkeepers, 5 defenders, 5 midfielders and 3 forwards</b>. You can pick <b>no more than three players from any one Premier League club</b>. New for this season, the game can build a starting squad for you automatically if you would rather start from a template and edit it.</p>", "<p>Each gameweek you choose a <b>starting XI</b> from your 15. It must have one goalkeeper, at least three defenders, at least two midfielders and at least one forward, so 3-4-3, 3-5-2, 4-3-3, 4-4-2, 4-5-1, 5-2-3, 5-3-2 and 5-4-1 are all allowed. The other four sit on your bench in an order you set.</p>", "<h2>How players score</h2>", "<table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plays up to 60 minutes / 60 minutes or more</td><td>1 / 2</td></tr><tr><td>Goal: goalkeeper / defender / midfielder / forward</td><td>10 / 6 / 5 / 4</td></tr><tr><td>Assist</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Clean sheet: goalkeeper or defender / midfielder</td><td>4 / 1</td></tr><tr><td>Every 3 saves (goalkeeper)</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Penalty save / penalty miss</td><td>5 / −2</td></tr><tr><td>Defensive contribution: defender 10+ actions / midfielder or forward 12+</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Every 2 goals conceded (goalkeeper or defender)</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td>Yellow card / red card / own goal</td><td>−1 / −3 / −2</td></tr><tr><td>Bonus points (the three best performers in each match)</td><td>1 to 3</td></tr></tbody></table>", "<p>Two things follow from the table. Defenders and goalkeepers from teams that keep clean sheets score well even without goals, and attacking midfielders are prized because a goal is worth 5 to them against 4 for a forward. Defensive contributions (clearances, blocks, interceptions and tackles, plus ball recoveries for midfielders and forwards) give steady points to busy players who never score.</p>", "<h2>Captain: the most important decision every week</h2>", "<p>Pick a <b>captain</b> and his points are <b>doubled</b>. If he does not play at all, your <b>vice-captain</b>'s points are doubled instead. Most weeks, the captain choice moves your score more than any transfer, so give it the most thought: a premium attacker at home against a weak defence is the classic pick.</p>", "<h2>Transfers</h2>", "<p>You get <b>one free transfer every gameweek</b>, and unused ones roll over, up to a maximum of <b>five</b> saved. Every extra transfer beyond your free ones costs <b>4 points</b> (a 'hit'). Beginners take too many hits: a hit only pays if the new player is likely to outscore the old one by more than four points over the weeks you keep him.</p>", "<p>Prices move during the season as managers buy and sell. If a player you own rises in price, you only keep part of the rise when you sell him (FPL keeps half of any profit, rounded down), so do not chase price rises for their own sake.</p>", "<p>The <b>deadline</b> for transfers and team changes is 90 minutes before the first kick-off of the gameweek. Miss it and your team from the previous week carries over.</p>", "<h2>Chips</h2>", "<p>In 2026/27 every manager has <b>two sets of four chips</b>, one set for each half of the season. The first set must be used before the Gameweek 19 deadline (13:30 GMT on Saturday 2 January):</p>", "<ul><li><b>Wildcard</b>: unlimited free transfers for one gameweek, and the changes stay.</li><li><b>Free Hit</b>: unlimited free transfers for one gameweek only; your old squad comes back afterwards.</li><li><b>Triple Captain</b>: your captain's points are tripled instead of doubled.</li><li><b>Bench Boost</b>: your four bench players' points count too.</li></ul>", "<p>Chips are most valuable in 'double gameweeks', when some teams play twice. A Bench Boost or Triple Captain in a double gameweek can be worth two normal weeks.</p>", "<h2>The five decisions that matter most</h2>", "<ol><li><b>Spend on the attack, save at the back.</b> Most points come from a handful of premium attackers; cheap defenders from solid teams fill the gaps well.</li><li><b>Fill the bench cheaply but with starters.</b> Bench players only matter when a starter misses out, so the cheapest regular starters are enough.</li><li><b>Captain with your head.</b> Favour form, fixtures and home games. The betting market is a useful guide: a team's chance of scoring two or more goals says more than last week's points.</li><li><b>Avoid knee-jerk transfers.</b> One bad week is noise. Bank free transfers and use them when injuries or fixture swings make a real difference.</li><li><b>Plan chips around the fixture list.</b> Keep at least one chip for the double gameweeks in each half of the season.</li></ol>", "<h2>Using the betting market for FPL</h2>", "<p>The odds on a match are the best free forecast available. A team priced as a strong favourite, with a low goals line against it, is a clean-sheet candidate; a team expected to score two or more is where your attackers and captain should come from. Each week our FPL deadline preview turns the sharp market's numbers into team-goals and clean-sheet chances for every fixture.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Premier League: FPL basics explained, how to pick a squad (2026/27)", "https://www.premierleague.com/en/news/2174419"], ["Flashscore: Fantasy Premier League 2026/27, all rule changes and new features (two sets of chips, five saved transfers)", "https://www.flashscore.com/news/soccer-premier-league-fantasy-premier-league-2026-27-all-rule-changes-and-new-features/KGmq7ts1/"], ["BeatFPL: FPL 2026/27 rules and scoring summary", "https://www.beatfpl.com/rules"], ["Official FPL rules (Fantasy Premier League help)", "https://fantasy.premierleague.com/help/rules"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting", "Football"], "sports": ["Football"], "id": "bet-builders-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Betting basics", "title": "Bet builders explained: how they are priced, why the margin is bigger, and when one makes sense", "dek": "A bet builder lets you combine several outcomes from one match into a single bet. Here is how bookmakers price them, why correlation matters, why the margin is usually much bigger than on a single, and how to check one.", "date": "2026-11-19T16:35:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>A <b>bet builder</b> (also called a 'same game multi' or 'request a bet') lets you combine several outcomes from <b>one match</b> into a single bet: for example, a team to win, over 2.5 goals and a named player to score. They are hugely popular in the UK because they turn one game into one big price. This guide explains how they are priced and why they usually cost you more than the same bets placed one at a time.</p>", "<p>One point first: a bet builder is still a bet, and a long-odds one at that. Stake only what you can afford to lose. 18+.</p>", "<h2>How a normal accumulator is priced</h2>", "<p>In an ordinary accumulator the selections come from <b>different</b> matches, so they are independent: one result tells you nothing about another. The price is simply the individual prices multiplied together. A 2.00 shot and a 3.00 shot make a 6.00 double.</p>", "<h2>Why a bet builder is different: correlation</h2>", "<p>Outcomes in the same match are <b>connected</b>. If a team wins, it is more likely the game had goals; if a striker scores, his team is more likely to win. Multiplying the prices would therefore overstate the true odds, so bookmakers cannot do it. Instead they price the combination as a whole with their own model of how the outcomes move together.</p>", "<p>A worked example. Say a team is 50% to win (fair price 2.00) and over 2.5 goals is also 50% (2.00). Multiplied, that is 4.00, implying 25%. But wins and goals go together, so the real chance of both might be nearer 32%, a fair price of about 3.13. A bookmaker offering 2.90 for the pair is <b>not</b> giving you a bigger price than the 4.00 the singles suggest; it is still charging a margin on top of the true 3.13.</p>", "<h2>Why the margin is bigger</h2>", "<p>On a main match-result market our own price scans show mainstream UK bookmakers building in a margin of roughly 4% to 8%. On a bet builder, the margin applies to every part of the combination and is hidden inside a correlation model you cannot see, so the total is usually far larger than on any single leg, and it grows with every leg you add, especially player props. You cannot see that margin directly, which is exactly why it is worth checking the legs one by one.</p>", "<p>The simple rule: the more legs, and the more player markets, the more you pay for the convenience.</p>", "<h2>How to check a bet builder</h2>", "<ol><li><b>Price the legs as singles.</b> Note each leg's best single price, ideally from a sharp bookmaker or the exchange.</li><li><b>Ask whether they are connected.</b> Legs that point the same way (a favourite to win and overs; a striker to score and his team to win) make the true price shorter than the singles multiplied. Legs that pull against each other (a team to win and under 1.5 goals) make it longer.</li><li><b>Compare.</b> If the builder price is close to, or above, what the singles multiplied would pay for connected legs, it may be fair. If it is well below, the margin is doing the work.</li><li><b>Keep it short.</b> Two connected legs at sensible prices can be reasonable; six legs with three player props almost never are.</li></ol>", "<h2>When a bet builder makes sense</h2>", "<p>Bet builders are best treated as entertainment on a match you are watching, with a small stake. If you are trying to bet on value, singles on the main markets, where margins are lowest and prices are most efficient, are almost always the better route. Our guides to <a href=\"betting-odds-explained.html\">betting odds</a> and <a href=\"asian-handicaps-explained.html\">Asian handicaps</a> show how to turn any price into a chance and check it.</p>", "<h2>The rules that protect you</h2>", "<p>In Great Britain, bet builders are offered by licensed bookmakers under the same Gambling Commission rules as any other bet: the terms must be clear, and you can set deposit limits, take a break or self-exclude at any time. If a bet builder is part of a promotion, the wider promotion rules apply too, including the cap on wagering requirements that came in on 19 January 2026.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["UK Gambling Commission: Gambling promotions to be safer and simpler (wagering cap from 19 January 2026)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/gambling-promotions-to-be-safer-and-simpler"], ["UK Gambling Commission: safer gambling tools and self-exclusion (GAMSTOP)", "https://www.gamstop.co.uk/"], ["BetMeisters calculations: correlated-leg pricing example and bookmaker margins on main markets (BetMeisters price scans, Nov 2026)", "https://betmeisters.news/betting-odds-explained.html"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Basketball", "Tennis", "Darts", "Ice hockey", "American football"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "other-sports-thursday-1911", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Other sports · Thursday 19 November", "title": "Thursday night: six Euroleague games, Zverev v Fils in Turin, Van Gerwen v Wade and Texans v Colts", "dek": "Euroleague's double-header night, the ATP Finals evening session, the Grand Slam of Darts last 16, the NFL's Thursday game and a ten-game NHL slate. Every main event with its chance on the sharp market.", "date": "2026-11-19T16:08:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>A full Thursday night away from football. Euroleague basketball runs from 5.15pm UK, the ATP Finals evening session in Turin brings <b>Alexander Zverev v Arthur Fils</b>, the Grand Slam of Darts finishes its last 16 in Wolverhampton, and the NFL's Thursday night game is <b>Houston v Indianapolis</b>. The NHL and NBA follow overnight.</p>", "<p>Every chance below comes from <b>Pinnacle</b>, the sharpest bookmaker in the market, with its margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at 1pm UK. They show what the market thinks, not what will happen. Where we give a handicap or a total, it is the line the market prices closest to 50/50. Times are UK, with US Eastern in brackets for the late games.</p>", "<h2>Euroleague</h2>", "<ul><li><b>Maccabi Tel Aviv v Dubai Basketball</b> (5.15pm): Dubai are favourites away, 58.5%. Maccabi +3 is the even line and the total is about 174.5.</li><li><b>Zalgiris v Panathinaikos</b> (6.00pm): close to a coin flip, Zalgiris 52.4% at home.</li><li><b>Bayern Munich v Baskonia</b> (7.00pm): Bayern 72.5%, with Bayern −7.5 the even handicap.</li><li><b>Olimpia Milano v Fenerbahce</b> (7.30pm): Fenerbahce 63.6% away; Milano +3.5 is the 50/50 line and the total about 165.5.</li><li><b>Paris Basketball v Hapoel Tel Aviv</b> (7.45pm): the closest game of the night, Hapoel 50.9%, Paris 49.1%.</li><li><b>Valencia v Virtus Bologna</b> (8.00pm): the biggest favourite, Valencia 81.5%, Valencia −10.5 the even line, total about 186.5.</li></ul>", "<p>Two of the six home sides are underdogs. Euroleague margins are tight, so most of these games sit within a few points on the handicap.</p>", "<h2>Tennis: ATP Finals, Turin</h2>", "<p><b>Alexander Zverev v Arthur Fils</b> (7.30pm): the market makes Zverev 68.8% to win. The afternoon match, Ben Shelton v Rafael Jodar, was already under way at the time of our scan, so we leave it out.</p>", "<h2>Darts: Grand Slam of Darts, last 16</h2>", "<p>The evening session in Wolverhampton settles four quarter-final places. Two of the ties in our scan:</p>", "<ul><li><b>Luke Humphries v Tom Sykes</b>: Humphries is a heavy favourite at 83.5%.</li><li><b>James Wade v Michael van Gerwen</b> (closes the night): the market makes Van Gerwen the outsider, 38.8%, with Wade 61.2%. The pair met in the 2016 Grand Slam final.</li></ul>", "<h2>NFL: Thursday Night Football</h2>", "<p><b>Houston Texans v Indianapolis Colts</b> (1.15am UK, 8.15pm ET): Houston 85.9% on the money line. Indianapolis +12.5 is the even handicap and the total is about 44.</p>", "<h2>NHL</h2>", "<ul><li><b>Carolina v Montreal</b> (midnight UK, 7pm ET): Carolina 72.5%, Carolina −1.5 the even puck line, total about 6.5.</li><li><b>Pittsburgh v St Louis</b> (midnight UK): exactly 50/50 on the money line; the total is about 6.5.</li><li><b>Washington v Buffalo</b> (midnight UK): Washington 55.4%.</li><li><b>Tampa Bay v Edmonton</b> (12.30am UK): Tampa Bay 56.7%.</li><li><b>Colorado v Anaheim</b> (2.00am UK): Colorado 54.3%.</li><li><b>Seattle v Vancouver</b> (2.40am UK): Seattle 60.7%.</li><li><b>Vegas v Dallas</b> (3.00am UK): Vegas 53.5%.</li></ul>", "<h2>NBA</h2>", "<ul><li><b>Minnesota v New York</b> (1.00am UK, 8pm ET): a pure coin flip, 50/50, total about 216.5.</li><li><b>Memphis v Golden State</b> (1.00am UK): Memphis 72.1%, Memphis −7.5 the even line, total about 228.5.</li><li><b>Milwaukee v Portland</b> (1.00am UK): Portland 78.4% away, Milwaukee +10 the even line.</li><li><b>San Antonio v LA Clippers</b> (1.00am UK): the biggest favourite of the night, San Antonio 89.6%, Clippers +16.5.</li></ul>", "<h2>How to read these numbers</h2>", "<p>A chance is not a tip. A 72% favourite still loses about one game in four, and a coin-flip game is exactly that. The numbers are useful because they let you compare a price with the market: if a bookmaker offers more than the fair price for a side (for 50%, that is anything above 2.00), the price is better than the sharp market thinks it should be. Our guide to <a href=\"betting-odds-explained.html\">betting odds</a> shows how to turn a price into a chance.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["BetMeisters price scan, 19 Nov 2026 13:00 UK (Pinnacle prices, margin removed)", "https://betmeisters.news"], ["Nine Dart News on X: Grand Slam of Darts last-16 order, 19 Nov 2026", "https://x.com/DaveDartsNews/status/2123329012130787520"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Basketball", "Ice hockey", "American football"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "us-sports-wednesday-night-1811", "section": "previews", "kicker": "US sports · Wednesday night, 18 November", "title": "Wednesday night in the US: Arizona v UConn, twelve NBA games and MACtion", "dek": "A big NBA night, college basketball headed by Arizona v UConn and Kansas v West Virginia, three NHL games and two midweek college football games. Every main game with its chance on the sharp market, the spread and the total.", "date": "2026-11-18T22:36:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>Wednesday night in the US is a full card: twelve NBA games, a long list of college basketball, three NHL games in our scan and two midweek college football games. The pick of the college basketball is <b>Arizona v UConn</b>; in the NBA, <b>Denver v Boston</b> is the closest of the late games.</p><p>The chances below come from <b>Pinnacle</b>, the sharpest bookmaker in the market, with its margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at 6pm UK (1pm ET). They show what the market thinks, not what will happen. Where we quote a spread or a total, it is the line the market prices closest to 50/50. Times are UK, with US Eastern in brackets.</p>", "<h2>NBA</h2><ul><li><b>Magic v 76ers</b> (midnight UK, 7.00pm ET): the closest game of the night. Philadelphia 50.7%, Orlando 49.3%, spread Philadelphia −1, total about 222.</li><li><b>Heat v Bucks</b> (midnight UK): the biggest favourite, Miami 84.9%, spread Miami −12, total about 237.</li><li><b>Pacers v Rockets</b> (midnight UK): Houston 72.5%, spread Houston −7.5, total about 225.</li><li><b>Cavaliers v Grizzlies</b> (midnight UK): Cleveland 63.6%, spread −4.5, total about 236.5.</li><li><b>Nets v Bulls</b> (12.30am UK, 7.30pm ET): Brooklyn 56.8%, spread −2.5, total about 240, the highest of the night.</li><li><b>Raptors v Wizards</b> (12.30am UK): Toronto 62.5%, spread −4.5, total about 225.5.</li><li><b>Mavericks v Warriors</b> (1.00am UK, 8.00pm ET): Dallas 63.8%, spread −4.5, total about 224.5.</li><li><b>Thunder v Hawks</b> (1.00am UK): Oklahoma City 69.0%, Atlanta +6 the 50/50 line, total about 228.5.</li><li><b>Pelicans v Pistons</b> (1.00am UK): Detroit 69.3%, New Orleans +6.5, total about 227.</li><li><b>Nuggets v Celtics</b> (2.30am UK, 9.30pm ET): Boston 55.7%, Denver +2.5, total about 220.5, the lowest of the night.</li><li><b>Lakers v Jazz</b> (3.00am UK, 10.00pm ET): the Lakers 79.0%, Utah +10, total about 228.5.</li><li><b>Kings v Suns</b> (3.00am UK): Phoenix 71.0%, spread −7, total about 226.5.</li></ul><p>The money line tells you who is likely to win; the spread tells you by how much. Miami at 84.9% with a 12-point spread and Philadelphia at 50.7% with a single point show the range.</p>", "<h2>College basketball</h2>", "<h3>Arizona v UConn · 2.00am UK (9.00pm ET)</h3><p>The game of the night in college hoops. The market makes <b>Arizona 60.9%</b> and <b>UConn 39.1%</b>, with the spread at <b>Arizona −3.5</b> and the total about <b>146.5</b>. A spread of three and a half points says the market expects a close game.</p>", "<h3>Kansas v West Virginia · 12.30am UK (7.30pm ET)</h3><p>Kansas are clear favourites at <b>78.8%</b>, with <b>West Virginia +8.5</b> the 50/50 line and a total about 150.5.</p>", "<h3>UNLV v Auburn · 3.00am UK (10.00pm ET)</h3><p>Auburn <b>78.5%</b>, spread <b>Auburn −9</b>, total about 159.</p>", "<h3>The close ones</h3><ul><li><b>Washington State v Hawaii</b> (3.00am UK): a genuine coin flip, Hawaii 50.3%.</li><li><b>Loyola Chicago v Wichita State</b> (1.00am UK): Wichita State 52.2%, total about 150.5.</li><li><b>Charleston Southern v Wofford</b> (midnight UK): Wofford 53.6%.</li><li><b>Gardner-Webb v UNC Greensboro</b> (1.00am UK): UNC Greensboro 57.7%.</li></ul>", "<h3>Big spreads</h3><p>November schedules bring mismatches, and the market's biggest lines tonight include <b>Missouri −42.5</b> against Alcorn State, <b>LSU −37.5</b> against Louisiana Monroe, <b>Texas Tech −36</b> against New Orleans, <b>Nebraska −33</b> against South Dakota and <b>UCLA −32</b> against Cal State Fullerton. Lines that big are set close to 50/50, and a favourite that empties its bench late can win easily without covering.</p>", "<h2>NHL</h2><ul><li><b>Red Wings v Bruins</b> (12.30am UK, 7.30pm ET): close to even, Detroit 51.2%, Boston 48.8%; Over 5.5 goals 55.0%.</li><li><b>Devils v Canadiens</b> (12.30am UK): New Jersey 60.1%; Under 6.5 goals 54.5%.</li><li><b>Utah v Avalanche</b> (3.00am UK, 10.00pm ET): Utah 50.5%, Colorado 49.5%; Over 6.5 goals 46.9%.</li></ul><p>Hockey money lines include overtime and shootouts unless the market says '60 minutes' or 'regulation time'. The puck line (−1.5) needs a win by two or more, which is why Detroit −1.5 is only about 29% even with the Red Wings slight favourites.</p>", "<h2>College football: MACtion</h2><ul><li><b>UMass v Akron</b> (midnight UK, 7.00pm ET): UMass 60.7%, total about 47.5.</li><li><b>Buffalo v Central Michigan</b> (12.30am UK): Buffalo 56.2%, spread Buffalo −2.5, total about 46.</li></ul>", "<h2>Reading tonight's numbers</h2><p>Three things are worth knowing before a night like this.</p><ul><li><b>Spreads are set to split opinion, not to predict the margin exactly.</b> When the market makes Houston −7.5 a 50/50 proposition, it is saying that a Rockets win by eight or more is about as likely as anything else. A favourite at 72.5% to win can still be a coin flip against the spread.</li><li><b>Half-points matter.</b> A spread of −1 or a total of 222 can push, with the stake returned, if the game lands exactly on the number; −1.5 or 222.5 cannot. In close NBA games a single free throw decides it.</li><li><b>Totals include overtime.</b> An overtime period adds points to a basketball total, a small boost for overs in close games. Hockey totals and money lines include overtime and shootouts unless they say otherwise.</li></ul><p>A month into the NBA season, the market still leans partly on pre-season ratings, and each result moves it, which is why lines can shift by a point or two through the day, especially when injury news lands.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle prices with the margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at about 6pm UK on Wednesday 18 November. Times are UK, with US Eastern where shown. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan, 18 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "event", "id": "preview-coventry-palace-gw11", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Advance preview · Premier League Gameweek 11", "title": "Coventry v Crystal Palace: a relegation six-pointer the market can barely split", "dek": "Saturday 21 November, 3pm UK. Coventry are 17th on nine points, Palace bottom on seven. The sharp market makes it 38% to 35% with a 27% draw. The table, the form, the goals lines and what the rest of the bottom five face.", "date": "2026-11-18T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>Of the ten Premier League games on the first weekend after the international break, none matters more to the bottom of the table than <b>Coventry City v Crystal Palace</b> at the Coventry Building Society Arena, kick-off <b>3pm UK on Saturday 21 November</b> (10am ET). Coventry are 17th, the last place above the relegation zone. Palace are 20th. A home win would put five points between them; a Palace win would lift Palace above Coventry.</p>", "<h2>The table</h2><p>After ten games, the bottom five are separated by two points:</p><ul><li>16th <b>Nottingham Forest</b>, 9 points, goal difference -4</li><li>17th <b>Coventry City</b>, 9 points, -9</li><li>18th <b>Manchester United</b>, 8 points, -5</li><li>19th <b>Sunderland</b>, 8 points, -9</li><li>20th <b>Crystal Palace</b>, 7 points, -14</li></ul><p>Palace's goal difference is the worst in the league, and it is a real handicap in a race this tight: on level points they lose out to everyone around them. Fulham, 15th on ten points, are only one point clear of Coventry.</p>", "<h2>The form</h2><p>Coventry have been a strange mix of excellent and poor. Their last six games, most recent first, according to Sports Mole: a 3-1 defeat at <b>Everton</b>, a 3-1 home win over <b>Sunderland</b>, a 1-0 home defeat by <b>Fulham</b>, a 3-1 defeat at <b>Tottenham</b>, a 4-1 home win over <b>Newcastle</b> and a 1-0 win at <b>Nottingham Forest</b>. Three wins, all against teams in or around the bottom half; three defeats, all by two goals or a single goal against sides above them. At home they have beaten Newcastle and Sunderland comfortably.</p><p>Palace's run is worse. Their last six in all competitions: a 3-1 home defeat by <b>Liverpool</b>, a 1-1 Europa League draw with <b>Hoffenheim</b>, a 2-0 defeat at <b>Tottenham</b>, a 1-0 League Cup win at <b>Fulham</b>, a 2-0 home defeat by <b>Newcastle</b> and a 2-2 draw at <b>Beşiktaş</b> in Europe. In the league, the run is three defeats in a row, scoring one goal and conceding seven. They are also still in Europe, which means Thursday-to-Sunday weeks that Coventry, without European football, do not have.</p><p>The clubs have not met often in recent years. The last meeting Sports Mole lists was a 1-1 draw at Coventry in March 2012.</p>", "<h2>What the market says</h2><p>Pinnacle's prices on Wednesday, with the margin removed:</p><ul><li><b>Coventry win: about 38.5%</b> (a fair price of about 2.60)</li><li><b>Draw: about 26.8%</b> (about 3.73)</li><li><b>Palace win: about 34.7%</b> (about 2.88)</li></ul><p>That is close to as even as a Premier League game gets. On the Asian handicap the market has Coventry on a level line (draw no bet) at about 52.6%, so it gives the home side a small edge and little more. Since Monday the price has barely moved: on 17 November Coventry were about 39.5% and Palace about 33.8%.</p><p>The goals lines point to an open game rather than a nervy one. <b>Over 2.5 goals is about 56%</b>, close to the average for this weekend's fixtures. Each side is close to a coin flip to score twice: Coventry about 45% and Palace about 42% to score two or more. Put together, the market's most likely shapes are a narrow win either way or a scoring draw, not a goalless game.</p>", "<h2>Why the draw is not a 'safe' result for either side</h2><p>The draw is priced at about 27%, but for Palace it would leave them bottom, two points behind Coventry, with the worst goal difference in the division. For Coventry, it would keep them two points clear of Palace but would do nothing about the clubs just below them. That changes how a game like this is played late on: whichever side is behind after 70 minutes has more reason to chase, which is one reason the market keeps the goals lines high.</p>", "<h2>The rest of the bottom five this weekend</h2><p>The results around this game matter almost as much, and on paper they are hard for everyone involved:</p><ul><li><b>Bournemouth v Nottingham Forest</b> (Saturday, 3pm): Forest about 24%, Bournemouth about 50%.</li><li><b>Aston Villa v Sunderland</b> (Saturday, 3pm): Sunderland about 22%, Villa about 52%.</li><li><b>Liverpool v Manchester United</b> (Sunday, 4.30pm): United about 23%, Liverpool about 55%.</li></ul><p>All three of the clubs around Coventry and Palace are underdogs away from home. That makes Saturday's game at Coventry the most likely source of points for anyone in the bottom five, and a winner here could climb out of the bottom three or put real distance between themselves and it in a single afternoon.</p>", "<h2>The verdict from the numbers</h2><p>Coventry about 38.5%, Palace about 34.7%, the draw about 26.8%. The table says Palace are in trouble; the market says they are almost as likely as Coventry to win this one. Coventry's home form against teams around them has been good, Palace's league run is three defeats in a row, and yet the gap in the price is small. Watch the team news: in a game this tight, one absence can move the market more than the table does.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, taken at about 4pm UK on Wednesday 18 November, three days before kick-off. Table after 10 games; results as reported by the sources below. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Premier League table after 10 games (WithinNigeria, 9 Nov 2026)", "https://www.withinnigeria.com/2026/11/09/premier-league-table-after-10-games-arsenal-top-on-27-points-brighton-second-as-man-united-fall-into-the-relegation-zone/"], ["Sports Mole: Coventry City vs Crystal Palace, form and head-to-head", "https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/premier-league/coventry-city-vs-crystal-palace_game_263118.html"], ["Pinnacle prices, 17 and 18 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "section": "fpl", "fplType": "deadline", "id": "fpl-gw11-deadline-preview", "previewType": "fpl", "kicker": "FPL · Gameweek 11 deadline preview", "title": "FPL Gameweek 11: the deadline, the captains the market likes, clean sheets and the transfers everyone is making", "dek": "The deadline is 11am UK on Saturday 21 November. Manchester City, Tottenham, Brighton and Liverpool have the best team-goals numbers; Brighton and Arsenal the best clean-sheet chances. Every fixture with its odds-based numbers.", "date": "2026-11-18T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>Fantasy Premier League returns after the international break with <b>Gameweek 11</b>. The official FPL site lists the deadline as <b>11am UK time on Saturday 21 November</b> (6am ET, 2pm in Kampala), 90 minutes before Manchester City v Fulham kicks off. Make your transfers and pick your captain before then.</p><p>This preview does something most FPL guides do not: it uses the betting market. Pinnacle, a sharp bookmaker, prices how many goals each team will score. With the margin removed, those prices give the chance of a team scoring two or more (useful for captains and attackers) and the chance of the opponent failing to score (a clean sheet for defenders and goalkeepers). All numbers are from Wednesday afternoon and will move with team news.</p>", "<h2>The fixtures</h2><ul><li>Saturday 12.30pm: <b>Manchester City v Fulham</b> (City about 68%)</li><li>Saturday 3pm: <b>Tottenham v Ipswich</b> (Spurs about 62%)</li><li>Saturday 3pm: <b>Aston Villa v Sunderland</b> (Villa about 52%)</li><li>Saturday 3pm: <b>Chelsea v Leeds</b> (Chelsea about 51%)</li><li>Saturday 3pm: <b>Bournemouth v Nottingham Forest</b> (Bournemouth about 50%)</li><li>Saturday 3pm: <b>Coventry v Crystal Palace</b> (Coventry about 39%, Palace about 35%)</li><li>Saturday 5.30pm: <b>Newcastle v Arsenal</b> (Arsenal about 61%)</li><li>Sunday 2pm: <b>Hull v Brighton</b> (Brighton about 64%)</li><li>Sunday 4.30pm: <b>Liverpool v Manchester United</b> (Liverpool about 55%)</li><li>Monday 8pm: <b>Brentford v Everton</b> (Brentford about 46%)</li></ul>", "<h2>Captains: who is most likely to score twice or more?</h2><p>The single best guide to captaincy is how many goals a player's team is expected to score. Ranked by the market's chance of scoring <b>two or more</b>:</p><ul><li><b>Tottenham</b> (home v Ipswich): about <b>63%</b></li><li><b>Brighton</b> (away at Hull): about <b>62%</b></li><li><b>Liverpool</b> (home v Manchester United): about <b>61%</b></li><li><b>Arsenal</b> (away at Newcastle): about <b>59%</b></li><li><b>Aston Villa</b> (home v Sunderland): about 53%</li><li><b>Chelsea</b> (home v Leeds): about 52%</li><li><b>Bournemouth</b> (home v Forest): about 50%</li></ul><p><b>Manchester City</b> are priced on a higher line: the market makes them about <b>42% to score three or more</b> at home to Fulham, which puts them above everyone else in expected goals for the week. That makes City's main attackers the obvious captaincy picks for managers who own them, with Spurs, Brighton and Liverpool players the strongest alternatives. Note the timing: City play in the 12.30pm game, so a City captain's score is known early, before the Saturday 3pm games.</p>", "<h2>Clean sheets: defenders and goalkeepers</h2><p>The market's chance of each favourite keeping a clean sheet (the opponent failing to score):</p><ul><li><b>Brighton</b> at Hull: about <b>40%</b></li><li><b>Arsenal</b> at Newcastle: about <b>39%</b></li><li><b>Manchester City</b> v Fulham: about <b>38%</b></li><li><b>Tottenham</b> v Ipswich: about <b>37%</b></li><li><b>Bournemouth</b> v Forest: about 34%</li><li><b>Aston Villa</b> v Sunderland: about 33%</li><li><b>Chelsea</b> v Leeds: about 32%</li></ul><p>No team is better than 40% for a clean sheet this week, which is low for a gameweek that includes several home favourites. In other words, the market expects goals at both ends almost everywhere. The defenders worth owning are the ones who also offer attacking returns.</p>", "<h2>The transfers managers are making</h2><p>Fantasy Football Fix's transfer data ahead of Gameweek 11 shows where the FPL crowd is moving:</p><ul><li><b>Nicolas Jackson</b> is the most-bought player, with 50 points in his last six gameweeks.</li><li><b>Joško Gvardiol</b> to <b>Jan Paul van Hecke</b> is the most popular transfer of all (22.1% of the transfers tracked).</li><li><b>João Pedro</b> has been the most-sold player for three weeks running.</li><li><b>Thierno Barry</b> scored 16 points in Gameweek 10; his next three fixtures are Brentford, Liverpool and Aston Villa.</li><li><b>Evanilson</b> has scored in five consecutive matches.</li><li><b>Pascal Groß</b> is one booking away from a suspension.</li><li><b>Dominik Szoboszlai</b> made his first league start for Liverpool since Gameweek 5 in Gameweek 10 and created six chances.</li></ul><p>The crowd is not always right, but it does move prices, and price rises reward managers who move first.</p>", "<h2>Team news</h2><p>Most clubs hold their press conferences on Thursday and Friday, and the FPL site flags injuries and doubts on each player. At the time of writing we have not seen confirmed injury news from a reputable source for this gameweek that we can report, so check the official flags and the pre-match press conferences before the deadline. A late absence can change a captaincy choice more than any fixture.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Team-goals and clean-sheet chances are from Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, taken at about 4pm UK on Wednesday 18 November. Deadline from the official Fantasy Premier League site. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Fantasy Premier League (official): Gameweek 11 deadline", "https://fantasy.premierleague.com/"], ["Fantasy Football Fix: FPL GW11 transfer tips 2026/27", "https://www.fantasyfootballfix.com/blog-index/fpl-gw11-transfer-tips-2026-27/"], ["Pinnacle prices, 18 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "daily-football", "id": "football-thursday-1911", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Daily football · Thursday 19 November", "title": "Thursday's football: Arsenal v OL Lyonnes, an MLS wild card with Griezmann and Cincinnati, and the Brasileirão", "dek": "The Women's Champions League finishes matchday 5, Orlando City host FC Cincinnati in the MLS Cup playoffs, and Vasco, São Paulo and Vitória play in Brazil. Every main game with its chance on the sharp market.", "date": "2026-11-18T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>Thursday's football runs from lunchtime in Asia to the early hours in the Americas. The headline games are the second half of the <b>Women's Champions League</b> matchday 5, with <b>Arsenal v OL Lyonnes</b> the pick at 8pm UK (3pm ET); an <b>MLS Cup playoff wild card</b> in Orlando at 7.30pm ET (00.30 UK); and three <b>Brasileirão</b> games. All times below are UK, with Eastern time for the evening games.</p><p>The chances quoted are from Pinnacle, a sharp bookmaker, with the margin removed. They are the market's view, for 90 minutes, taken at about 4pm UK on Wednesday.</p>", "<h2>Women's Champions League, matchday 5 (Thursday)</h2><p>Five games, completing a matchday that started on Wednesday. According to UEFA's preview, <b>OL Lyonnes lead the league phase</b> and <b>Arsenal are fourth</b>; holders <b>Barcelona</b> had a perfect start until a 1-1 home draw with Arsenal on matchday 4.</p>", "<h3>Arsenal v OL Lyonnes · 8pm (3pm ET)</h3><p>The game of the night. It is the 13th meeting between the clubs, which UEFA notes makes it, with OL Lyonnes v Wolfsburg, the joint-most-played fixture in the competition. Lyonnes won last season's league phase meeting 2-1 at Meadow Park. Arsenal arrive with that draw in Barcelona and a win over Benfica in which Alessia Russo scored a hat-trick.</p><p>The market still makes the visitors clear favourites: <b>OL Lyonnes about 54%</b>, the draw about 24%, <b>Arsenal about 22%</b>. It expects goals too: over 2.5 goals is about 62%. Arsenal at home to the leaders at roughly 1 in 4.5 is a big underdog price for a side that has just drawn in Barcelona.</p>", "<h3>Austria Wien v Bayern München · 5.45pm (12.45pm ET)</h3><p>The most one-sided game of the night on the numbers. Austria Wien are bottom; Bayern are unbeaten and beat Paris SG 2-1 on matchday 4 to move into the top four. The market makes <b>Bayern about 87%</b>, the draw 10% and Austria Wien about 4%.</p>", "<h3>The other three</h3><p><b>Paris FC v HB Køge</b> (5.45pm) is a big game for the hosts, who are still without a win in the league phase. <b>Barcelona v Servette</b> and <b>Benfica v Roma</b> kick off at 8pm. Benfica are ninth on six points after beating Austria Wien; Roma are 12th after their first league phase win, against Servette. We did not have reliable prices for these three when we wrote this.</p>", "<h2>MLS Cup playoffs: Orlando City v FC Cincinnati · 7.30pm ET (00.30 UK)</h2><p>The Eastern Conference wild card, at Inter.co Stadium in Orlando, live on Apple TV. The winner plays <b>Nashville SC</b>, the Supporters' Shield winners, in a best-of-three first-round series.</p><p>MLS's own preview sets it up as a goals game. <b>Antoine Griezmann</b>, who joined Orlando from Atlético Madrid in the summer, has 14 goals and 8 assists in 18 appearances, and Orlando won nine of their 13 games after his debut. <b>Martín Ojeda</b> has 16 goals. For Cincinnati, <b>Kévin Denkey</b> has 17 and <b>Evander</b> has a club-best 31 goal contributions (14 goals, 17 assists). Both sides were in the league's top ten for goals scored and for goals conceded, and when they met in May, Cincinnati won 6-2.</p><p>The market sees a coin flip in 90 minutes: <b>Orlando about 39%</b>, <b>Cincinnati about 38%</b>, the draw about 24%. The goals line is the striking number: <b>over 2.5 goals about 74%</b>, the highest of any game on Thursday's card.</p>", "<h2>Brazil: Série A</h2><p><b>Vasco da Gama v Internacional</b> (10.30pm UK, 5.30pm ET): Vasco are about <b>59%</b> at home, Internacional about 18%. <b>Vitória v Red Bull Bragantino</b> (10.30pm) is close to even: Vitória 35%, draw 28%, Bragantino 37%. Late on, <b>São Paulo v Fluminense</b> (00.30 UK Friday, 7.30pm ET Thursday) has São Paulo at about 40% and Fluminense about 31%, with over 2.5 goals only about 45%: the market expects a tight game.</p>", "<h2>The rest of the Americas</h2><p>In Uruguay's Clausura, <b>Peñarol</b> are about 66% at home to Cerro Largo (7.30pm UK), and <b>Deportivo Maldonado</b> about 53% away at Boston River. In Colombia, <b>Real Cartagena</b> are about 68% at home to Tigres (00.45 Friday), while Independiente Valle del Cauca v Envigado is priced almost exactly even at 34% each.</p>", "<h2>Europe and Africa</h2><p>In France's Ligue 3, <b>Amiens v Caen</b> (7.45pm) has Amiens about 43% and Caen about 28%. The cups in Slovakia and Slovenia have big favourites: <b>Košice</b> about 73% at Zvolen and <b>Celje</b> about 66% at Beltinci. In Algeria, <b>JS Kabylie v USM Alger</b> (7pm) is the pick of Thursday's Algerian Ligue 1 games: Kabylie about 43%, the draw an unusually high 33%, and over 2.5 goals only about 34%.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, taken at about 4pm UK on Wednesday 18 November; 90 minutes only. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["UEFA: Women's Champions League matchday 5 preview", "https://www.uefa.com/womenschampionsleague/news/02ab-220ff9af21a9-748c51d8b995-1000--women-s-champions-league-matchday-5-preview-chelsea-vs-pa/"], ["MLSsoccer.com: Orlando City vs FC Cincinnati, 5 biggest storylines", "https://www.mlssoccer.com/playoffs/2026/news/orlando-city-vs-fc-cincinnati-5-biggest-storylines-for-eastern-conference-wild-card"], ["Pinnacle prices, 18 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "previewType": "racing-card", "id": "racing-card-1911", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Racing card · Thursday 19 November", "title": "Thursday's racing: two Listed fillies' races at Lingfield, a veterans' chase at Warwick and Thurles over jumps", "dek": "Four meetings and 33 races. Lingfield's All-Weather Championships card has two Listed races for fillies, Warwick stages a Class 2 veterans' handicap chase, Newcastle races under lights and Thurles has seven over jumps. Every meeting, the early market and what to watch.", "date": "2026-11-18T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>Thursday's racing in Britain and Ireland is spread across four meetings. <b>Lingfield</b> (all-weather) and <b>Warwick</b> (jumps) start just after 11am UK time, <b>Thurles</b> runs seven races over jumps in Ireland from 12.08pm, and <b>Newcastle</b> takes over on its all-weather track from 3.35pm until the last at 7.40pm. That is 33 races, from 11.18am to 7.40pm UK time (6.18am to 2.40pm Eastern). The ground is <b>good</b> at Warwick and Thurles; both all-weather tracks are <b>standard</b>.</p><p>The prices below are <b>early prices</b>, taken on Wednesday evening. Most races are still priced by a single bookmaker, so treat them as a first view rather than a market. Only Lingfield's two Listed races are priced widely enough (seven bookmaker groups) for us to work out fair chances with the margin removed. The Irish card at Thurles had not been priced at all when we wrote this. Everything here comes from The Racing API racecards and bookmaker feed.</p>", "<h2>The feature races: Lingfield's Listed double</h2><p>Lingfield's card is part of the <b>All-Weather Championships</b>, which began their fourteenth season this autumn, and two races carry Listed status and prize money far above anything else on Thursday.</p>", "<h3>2.03 · River Eden EBF Fillies' Stakes (Listed), 1m5f (14 runners)</h3><p>Worth £39,697, the most valuable race of the day. John and Thady Gosden's <b>Morrophore</b> (Ryan Moore) is rated 104, with form figures 41-103; she last ran 64 days ago. Ralph Beckett's <b>Likealot</b> (Rossa Ryan), rated 102 with form 221-41, returns from 117 days off. Roger Varian's <b>Little Dorrit</b> (101) and a second Gosden runner, <b>Yamnah</b> (83, form 421), complete the front of the betting.</p><p>With the bookmakers' margin removed, the market makes <b>Morrophore about 25%</b> (a fair price of about 4.0) and <b>Likealot about 22%</b> (about 4.5). The best early prices, 3.5 and 4.0, are both <b>shorter</b> than those fair prices, so the early market here is tight rather than generous. A 14-runner Listed race over a mile and five furlongs, with the two market leaders both around 1 in 4, is far from a two-horse race.</p>", "<h3>2.38 · Fleur De Lys Fillies' Stakes (Listed), 1m (12 runners)</h3><p>Worth £34,026. George Boughey's <b>Enchanted Queen</b> (Billy Loughnane) comes here on a winning run: her form reads 232-11 and she last ran 48 days ago. She is rated 94, well below <b>Kon Tiki</b> (Jane Chapple-Hyam, Ryan Moore, rated 107) and James Fanshawe's <b>Sky Safari</b> (106). Andrew Balding's <b>Pacific Mission</b> (101) ran only 19 days ago.</p><p>The market's fair chances: <b>Enchanted Queen about 26%</b> (fair price about 3.8), <b>Kon Tiki about 19%</b> (about 5.3) and <b>Sky Safari about 15%</b> (about 6.9). Again the best early prices (3.5, 5.0 and 6.5) are a little shorter than fair. The interesting question is ratings against form: Enchanted Queen is the improving filly, Kon Tiki and Sky Safari have the higher marks.</p>", "<h2>Lingfield: the rest of the card</h2>", "<h3>11.18 · Novice stakes, 1m (9 runners)</h3><p>Harry Eustace's <b>Silk Braid</b> (Kaiya Fraser), rated 92 with form 1-2, is around 8/13 on a first run for 208 days. The Kublers' <b>Nascondino</b> (78) ran 24 days ago and is next at around 5/2.</p>", "<h3>11.48 · Nursery, 7f (9 runners)</h3><p>A tight race: Richard Hughes' <b>Boy Basilica</b> around 7/2, with <b>Rogue Imperial</b> (Archie Watson, rated 82, form 537211), <b>Rosevannion</b> (83) and Balding's <b>Show Me Glory</b> (form 321) all around 4/1.</p>", "<h3>12.18 and 12.53 · Restricted fillies' maidens, 7f</h3><p>Owen Burrows' unraced <b>Valley Rising</b> heads the first division at around 2/1. In the second, Tim Easterby's <b>Classic Bellini</b> (form 73632, last run 14 days ago) and Balding's newcomer <b>Star Run</b> are joint favourites at around 9/4.</p>", "<h3>1.28 · Fillies' handicap, 6f (5 runners)</h3><p>Ralph Beckett's <b>Zooella</b> (rated 83, form -11051) is around 15/8, with John Wainwright's <b>Startling</b> (form 211081) at around 3/1.</p>", "<h3>3.08 and 3.40 · The handicaps</h3><p>The 3.08 is a 14-runner Class 6 race with no favourite shorter than 9/2: Marco Botti's <b>Orange Emperor</b> heads the betting. In the 3.40 sprint, Clive Cox's <b>Got The Booty</b> (Rossa Ryan) is around 2/1.</p>", "<h2>Warwick: the veterans' chase and a day of novices</h2><p>Warwick has eight races over jumps on good ground. Two trainers dominate the entries: <b>Olly Murphy</b>, who has runners right through the card with Sean Bowen riding several of them (two of the races are named after his yard's sponsorship), and <b>Nicky Henderson</b>.</p>", "<h3>1.37 · Veterans' handicap chase, 3m (8 runners, Class 2)</h3><p>The best race at Warwick, worth £15,609, and leg nine of the Stayers' Veterans' Chase series. Rebecca Curtis' <b>The Boola Boss</b> (Jamie Brace, rated 120, form 341-31) is the early favourite at around 3/1. Olly Murphy's <b>Sure Touch</b> (129) is around 4/1, with Jamie Snowden's <b>Git Maker</b> (132) and Tom Lacey's <b>Nocte Volatus</b> (134, form F117-4) at around 5/1. Git Maker returns from 215 days off. In a race for horses aged ten and over, fitness and the handicapper's view of old form matter as much as raw ability.</p>", "<h3>11.27 · Fillies' juvenile hurdle (4 runners)</h3><p>Four runners, none with a run listed. James Owen's <b>Saq of Gold</b> and Harry Derham's <b>Leslie's Pride</b> are close in the betting at around 6/4 and 7/4.</p>", "<h3>11.57 and 12.27 · Maiden hurdles (two divisions)</h3><p>The first division is a good one. Olly Murphy's <b>L'Unique Allen</b> (second last time), Alan King's <b>Friendly Glance</b> (a winner last time) and Henderson's <b>St Gabriel</b> (also a last-time winner) are all between 5/2 and 3/1. Paul Nicholls' <b>Artaban</b> is around 13/2. In the second division, Ben Pauling's <b>Smile John Boy</b> and Murphy's <b>Rock On Ronnie</b>, both third or second last time out 21 days ago, head the betting.</p>", "<h3>1.02 · Mares' novices' hurdle (10 runners)</h3><p>Dan Skelton's <b>Capricciose</b> (form 315-3) is around 2/1. Murphy's <b>Largy Force</b>, rated 127 and a winner last time (form 1212-1), is close behind at around 9/4 on her first run for 189 days.</p>", "<h3>2.12, 2.47 and 3.17 · Mares' races</h3><p>The 2.12 mares' handicap hurdle is open: Murphy's <b>Easy To Follow</b> and Fergal O'Brien's <b>Strong Run</b> (rated 117) at around 7/2. In the 2.47 mares' chase, Kim Bailey and Mat Nicholls' <b>Coolanna</b> (form 2/114-) is around 15/8 on her first run for 264 days. The card ends with a 17-runner mares' bumper headed by Skelton's newcomer <b>Elodie Bijou</b>.</p>", "<h2>Newcastle: the evening card</h2><p>Seven races on the all-weather from 3.35pm to 7.40pm (10.35am to 2.40pm Eastern).</p>", "<h3>3.35 · Handicap, 1m2f (8 runners, Class 2)</h3><p>Worth £13,190, the best race at Newcastle. The Gosdens' <b>Water To Wine</b> (Robert Havlin, rated 102, form 1130) is favourite at around 9/4. Tim Easterby's <b>Stoneacre Donny</b> (91) ran only 13 days ago and is around 11/4; Iain Jardine's <b>Paddy The Squire</b> is rated 104, the highest of the front four in the betting.</p>", "<h3>4.10, 5.10 and 5.40 · Novice stakes</h3><p>William Haggas' unraced <b>El Ra'ady</b> heads the 4.10 at around 13/8. The two divisions of the EBF novice stakes over seven furlongs are both small fields: Archie Watson's <b>High Threshold</b> (Hollie Doyle), second on the only previous start, is around 11/8 in the first; in the second, K. R. Burke's newcomer <b>Sede Vacante</b> and Varian's unraced <b>On A Boat That Day</b> are around 7/4 and 2/1.</p>", "<h3>The handicaps</h3><p>Ed Dunlop's <b>Starmade</b> (rated 85) is the clearest favourite of the evening at around 9/4 in the 6.40. The 6.10, 7.10 and 7.40 are 14-runner handicaps with favourites at 4/1 or bigger, the kind of races where the early price tells you little.</p>", "<h2>Thurles</h2><p>Seven races over jumps on good ground, from 12.08pm to 3.30pm. No bookmaker had priced the card when we wrote this. The names to note: <b>Willie Mullins</b> runs <b>Doctor Steinberg</b> (Paul Townend) in the 12.08 beginners' chase, and <b>Gordon Elliott</b> has <b>Brother Walfrid</b> in the four-runner novice hurdle at 1.15, where <b>Minella Emperor</b> (Emmet Mullins) is the top-rated runner on 124 and <b>Hi Ho Idaho</b> (Cian Collins) is rated 121. The two William Hill Challenge Series handicaps (12.40 chase and 2.25 hurdle) have 15 and 17 runners.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Early prices and fair chances from The Racing API at about 4pm UK on Wednesday 18 November; fair chances only for races priced by at least six bookmaker groups. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["The Racing API racecards and bookmaker feed, Thursday 19 November 2026", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Tennis", "Basketball", "Darts", "Ice hockey", "Handball", "Snooker"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "other-sports-wednesday-1811", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Other sports · Wed 18 November", "title": "Wednesday's other sports: Alcaraz v Medvedev in Turin, Real Madrid in Istanbul, the Grand Slam of Darts and a ten-game NBA night", "dek": "ATP Finals singles and doubles, Euroleague, Grand Slam of Darts, Norwegian and European handball, then the NBA, NHL and college basketball overnight. The sharp market's numbers for each, plus running in these sports.", "date": "2026-11-18T12:18:00+03:00", "read": 3, "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan at 12:02 EAT (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]], "body": ["<p>Tennis from Turin this afternoon, Real Madrid in Istanbul this evening, the darts in Wolverhampton and a ten-game NBA night after midnight. All chances below come from the sharp Pinnacle market with the bookmaker margin removed, taken at 12:02 Kampala time, so they will move. Times are Kampala (EAT); UK is three hours behind.</p>", "<h2>Tennis · ATP Finals, Turin</h2><p><b>Carlos Alcaraz v Daniil Medvedev, 16:00:</b> the market makes Alcaraz a <b>79%</b> favourite, so Medvedev wins this about one time in five.</p><p><b>Marcel Granollers and Horacio Zeballos v Kevin Krawietz and Tim Puetz, 20:00:</b> a near coin flip in the doubles, <b>51%</b> to 49% for Granollers and Zeballos.</p><p><b>Flavio Cobolli v Frances Tiafoe, 22:30:</b> Tiafoe is the narrow favourite at <b>56%</b>.</p>", "<h2>Darts · Grand Slam of Darts</h2><ul><li><b>Rob Cross v Niko Springer, 22:15:</b> Cross 63%.</li><li><b>Gerwyn Price v Ryan Searle, 23:15:</b> Price 71%.</li><li><b>Luke Littler v Jermaine Wattimena, 00:15:</b> Littler <b>88%</b>, the biggest favourite of the night.</li><li><b>Kevin Doets v Cameron Menzies, 01:15:</b> Doets 62%.</li></ul>", "<h2>NBA overnight</h2><ul><li><b>Pacers v Rockets (03:00):</b> Rockets 72% away, −7, total 225.</li><li><b>Heat v Bucks (03:00):</b> Heat 87%, −12.5.</li><li><b>Cavaliers v Grizzlies (03:00):</b> Cavaliers 65%, −5, total 235.5.</li><li><b>Magic v 76ers (03:00):</b> 76ers 55% away, −2.</li><li><b>Thunder v Hawks (04:00):</b> Thunder 72%, −4.5.</li><li><b>Mavericks v Warriors (04:00):</b> Mavericks 68%, −6.</li><li><b>Pistons at Pelicans (04:00):</b> Pistons 72% away, −7.5.</li><li><b>Nuggets v Celtics (05:30):</b> Celtics 56% away, −2, total 222.</li><li><b>Kings v Suns (06:00):</b> Suns 70% away, −6.</li><li><b>Lakers v Jazz (06:00):</b> Lakers 78%.</li></ul>", "<h2>College basketball and NHL</h2><p><b>Houston v Florida (00:30)</b> is the pick of the college games: Florida 54% away, −2, total 149. <b>Arizona v UConn (05:00)</b> has Arizona 60% at home. In the NHL, <b>Red Wings v Bruins (03:30)</b> is priced at exactly 50/50, and <b>Devils v Canadiens (03:30)</b> has the Devils 61%.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Prices move; numbers taken at 12:02 EAT on 18 November. Times are Kampala (EAT). No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"]}, {"topics": ["Regulation", "UK"], "sports": [], "id": "gambling-policy-commission-review-1811", "section": "news", "kicker": "News · UK regulation", "title": "Does Britain need a new Gambling Act? An independent commission of economists wants your evidence", "dek": "The Gambling Policy Commission, chaired by Cambridge economist Jagjit Chadha, has opened a review of the 2005 Act. It is not a government inquiry, but it is asking the questions the next round of reform will have to answer.", "date": "2026-11-18T12:18:00+03:00", "read": 3, "sources": [["Casinos.com: Gambling Policy Commission opens independent review of UK Gambling Act (10 Nov 2026)", "https://www.casinos.com/uk/news/gambling-policy-commission-opens-independent-review-of-uk-gambling-act"], ["CDC Gaming / iGB: New commission opens review into whether UK needs a new Gambling Act (9 Nov 2026)", "https://cdcgaming.com/brief/new-commission-opens-review-into-whether-uk-needs-a-new-gambling-act/"]], "body": ["<p>More than twenty years after the Gambling Act 2005 became the legal backbone of betting and gambling in Great Britain, a new body has opened a review into whether it is still fit for purpose. The <b>Gambling Policy Commission</b> launched its review on 9 November and has asked researchers, consumers, the industry, regulators and policymakers to send evidence.</p>", "<h2>Who is behind it</h2><p>The commission describes itself as independent of government, political parties, regulators and the industry. It is chaired by <b>Professor Jagjit Chadha OBE</b>, a Cambridge economist and former director of the National Institute of Economic and Social Research, with <b>Professor James Reade</b> of the University of Reading as chief economist. Its members come mostly from economics, with others from law, public policy, health and sport; several have advised the Bank of England or HM Treasury.</p><p>Chadha's case for the review is simple: the last full, independent look at gambling law was done about a quarter of a century ago, before smartphones, in-play betting on every game, or the online casino boom.</p>", "<h2>What it is looking at</h2><ul><li>How the 2005 Act works today, and the effect of the reforms that followed the 2023 White Paper (stake limits on online slots, financial risk checks, the statutory levy).</li><li>Gambling taxation.</li><li>Illegal and offshore gambling.</li><li>Gambling-related harm and consumer protection.</li><li>Gambling and sport.</li><li>New technology and products.</li><li>How regulation is coordinated across government.</li></ul><p>The commission says it particularly wants empirical evidence and comparisons with other countries.</p>", "<h2>What it can and cannot do</h2><p>This is <b>not a government review</b> and it has <b>no power to change the law</b>. That matters: the formal route for change still runs through the Department for Culture, Media and Sport, Parliament and the Gambling Commission, the regulator. What an independent review can do is gather evidence in one place and set out options that ministers and MPs then have to respond to. Reviews of this kind often shape the terms of the next debate.</p>", "<h2>What it means for punters</h2><p>Nothing changes for bettors today. But the topics on the list are the ones that touch everyday betting: how affordability and risk checks work, how tax on operators flows through to prices and promotions, and how hard it is for people to end up on unlicensed sites. If you have a view, or data, the commission is asking for it.</p>", "<h2>How to respond</h2><p>Submissions go through the commission's website as PDF documents by <b>12 February 2027</b>. The commission plans to publish what it receives; contributors can mark parts as confidential. No date has been given yet for its final report.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. BetMeisters.news has no commercial relationship with the commission. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "id": "how-to-read-a-racecard", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Horse racing", "title": "How to read a racecard: form figures, weights, ratings, headgear and the symbols that matter", "dek": "A racecard packs a horse's whole story into one line. Here is what every part of it means, using a real handicap at Kempton tonight, and how to use it without getting lost in the detail.", "date": "2026-11-18T07:37:00+03:00", "body": ["<p>A racecard looks like a code the first time you see one: strings of numbers and letters, weights in stones and pounds, odd symbols after a horse's name. But every part of it means something simple, and once you know the code you can read a horse's recent career in a few seconds. This guide goes through a card from top to bottom, using a real race: the <b>7.00pm at Kempton tonight, Wednesday 18 November</b> (10.00pm Kampala, 2.00pm ET), a Class 2 handicap over a mile on the all-weather.</p><p>Racecards look slightly different in a newspaper, on a racecourse programme and on a betting site, but the information is the same. 18+.</p>", "<h3>The race heading</h3><p>Before the runners, the card tells you what kind of race it is. For tonight's Kempton race it reads, in short: <b>7.00 · London Mile Series Qualifier Handicap · Class 2 · 0-105 · 3yo+ · 1m · Polytrack, standard to slow · 14 runners · £13,400</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Class</b> runs from 1 (the best races, including Group and Listed races on the Flat and Graded races over jumps) down to 7. Class 2 is a good-quality race.</li><li><b>0-105</b> is the <b>rating band</b>: only horses with an official rating up to 105 can run (a horse a pound or two above can still get in, carrying extra weight). You only see a band in handicaps.</li><li><b>3yo+</b> means horses aged three or older.</li><li><b>Distance</b> is in miles, furlongs and yards. A furlong is 220 yards, so 8 furlongs make a mile; a card might say 1m7f182y, which is just short of two miles.</li><li><b>Going</b> is the official description of the ground: from heavy and soft through good to firm on turf, and standard, standard to slow or slow on all-weather surfaces. It changes during the day, so check it on the morning of the race. At Hexham today it was updated to good, good to soft in places.</li><li><b>Race type</b>: Flat, hurdle, chase (steeplechase), or a National Hunt flat race, known as a bumper.</li></ul>", "<h3>One runner, line by line</h3><p>Here are four of tonight's 14 runners as the card shows them. We will use them to explain each column.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto\"><table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>No. (draw)</th><th>Horse</th><th>Form</th><th>Age</th><th>Weight</th><th>OR</th><th>Jockey</th><th>Trainer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 (11)</td><td>Ebt's Guard <small>D</small></td><td>944332</td><td>5</td><td>10-0</td><td>104</td><td>Rhys Elliott (5)</td><td>William Muir &amp; Chris Grassick</td></tr><tr><td>9 (1)</td><td>Palladas <small>CD BF</small></td><td>114</td><td>3</td><td>9-3</td><td>95</td><td>Connor Planas</td><td>Charlie Appleby</td></tr><tr><td>13 (8)</td><td>Oliver Show (tp) <small>CD</small></td><td>442766</td><td>6</td><td>8-13</td><td>89</td><td>Toby Moore (7)</td><td>George Boughey</td></tr><tr><td>14 (3)</td><td>Princling <small>D</small></td><td>-14563</td><td>3</td><td>8-12</td><td>90</td><td>M. P. Sheehy</td><td>William Haggas</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Number and draw.</b> The first number is the saddlecloth. The number in brackets is the <b>draw</b>, the starting stall. Draws only exist on the Flat, because jumps races start from a tape. Palladas is drawn 1, the stall nearest the inside rail; Ebt's Guard is drawn 11, out wide. Whether a low or high draw helps depends on the course and distance, and on some tracks it matters a lot.</p><p><b>Name and symbols.</b> Small letters after a name show past wins: <b>C</b> means a course winner, <b>D</b> a distance winner, and <b>CD</b> a winner over this course and distance. <b>BF</b> means the horse was a <b>beaten favourite</b> last time. So Palladas has already won over a mile at Kempton, and started favourite and lost on his latest run. Letters in brackets are <b>headgear</b> (see below): Oliver Show wears (tp).</p><p><b>Age.</b> In Britain and Ireland every horse has an official birthday of 1 January, so a three-year-old tonight is in its third calendar year of age, whatever its actual foaling date.</p><p><b>Trainer.</b> Many cards also show how the trainer is doing. In the 14 days to Wednesday, Charlie Appleby had two winners from four runners (50%) and William Haggas two from seven (29%). Trainer form over a short period is a hint, not proof: a small number of runners can make a strike rate look very good or very bad.</p>", "<h3>Form figures: reading the string</h3><p>The form figures are a horse's recent finishing positions, <b>read from left to right, with the most recent run on the right</b>. Numbers 1 to 9 are finishing positions, and <b>0</b> means the horse finished tenth or worse. Two symbols mark breaks: a <b>hyphen (-)</b> separates seasons, and a <b>slash (/)</b> means a longer gap, two seasons or more.</p><p>Letters show runs where the horse did not finish, which mostly happen over jumps:</p><ul><li><b>F</b> fell · <b>U</b> unseated rider · <b>P</b> pulled up · <b>B</b> brought down · <b>R</b> refused · <b>S</b> slipped up · <b>V</b> the race was void.</li></ul><p>So in tonight's race:</p><ul><li><b>Palladas, 114:</b> won his first two races, then finished fourth last time.</li><li><b>Princling, -14563:</b> the hyphen shows a new season started before his first run this year. Since then he has finished 1st, 4th, 5th, 6th and, last time, 3rd.</li><li><b>Ebt's Guard, 944332:</b> ninth, two fourths, two thirds and a second. Consistent, but without a win in those six runs.</li><li><b>Oliver Show, 442766:</b> two fourths and a second, then 7th and two 6ths: his recent form has tailed off.</li></ul><p>Form figures tell you where a horse finished, not how good the race was or how far it was beaten. A third in a Class 2 handicap can be better form than a win in Class 6. That is why form figures are a starting point, not the whole story.</p>", "<h3>Days since the last run</h3><p>Most cards show a number of days since the horse last ran. Tonight Oliver Show ran 20 days ago and Palladas 26; Princling has had 47 days off. Some cards show a second figure in brackets for a run under a different code, for example a jumps horse that last ran on the Flat. A long break is not a bad sign in itself, but it is a question: some horses go well fresh, others need a run.</p>", "<h3>Weight, official ratings and how a handicap works</h3><p>Weights are shown in <b>stones and pounds</b>. A stone is 14 pounds, so 10-0 is 140lb and 9-3 is 129lb. The weight includes the jockey and the saddle, with lead added to reach the right figure.</p><p>The <b>OR</b> column is the horse's <b>official rating</b>, set by the British Horseracing Authority's handicappers and updated every week. In a <b>handicap</b>, the weights are set from those ratings: the highest-rated horse carries top weight, and the others carry less in proportion, so that in theory every horse has the same chance. Each point of rating is worth one pound.</p><p>You can see it working in tonight's race. Ebt's Guard (rated 104) carries 10-0. Ebt's Guard and the four-year-old filly <b>Chantilly Lace</b> (103) are a pound apart in both rating and weight (139lb). The three-year-olds get a little more: Princling is rated 14 pounds below Ebt's Guard but carries 16 pounds less. The extra two pounds is the <b>weight-for-age</b> allowance, which younger horses receive against older ones.</p><p>In races that are <b>not</b> handicaps (maidens, novice races, Group races), weights are set by the race conditions instead: by age and sex, plus penalties for previous wins. There, a horse with a much higher rating can be meeting rivals on level weights, which is why the best horse often wins and the favourites are shorter.</p>", "<h3>The jockey's claim</h3><p>A number in brackets after a jockey's name is a <b>claim</b>: a weight allowance for a less experienced rider. Tonight <b>Rhys Elliott (5)</b> and <b>Toby Moore (7)</b> are claiming. Ebt's Guard is allotted 10-0 but actually carries 5lb less, 9-9; Oliver Show carries 7lb less than his 8-13. The claim reduces as the rider wins more races. A good young rider with a claim can be an advantage, because the horse carries less weight than its rating says it should.</p>", "<h3>Headgear codes</h3><p>Headgear is equipment worn to help a horse concentrate or settle. The common codes are:</p><ul><li><b>b</b> blinkers · <b>v</b> visor · <b>h</b> hood · <b>t</b> tongue-tie · <b>p</b> cheekpieces · <b>e/s</b> eye shield · <b>e/c</b> eye cover.</li></ul><p>A <b>1</b> after the code means the horse is wearing it for the first time, for example <b>b1</b> for first-time blinkers. That is worth noticing: a trainer only changes equipment when he or she thinks it might help. Oliver Show's <b>(tp)</b> means a tongue-tie and cheekpieces.</p>", "<h3>The market</h3><p>The last column on a betting site is the price. On Tuesday evening Palladas was about 13/8 favourite for tonight's race; by 7.30am Kampala on Wednesday he was about 6/5, with Princling about 9/4. A price is just the market's view of the chance (6/5 is about 45% before the bookmaker's margin, 9/4 about 31%), and it changes through the day. Our <a href=\"article.html#betting-odds-explained\">guide to betting odds</a> shows how to turn any price into a chance.</p><p>If a horse is withdrawn after you have taken a price, a <b>Rule 4</b> deduction may be taken from your winnings, because the rest of the field has a better chance. Our guide to <a href=\"article.html#best-odds-guaranteed-extra-places\">best odds guaranteed and extra places</a> explains how deductions and the starting price work.</p>", "<h3>A simple way to use a racecard</h3><ol><li><b>Start with the race.</b> Class, distance, going and whether it is a handicap tell you what kind of contest it is.</li><li><b>Look for the right symbols.</b> C, D and CD show horses that have won in similar conditions; BF and first-time headgear are worth a second look.</li><li><b>Read the form string from the right.</b> The last two or three runs matter most, and a hyphen or slash tells you about a break.</li><li><b>In a handicap, compare rating and weight.</b> A horse well in at the weights, or ridden by a good claimer, can carry less than its ability suggests.</li><li><b>Check the going and the draw on the day.</b> Both change, and both can matter more than the form.</li><li><b>Then look at the price.</b> The question is never just 'can it win?' but 'is the price bigger than its chance?'</li></ol>", "<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li><b>Reading form left to right as if the first figure were the latest.</b> It is the other way round.</li><li><b>Treating every win the same.</b> A win in a weak race is not the same as a place in a strong one.</li><li><b>Ignoring the going.</b> Some horses act on soft ground and others do not, and form on one may say little about the other.</li><li><b>Forgetting the claim.</b> The weight shown is not always the weight carried.</li><li><b>Overrating short-term trainer form.</b> Two winners from four runners is a good week, not a guarantee.</li></ul>", "<h3>FAQs</h3><p><b>What does 0 mean in form figures?</b> The horse finished tenth or worse.</p><p><b>Why do some form strings have letters?</b> They show runs where the horse did not finish: F fell, U unseated rider, P pulled up, B brought down, R refused.</p><p><b>What is the OR?</b> The official rating, the BHA's measure of a horse's ability in pounds. In handicaps it sets the weight carried.</p><p><b>What does CD mean?</b> The horse has won over this course and distance before.</p><p><b>Does the draw matter over jumps?</b> No. Jumps races start from a tape, so there is no draw.</p><p class=\"note\">18+. Racecard details for Kempton's 7.00pm on Wednesday 18 November are from The Racing API's racecards; prices were taken at about 7.30am Kampala and will move. BetMeisters.news has no affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["British Horseracing Authority: How handicap weights are determined", "https://www.britishhorseracing.com/?p=21685"], ["Horse & Hound: A bluffer's guide to reading a racecard (form letters, headgear codes, C/D/BF)", "https://www.horseandhound.co.uk/racing/how-to-read-a-racecard-917782"], ["Sporting Life: Understanding a racecard", "https://www.sportinglife.com/racing/news/understanding-a-racecard/190113"], ["The Racing API: racecards for Wednesday 18 November (Kempton 7.00pm)", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]], "read": 9}, {"topics": ["Sports betting", "Bonuses, rewards and VIP"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "id": "best-odds-guaranteed-extra-places", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Horse racing", "title": "Best odds guaranteed and extra places explained: what they pay, what they are worth and the terms to check", "dek": "Two of the most common racing concessions can make a real difference to what you are paid, but only if you understand the terms. Here is how they work, with each-way maths and real place terms from today's cards.", "date": "2026-11-18T07:37:00+03:00", "body": ["<p>Walk into any conversation about racing betting in Britain and two phrases come up: <b>best odds guaranteed</b> (BOG) and <b>extra places</b>. Both are concessions that bookmakers offer to win racing customers, and both can genuinely add to what you are paid. Neither is free money, and both come with terms. This guide explains how each one works, shows the each-way maths, and uses the place terms that bookmakers were offering on today's British cards.</p><p>As always, a better price is only worth having on a bet you meant to make anyway. 18+.</p>", "<h3>First, the starting price</h3><p>In British and Irish racing, every runner has an official <b>starting price (SP)</b>: its price at the moment the race starts. If you bet \"at SP\", you are paid at whatever that turns out to be. If you take a fixed price earlier (an early price, or the board price before the off) you are normally paid at that price, whatever happens to the SP.</p><p>Prices move all day as money comes in and as non-runners are announced. Some horses shorten, some drift. Taking an early price is a bet on the price as well as the horse.</p>", "<h3>What best odds guaranteed means</h3><p>With BOG, if you take a fixed price and the SP turns out to be <b>bigger</b>, you are paid at the SP. If the SP is shorter, you keep the price you took. You get whichever is better.</p><ul><li>You back a horse at <b>5/1</b> and it starts at <b>4/1</b>: you are paid at 5/1.</li><li>You back it at <b>5/1</b> and it starts at <b>6/1</b>: you are paid at 6/1.</li><li>You back it at <b>5/1</b> and it starts at 5/1: you are paid at 5/1.</li></ul><p>In money: a £10 win bet at 5/1 that wins returns £60. If the horse drifted to 8/1 and you had BOG, it returns <b>£90</b> instead. Without BOG it would still return £60.</p><p>BOG only matters when the horse wins (or places, for each-way bets), and only when it drifts. But it takes away one of the risks of betting early: that you take a price and the market then moves against you.</p>", "<h3>BOG and each-way bets</h3><p>Where BOG applies to each-way bets, the better price is used for both parts, so the place part is paid at the place fraction of the better price. If you back a horse at 8/1 each way, it drifts to 10/1 and finishes second with 1/5 place terms, the place part is paid at 10/1 × 1/5 = 2/1 instead of 8/5.</p>", "<h3>The terms to check</h3><p>BOG is a concession, not a rule, and every bookmaker sets its own terms. The common conditions are:</p><ul><li><b>Which races:</b> usually UK and Irish racing, sometimes only certain meetings or only from a certain time on the day of the race.</li><li><b>Which bets:</b> usually win, place and each-way singles. Accumulators and some promotional bets are often excluded.</li><li><b>Odds limits:</b> some bookmakers set a minimum price, and very short prices may not qualify.</li><li><b>Who gets it:</b> some offers are new-customer only, and free bets may be excluded.</li><li><b>Limits:</b> some bookmakers cap the extra amount BOG can add, or the stake it applies to.</li></ul><p>If a bookmaker removes BOG from your account, the only sign may be that the offer stops appearing on your bet slip. Check before you bet, not after the race.</p>", "<h3>Non-runners, Rule 4 and BOG</h3><p>When a horse is withdrawn after you have taken a price, the rest of the field has a better chance of winning, so the Tattersalls Committee's <b>Rule 4(C)</b> allows a deduction from your winnings. The size of the deduction depends on the withdrawn horse's price, from 90p in the pound for a 1/9 shot down to nothing for a horse over 14/1, and the total deduction is capped at 90p in the pound. Bets at SP are not affected, except when there was not enough time to form a new market.</p><p>This matters for BOG. After a non-runner, your fixed price may be reduced by Rule 4 while the SP is not, so the comparison is between your price after the deduction and the SP. How each bookmaker applies BOG when a Rule 4 deduction is in force is set out in its own terms. On Wednesday's cards, for example, Kalo Athena was taken out of the 1.33 at Hexham and My Gift To You out of the 2.38, both at prices around 6/1 to 10/1, so early bets on those races could carry small deductions.</p>", "<h3>Each-way place terms: the standard</h3><p>To understand extra places, you need the standard terms first. Unless a bookmaker displays different terms, each-way bets in Britain follow Tattersalls Rule 3:</p><table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>Runners</th><th>Race type</th><th>Place odds</th><th>Places paid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 to 4</td><td>Any</td><td>Win only (with 3-4, a bookmaker may offer 1/5, places 1-2)</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>5 to 7</td><td>Any</td><td>1/4</td><td>1-2</td></tr><tr><td>8 or more</td><td>Non-handicap</td><td>1/5</td><td>1-2-3</td></tr><tr><td>8 to 11</td><td>Handicap</td><td>1/5</td><td>1-2-3</td></tr><tr><td>12 to 15</td><td>Handicap</td><td>1/4</td><td>1-2-3</td></tr><tr><td>16 or more</td><td>Handicap</td><td>1/5 (1/4 with 22+)</td><td>1-2-3-4</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The terms are fixed by the number of runners when the bet is settled, which is why a non-runner can change the place terms. A handicap that drops from 16 runners to 15 goes from four places at 1/5 to three places at 1/4.</p>", "<h3>What extra places are</h3><p>An <b>extra place</b> offer means the bookmaker pays one or more places beyond the standard. In a 14-runner handicap the standard is 1/4 the odds for three places; with an extra place, it might pay four places at 1/5.</p><p>Extra places are now offered on ordinary weekday races, not just big festivals. In the racecard data for Wednesday's British races, taken at 4pm UK on Tuesday:</p><ul><li><b>Kempton 7.00</b> (14-runner handicap): of the 18 bookmakers showing each-way terms, <b>7 paid four places at 1/5</b> (10Bet, bet365, BetMGM, Betfred, BoyleSports, Unibet and William Hill) and 11 paid the standard three places at 1/4.</li><li><b>Ffos Las 2.18</b> (15-runner handicap chase): <b>10 of 19</b> paid four places at 1/5.</li><li><b>Kempton 8.00 and 8.30</b> (13-runner handicaps): 7 paid four places in each.</li><li><b>Hexham 3.08</b> (12-runner handicap chase): 5 paid four places.</li></ul><p>Every other British race on Wednesday was offered on the standard terms across the board, apart from one firm paying three places at 1/5 in Kempton's seven-runner 7.30. So extra places are mostly a <b>handicap</b> concession, concentrated in the bigger fields, and they vary between bookmakers in the same race. They are announced race by race and can change, so check the terms on the bet slip.</p>", "<h3>Is an extra place worth it? The each-way maths</h3><p>An extra place usually comes with a smaller fraction: four places at 1/5 instead of three at 1/4. So you gain a place but are paid less for each one. Here is a £5 each-way bet (£10 in total) at 10/1:</p><table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>Finishes</th><th>1/4, three places</th><th>1/5, four places</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st</td><td>£55 + £17.50 = £72.50</td><td>£55 + £15 = £70</td></tr><tr><td>2nd or 3rd</td><td>£17.50</td><td>£15</td></tr><tr><td>4th</td><td>£0</td><td>£15</td></tr><tr><td>5th or worse</td><td>£0</td><td>£0</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Which is better depends on how often the horse finishes fourth compared with how often it finishes in the first three. As an <b>illustration only</b>, take a horse with about a 9% chance of finishing in each of the first four positions. On the place part alone, the standard terms return about £0.95 per £1 staked (a 27% chance of £3.50), while the extra-place terms return about £1.08 (a 36% chance of £3). In this example the extra place turns a losing place bet into a winning one.</p><p>The extra place is worth most for horses with a real chance of finishing fourth but not much chance of winning: mid-priced runners in big handicaps. It is worth least for a short favourite, where the lower fraction cuts the place return and fourth place rarely comes into it.</p><p>One more point: the bookmakers paying extra places do not always have the best win price. A slightly bigger win price on standard terms can be worth more than an extra place on a shorter price, especially for horses near the front of the market.</p>", "<h3>BOG and extra places together</h3><p>Many bookmakers offer both on the same race. Combined, they help with two separate risks: BOG protects you if the price drifts after you bet, and the extra place pays out on a fourth (or fifth) that would otherwise lose. But they are still bets with a bookmaker's margin built in, and the same terms apply: check the race qualifies, the bet type qualifies, and the place terms are the ones on your bet slip.</p>", "<h3>A quick checklist</h3><ol><li>Is the race covered by BOG, and is your bet type included?</li><li>What are the each-way terms: how many places, at what fraction?</li><li>Is an extra place on offer, and is it on the bet slip?</li><li>How does the win price compare across bookmakers? An extra place does not make up for a much shorter price.</li><li>Has a horse been withdrawn? A Rule 4 deduction or a change in place terms can follow.</li></ol>", "<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li><b>Assuming BOG is always on.</b> It is a concession with its own terms, and it can be removed from an account.</li><li><b>Forgetting the place fraction.</b> An extra place at 1/5 pays less per place than three places at 1/4.</li><li><b>Chasing extra places on short favourites.</b> The value is mainly in mid-priced runners in big fields.</li><li><b>Ignoring non-runners.</b> A withdrawal can change the place terms and bring a Rule 4 deduction.</li></ul>", "<h3>FAQs</h3><p><b>Does BOG cost anything?</b> No: you are simply paid at the better of your price and the SP. But it does not change the margin built into the prices.</p><p><b>Does BOG apply to accumulators?</b> Often not. Check the bookmaker's terms.</p><p><b>Are extra places a promotion?</b> They are a concession offered race by race. They usually apply to each-way bets on the named race only.</p><p><b>What happens to each-way terms if there is a non-runner?</b> They are set by the number of runners when the bet is settled, so a withdrawal can reduce the number of places paid.</p><p class=\"note\">18+. Place terms are from The Racing API's racecard data for Wednesday 18 November at about 4pm UK on Tuesday, and bookmakers can change them at any time. BetMeisters.news has no affiliate links and is not paid by any bookmaker named here. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["Tattersalls Committee: Rules on each-way betting terms, dead heats and Rule 4 (via Sandown Park)", "https://int.thejockeyclub.co.uk/globalassets/racecourses/sandown-park/plan-your-day/tatts_rules_each_way_betting_terms_jan_2015.pdf"], ["Sporting Life: Best odds guaranteed and the starting price explained", "https://www.sportinglife.com/free-bets/news/best-odds-guaranteed-starting-price/277"], ["The Racing API: racecards and each-way terms for Wednesday 18 November", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]], "read": 8}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Basketball", "Ice hockey"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "us-sports-tuesday-night-1711", "section": "previews", "kicker": "US sports · Tuesday night, 17 November", "title": "Tuesday night in the US: Duke v Illinois, the Knicks in Toronto and a full NHL slate", "dek": "College basketball's big Tuesday, five NBA games and eleven NHL games. Every main game with its chance on the sharp market, the spread and the total.", "date": "2026-11-17T22:36:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>Tuesday night in the US is a full card: dozens of college basketball games, five NBA games and eleven NHL games. The headline game in college hoops is <b>Duke v Illinois</b>; in the NBA, the <b>Knicks</b> are in Toronto.</p><p>The chances below come from <b>Pinnacle</b>, the sharpest bookmaker in the market, with its margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at 7pm UK (2pm ET). They show what the market thinks. US markets are mostly spreads and totals: where we quote a spread or total, it is the line the market prices closest to 50/50. Times are UK, with US Eastern in brackets.</p>", "<h2>College basketball</h2>", "<h3>Duke v Illinois · midnight UK (7.00pm ET)</h3><p>The marquee game of the night. The market makes <b>Duke 77.5%</b> to win and <b>Illinois 22.5%</b>. The spread sits at <b>Duke −8</b>, priced close to 50/50, and the total at about <b>167</b>, the highest of the big college games tonight. A Duke win is the expected result; whether they win by more than eight is the real question.</p>", "<h3>Kansas v UNLV · 2.30am UK (9.30pm ET)</h3><p>Kansas are big favourites with the spread at <b>Kansas −17</b> (about 49% to cover) and a total around 157.</p>", "<h3>The close ones</h3><ul><li><b>West Virginia v Auburn</b> (5.00am UK, midnight ET): a genuine coin flip, West Virginia 50.2%, Auburn 49.8%.</li><li><b>UCF v Mississippi State</b> (midnight UK): Mississippi State 50.7%, total about 155.</li><li><b>Tennessee v Michigan State</b> (2.00am UK, 9.00pm ET): Michigan State 56.4%, spread −2.5.</li><li><b>Pittsburgh v Villanova</b> (midnight UK): Villanova 53.5%, spread −1.5.</li><li><b>Arkansas v Indiana</b> (12.30am UK): Indiana 54.4%.</li><li><b>Liberty v Southern Illinois</b> (11.00pm UK): Southern Illinois 55.0%, with +2 the 50/50 line for Liberty.</li></ul>", "<h3>Big spreads</h3><p>Early-season college schedules include a lot of mismatches. The market's biggest lines tonight include <b>Miami −41.5</b> against Rider, <b>Vanderbilt −41.5</b> against Eastern Illinois, <b>Xavier −33</b> against Cleveland State, <b>DePaul −30.5</b> against Maryland Eastern Shore and <b>Providence −30</b> against Sacred Heart. Spreads that big are close to 50/50 by design, and the end of the game matters: a big favourite that rests its starters can fail to cover a 40-point line even while winning easily.</p>", "<h2>NBA</h2><ul><li><b>Knicks at Raptors</b> (12.30am UK, 7.30pm ET): Knicks 61.3%, spread Knicks −4.5, total about 214.5, the lowest of the five games.</li><li><b>Trail Blazers at Timberwolves</b> (1.00am UK, 8.00pm ET): the closest NBA game, Minnesota 53.6%, spread −2, total about 227, the highest of the night.</li><li><b>Pistons at Spurs</b> (1.00am UK, 8.00pm ET): San Antonio 79.2%, spread Spurs −10, total about 223.</li><li><b>Jazz at Clippers</b> (3.30am UK, 10.30pm ET): Clippers 68.1%, spread −6, total about 225.5.</li><li><b>Hornets at Lakers</b> (4.00am UK, 11.00pm ET): Lakers 72.4%, spread −7, total about 220.5.</li></ul><p>All five spreads are close to 50/50 at the numbers above, which is how the market sets them. The money line tells you who is likely to win; the spread tells you by how much.</p>", "<h2>NHL</h2><p>Eleven games, most of them close on the money line, which is normal in hockey.</p><ul><li><b>Oilers at Panthers</b> (midnight UK, 7.00pm ET): Florida 54.0%, Edmonton 46.0%.</li><li><b>Capitals at Lightning</b> (12.30am UK): Tampa Bay 60.0%, total about 6.5.</li><li><b>Maple Leafs at Blackhawks</b> (12.30am UK): Toronto 56.4% away, total about 6.5.</li><li><b>Kings at Bruins</b> (midnight UK): Boston 59.1%.</li><li><b>Predators at Wild</b> (1.00am UK): Minnesota 65.2%, the biggest favourite of the night.</li><li><b>Stars at Golden Knights</b> (3.00am UK, 10.00pm ET): Vegas 55.2%.</li></ul><p>The other games: Columbus at the Islanders (New York 56.6%), Sabres at Flyers (Buffalo 54.3% away), Senators at Jets (Ottawa 55.1% away), Ducks at Blues (St. Louis 54.9%) and Flames at Sharks (San Jose 55.1%). Hockey money lines include overtime and shootouts unless the market says '60 minutes' or 'regulation time'.</p>", "<h2>Reading US lines</h2><p>US markets look different from the football markets most UK readers know. Three things are worth knowing before a night like this.</p><ul><li><b>Spreads are set to split the money, not to predict the margin exactly.</b> When the market makes Duke −8 a 50/50 proposition, it is saying that Duke winning by nine or more is as likely as anything else. A favourite at 77.5% to win can still be a coin flip against the spread.</li><li><b>Half-points matter.</b> A spread of −8 can push (stake returned) on an eight-point win; −8.5 cannot. In college basketball the most common winning margins sit in single figures, so moving across a key number changes the chance more than it looks.</li><li><b>Totals include overtime.</b> In basketball, an overtime period adds points to the total, which is a small boost for overs in close games. Hockey totals and money lines include overtime and shootouts unless they say otherwise.</li></ul><p>The money-line chances above are the clearest guide to who is likely to win. If you want a quick way to see how one-sided a game is, compare the two: Spurs at 79.2% with a 10-point spread, Lakers at 72.4% with 7 points, Knicks at 61.3% with 4.5.</p>", "<h2>Early-season form</h2><p>A month into the NBA season, the market still leans partly on pre-season ratings, and each new result moves it. That is why spreads and totals can move a point or two through the day, especially when injury news lands. In college basketball, November games come before conference play, so many lines rest on a handful of games and last season's ratings. That is one reason college spreads move more than NBA spreads between the opening line and tip-off.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle prices with the margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at about 7pm UK on Tuesday 17 November. Times are UK, with US Eastern where shown. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan, 17 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "event", "id": "preview-newcastle-arsenal-gw11", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Advance preview · Premier League Gameweek 11", "title": "Newcastle v Arsenal: the leaders go to St James' Park as 61% favourites", "dek": "Saturday 21 November, 5.30pm UK. Arsenal top the table on 27 points from ten games; Newcastle are 12th under Matthias Jaissle and have won one of their last five in all competitions. The early sharp market, the goals lines and what to watch before kick-off.", "date": "2026-11-17T19:08:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>The Premier League returns from the international break on Saturday, and the evening game is <b>Newcastle United v Arsenal</b> at St James' Park, kick-off <b>5.30pm UK</b> (12.30pm ET). The leaders, who have dropped points only once in the league all season, travel to a Newcastle side in mid-table and short of form.</p><p>This is an <b>advance preview</b>, written four days before kick-off. Team news, injuries and prices will change after the last international games this week. We will update the numbers in our weekend football preview on Thursday. Everything below is sourced, and the prices are the sharp market's early view.</p>", "<h2>Where the two clubs stand</h2><p>After ten games, <b>Arsenal are top on 27 points</b>, five clear of Brighton (22) and six clear of Manchester City (21). Twenty-seven points from ten games means nine wins and one defeat. They went into the break on a run of league wins, including <b>1-0 at Liverpool</b> on 1 November and <b>1-0 at home to Hull</b> on 7 November. In Europe they won 2-1 at Slavia Prague and drew 2-2 with Bayern Munich. Kai Havertz is their leading scorer.</p><p><b>Newcastle are 12th on 12 points</b>. It is their first season under <b>Matthias Jaissle</b>, who was appointed head coach on 5 August after Eddie Howe left the club. Their recent run is poor: a 1-1 draw at Fulham on 7 November, before that a 1-0 home defeat by Everton, a 3-1 League Cup defeat at Everton, a 2-0 win at Crystal Palace and a 1-0 home defeat by Aston Villa. Earlier in the season they lost 4-1 at Coventry. Anthony Elanga is their leading scorer.</p><p>The recent meetings have been tight. Arsenal won both league games last season: <b>2-1 at St James' Park</b> in September 2025 and <b>1-0 at the Emirates</b> in April 2026.</p>", "<h2>What the market says</h2><p>On Tuesday afternoon (UK), Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed gave:</p><ul><li><b>Arsenal win: 61.0%</b> (fair price about 1.64)</li><li><b>Draw: 22.3%</b> (about 4.48)</li><li><b>Newcastle win: 16.7%</b> (about 5.99)</li></ul><p>That has not moved since Monday, when we first priced Gameweek 11. Arsenal are the second-shortest away favourites of the round, behind only Brighton at Hull (63.8%). For context, the market rated Liverpool only 54% at home to Manchester United in our <a href=\"article.html#preview-liverpool-man-utd-gw11\">Liverpool v Manchester United preview</a>.</p><p>On the Asian handicap, the main line is <b>Arsenal −1</b>, priced close to even money (about 49% for Arsenal to cover). A whole-goal line works differently from a half-goal line: a two-goal Arsenal win pays, a one-goal Arsenal win returns the stake, and a draw or Newcastle win loses.</p>", "<h3>Goals</h3><p>The market expects a fairly normal game for goals. Over 2.5 goals is about <b>56%</b>, and the main line sits at 2.75 (about 51%). Over 3.5 is about 35%. Compare Liverpool v Manchester United, where over 2.5 is about 65%.</p><p>The team goals lines say more. The market makes it about <b>62%</b> that Newcastle score at least once, and about <b>59%</b> that Arsenal score two or more. So the most likely shape is an Arsenal win in which Newcastle still score: the market does not expect a clean sheet at St James' Park, even from the league leaders.</p>", "<h2>Reading the whole market</h2><p>The handicap and team-goals markets give a fuller picture than the match result alone. Pinnacle's lines, with the margin removed, make it:</p><ul><li><b>Arsenal to win by two or more</b>: about <b>37%</b> (Arsenal −1.5).</li><li><b>Arsenal to win by exactly one</b>: about <b>24%</b> (the 61% win chance minus the 37%).</li><li><b>Arsenal, draw no bet</b>: about <b>79%</b>. With the draw taken out, Arsenal win roughly four times in five.</li><li><b>Newcastle to score two or more</b>: about <b>25%</b>; <b>Arsenal to score three or more</b>: about <b>32%</b>.</li><li><b>Newcastle +1</b>: about <b>51%</b>, the line where the market splits the game in half.</li></ul><p>Put together, the market's central picture is a one- or two-goal Arsenal win in a game with two or three goals. The single most likely Arsenal margin is one goal, but a bigger win is only a little less likely.</p>", "<h2>Fantasy Premier League angle</h2><p>For FPL managers, the team-goals lines translate straight into the numbers that matter. Arsenal are about <b>59%</b> to score twice or more, which makes their attackers among the better captaincy options of the weekend, alongside the Liverpool and Manchester City players in higher-scoring games. Newcastle are about <b>62%</b> to score, so the market gives Arsenal's defenders only about a <b>38%</b> chance of a clean sheet, below what you might expect from the league leaders. Newcastle's defenders face a side the market makes 59% to score twice or more, which makes them hard to justify. Our full FPL weekend preview, with the deadline, goes up on Wednesday.</p>", "<h2>The fixture list around it</h2><p>Both clubs have a demanding few days. Arsenal play Newcastle on Saturday evening, <b>Borussia Dortmund</b> away in the Champions League on Tuesday 24 November, and <b>Manchester City</b> at home on Sunday 29 November, where the early market makes them 46.3%. Newcastle's next league game after this is <b>Brighton away</b> on 29 November, where Brighton are about 61%. For Newcastle, a home game against the leaders may be the best chance of points in the next fortnight; for Arsenal, it is the first of three hard games in nine days. That is the kind of context the market prices in, but it is also the kind of context that makes team selection uncertain until Friday.</p>", "<h2>The case each way</h2><p><b>For Arsenal:</b> nine wins from ten, away wins at Liverpool and Slavia Prague in the last month, and a Newcastle side that has lost three of its last five matches. The market's 61% reflects the gap between the top of the table and twelfth.</p><p><b>For Newcastle:</b> home advantage at one of the loudest grounds in the league, and the timing. Arsenal's players are spread across international squads this week and play Borussia Dortmund in the Champions League on 24 November, three days later. Saturday evening games after an international break can be flat for the bigger side. A draw or Newcastle win is about a 39% chance between them, which is far from nothing.</p>", "<h2>The rest of Gameweek 11</h2><p>The early market's view of the other key games:</p><ul><li><b>Liverpool v Manchester United</b> (Sun 4.30pm): Liverpool 54.3%, United 23.0%, over 2.5 goals 64.7%.</li><li><b>Hull v Brighton</b> (Sun 2.00pm): Brighton 63.8% away.</li><li><b>Brentford v Everton</b> (Mon 8.00pm): Brentford 46.1%, Everton 27.9%.</li></ul><p>And one date to note: Arsenal host Manchester City on Sunday 29 November. The early market makes Arsenal 46.3% for that one.</p>", "<h2>What to watch before Saturday</h2><ul><li><b>International fitness.</b> Both squads have players away this week. Injuries picked up in the last internationals often move prices on Thursday and Friday. We do not report team news until it is confirmed by the clubs or reputable outlets.</li><li><b>Friday's press conferences</b> are the first reliable guide to fitness for both sides.</li><li><b>Price movement.</b> If Arsenal shorten below about 1.60 before team news, the market is reacting to information. A drift past 1.70 would say the same in the other direction.</li></ul>", "<h2>The short version</h2><p>Arsenal are clear favourites at about 61%, Newcastle about 17%, the draw about 22%. The market expects Newcastle to score but Arsenal to score more. With the leaders three days away from a Champions League trip to Dortmund and Newcastle short of form, the price movement between now and Saturday will tell you more than the table does.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, taken at about 4.10pm UK on Tuesday 17 November, four days before kick-off. Table after 10 games; results and scorers as reported by the sources below. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Premier League table after 10 games (WithinNigeria, 9 Nov 2026)", "https://www.withinnigeria.com/2026/11/09/premier-league-table-after-10-games-arsenal-top-on-27-points-brighton-second-as-man-united-fall-into-the-relegation-zone/"], ["Sports Mole: Newcastle United vs Arsenal, recent results and head-to-head", "https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/premier-league/newcastle-united-vs-arsenal_game_263178.html"], ["Sky Sports: Newcastle United appoint Matthias Jaissle as head coach (5 Aug 2026)", "https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11678/13568428/newcastle-united-appoint-matthias-jaissle-as-head-coach-following-eddie-howes-departure-from-st-james-park"], ["Little Big Red Dot: Arsenal v Newcastle preview, kick-off and Dortmund date", "https://littlebigreddot.com/arsenal-newcastle-preview-st-james-park-21-november-2026/"], ["Pinnacle prices, 17 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "daily-football", "id": "football-wednesday-1811", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Daily football · Wednesday 18 November", "title": "Wednesday's football: Chelsea v PSG in the Women's Champions League, Copa del Rey ties and a Brasileirão night", "dek": "The internationals are over, so Wednesday is a club night: the Women's Champions League, the Women's League Cup, six Copa del Rey ties and six Brazilian Série A games. Every main game with its chance on the sharp market.", "date": "2026-11-17T19:08:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>After a full week of internationals, Wednesday is quieter, and almost all club football. The headline games in Europe are in the <b>UEFA Women's Champions League</b>, with Chelsea at home to Paris Saint-Germain and Manchester City away in Sweden. England's <b>Women's League Cup</b> has four group games, Spain's <b>Copa del Rey</b> has six ties, and in the evening the <b>Brasileirão</b> has six games, including Flamengo and Palmeiras.</p><p>The chances below come from <b>Pinnacle</b>, the sharpest bookmaker in the market, with its margin removed. They show what the market thinks, not what we think. A chance is not a prediction: a 60% favourite fails to win four times in ten. All times are UK time, with US Eastern time for the evening games.</p>", "<div class=\"update\"><h3>Updated 07:37 Kampala: the morning market</h3><p>BetMeisters' 7.30am price snapshot (Pinnacle, margin removed) has moved the big Women's Champions League games a little. <b>Chelsea</b> are now about <b>76%</b> to beat PSG (72.3% on Tuesday), with PSG about 9% and over 2.5 goals about 66%. <b>Juventus</b> have eased slightly to about <b>74%</b> against OH Leuven (77.0%). <b>Manchester City</b> are about 76.5% in Gothenburg, much as they were.</p><p>Elsewhere the numbers have barely changed: <b>West Ham</b> about 84% against Wolves in the Women's League Cup, <b>Liverpool</b> about 67% at Nottingham Forest, <b>Mallorca</b> about 61% at Alcoyano, <b>Flamengo</b> about 77.5% against Athletico Paranaense and <b>Cruzeiro v Palmeiras</b> still close to a three-way split (35% / 27% / 37%).</p><p>We have not added team news: none of it had been confirmed by a source we trust at the time of writing.</p></div>", "<h2>Women's Champions League</h2>", "<h3>Chelsea v Paris Saint-Germain · 8.00pm (3.00pm ET)</h3><p>The biggest game of the night in England. The market makes <b>Chelsea 72.3%</b>, the draw 17.1% and <b>PSG 10.6%</b>, a clear home favourite. It also expects goals: over 2.5 goals is about <b>61%</b>.</p>", "<h3>Häcken v Manchester City · 5.45pm (12.45pm ET)</h3><p>City travel to Gothenburg as heavy favourites: <b>Manchester City 74.5%</b>, the draw 15.7%, Häcken 9.9%. Over 2.5 goals is about <b>65%</b>.</p>", "<h3>Juventus v OH Leuven · 8.00pm (3.00pm ET)</h3><p>The most one-sided game of the three: <b>Juventus 77.0%</b>, Leuven 8.1%, and over 2.5 goals about 66%, the highest of the European games.</p>", "<h2>Women's League Cup · 7.00pm</h2><p>Four group games kick off at the same time. <b>West Ham v Wolves</b> is the most lopsided: West Ham about 84%. <b>Nottingham Forest v Liverpool</b> has Liverpool about 67% away. <b>Manchester United v Everton</b> has United about 64%, and <b>London City Lionesses v Brighton</b> has London City about 58%. The market expects goals in all of them: over 2.5 is between 61% and 64% in the three games where it is priced. Prices on the women's game are thinner than on the men's, so they can move a lot once team news is out.</p>", "<h2>Copa del Rey</h2><p>Six ties, with the bigger clubs travelling to smaller grounds, which is where cup shocks come from.</p><ul><li><b>Alcoyano v Mallorca</b> (7.00pm): Mallorca 61.4%, Alcoyano 14.7%, draw 23.9%. Under 2.5 goals is slightly favoured at 52.5%.</li><li><b>Badajoz v Tenerife</b> (7.00pm): Tenerife 54.7%, Badajoz 19.6%.</li><li><b>Utebo v Sabadell</b> (6.00pm): Sabadell 58.9%, Utebo 16.3%.</li><li><b>Minera v Cartagena</b> (7.30pm): the closest tie, Cartagena 35.9%, Minera 32.9%, draw 31.2%, and under 2.5 goals about 60%.</li><li><b>Reus v Barakaldo</b> (7.30pm): Barakaldo 46.4%.</li><li><b>Portugalete v Real Unión</b> (6.00pm): Real Unión 39.4%, Portugalete 31.0%.</li></ul><p>Note that cup ties can go to extra time and penalties. Most match-result bets, including these chances, are settled on the 90 minutes only.</p>", "<h2>Brazil: Série A</h2><p>Six games from 10.30pm UK (5.30pm ET).</p><ul><li><b>Flamengo v Athletico Paranaense</b> (11.30pm, 6.30pm ET): the shortest favourite of the night, <b>Flamengo 78.0%</b>, and over 2.5 goals about 64%.</li><li><b>Cruzeiro v Palmeiras</b> (11.30pm, 6.30pm ET): the game of the night in Brazil and close to a coin flip: Palmeiras 38.9%, Cruzeiro 33.2%, the draw 27.8%.</li><li><b>Corinthians v Botafogo</b> (12.30am Thursday, 7.30pm ET): Corinthians 51.7%, Botafogo 23.2%.</li><li><b>Grêmio v Bahia</b> (12.30am, 7.30pm ET): Grêmio 42.3%, Bahia 31.8%.</li><li><b>Remo v Chapecoense</b> (11.00pm): Remo 61.0%.</li><li><b>Coritiba v Santos</b> (10.30pm): Santos 45.7% away.</li></ul>", "<h2>And the rest</h2><p>Earlier in the day, the <b>ASEAN Club Championship</b> has Buriram United about 45.5% away to Cong An Ho Chi Minh City (11.00am). Overnight into Wednesday (3.00am UK, 10.00pm ET on Tuesday), Nicaragua are 79% favourites against Montserrat in the CONCACAF Nations League play-offs.</p>", "<h2>The favourites of the night</h2><p>Put together, Wednesday has an unusual number of very short favourites. On the sharp market, six sides are 70% or more to win in 90 minutes:</p><ul><li><b>West Ham</b> v Wolves (Women's League Cup): about 84%</li><li><b>Flamengo</b> v Athletico Paranaense: 78.0%</li><li><b>Juventus</b> v OH Leuven: 77.0%</li><li><b>Manchester City</b> at Häcken: 74.5%</li><li><b>Chelsea</b> v PSG: 72.3%</li><li><b>Nicaragua</b> v Montserrat: 79.3%</li></ul><p>Short favourites are the most popular legs in accumulators, and it is worth being clear about what they mean. Even if each of those six really is as likely to win as the market says, the chance that <b>all six</b> win is about 0.84 × 0.78 × 0.77 × 0.745 × 0.723 × 0.793, or roughly <b>21%</b>, about one in five. Each leg you add multiplies in another chance of something going wrong, which is why a six-fold of 'bankers' still loses about four times in five.</p>", "<h2>Two kinds of goals market</h2><p>The most striking pattern on Wednesday's card is the gap between the women's games and the Spanish cup ties. In every women's game where it is priced, over 2.5 goals is <b>61% or higher</b>: 61% in Chelsea v PSG and London City v Brighton, 64% in Nottingham Forest v Liverpool, 65% in Häcken v Manchester City and 66% in Juventus v Leuven. In the Copa del Rey it is <b>below 50% in every tie where it is priced</b>: 47% to 48% in Alcoyano v Mallorca, Badajoz v Tenerife and Utebo v Sabadell, 44% in Portugalete v Real Unión and only 40% in Minera v Cartagena.</p><p>The reasons are not mysterious. The women's games involve bigger gaps in quality between the sides, and big gaps produce goals at one end. Early-round cup ties between a lower-division home side and a stronger visitor tend to be cagey for an hour: the home team defends deep, the visitors are often rotated, and a single goal can decide it. When you see a goals line, it is worth asking which kind of game you are looking at before you look at the price.</p>", "<h2>Cup ties: what '90 minutes' means</h2><p>Copa del Rey ties are settled on the night, so a level score after 90 minutes goes to extra time and, if needed, penalties. That matters for betting. Almost every match-result market (home, draw, away) and every goals line is settled on the <b>90 minutes only</b>: a draw bet wins if it is level at 90 minutes, even if one side goes through later. There is usually a separate market, 'to qualify' or 'to progress', that includes extra time and penalties, and its prices are different.</p><p>On Wednesday's ties the market puts the 90-minute draw at between 24% and 31%. In <b>Minera v Cartagena</b> and <b>Portugalete v Real Unión</b> it is close to 30%, so roughly three ties in ten like those go beyond 90 minutes. If you back a favourite 'to win' in a cup tie, you are not backing it to go through; you are backing it to win inside 90 minutes.</p>", "<h2>Brazil in more detail</h2><p>The Brasileirão games run through the US evening, and the market has a clear view of each one. <b>Cruzeiro v Palmeiras</b> is the tightest: no outcome is above 39%, and over 2.5 goals is about 54%. <b>Corinthians v Botafogo</b> is a coin flip on goals (over 2.5 about 51%), while <b>Grêmio v Bahia</b> leans to goals at about 57%. <b>Remo v Chapecoense</b> has the home side 61% with over 2.5 goals at 61%, and <b>Coritiba v Santos</b> has the visitors as favourites at 46%, with over 2.5 goals about 55%.</p><p>Home advantage in Brazil is often said to be strong, with long journeys across a big country and very different conditions from city to city. Whatever its size, the market already includes it, which is why Flamengo are such short favourites at home and why Santos are only 46% away even as favourites.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle prices with the margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at about 4pm UK on Tuesday 17 November. Times are UK, with US Eastern where shown. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan, 17 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]], "updated": "2026-11-18T07:37:00+03:00"}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "previewType": "racing-card", "id": "racing-card-1811", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Racing card · Wednesday 18 November", "title": "Wednesday's racing: Old Park Star's chase debut at Ffos Las, Skelton at Hexham and the London Mile at Kempton", "dek": "Four meetings and 32 races. A 155-rated hurdler goes chasing in a five-runner beginners' chase, Dan Skelton has twelve runners, and Kempton's evening card includes a Class 2 London Mile qualifier. Every meeting, the early market and the angles that matter.", "date": "2026-11-17T19:08:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>Wednesday's racing in Britain and Ireland is spread across four meetings: <b>Hexham</b> and <b>Ffos Las</b> over jumps from lunchtime, <b>Dundalk</b> on the all-weather through the afternoon, and <b>Kempton</b> under lights from 4.25pm. That is 32 races, from 12.33pm to 8.30pm UK time (7.33am to 3.30pm Eastern). The ground is <b>good</b> at both jumps tracks; Kempton's Polytrack is <b>standard to slow</b> and Dundalk's is <b>standard</b>.</p><p>The prices below are <b>early prices</b>, taken on Tuesday evening. At this stage most races are priced by a single bookmaker, so they are a first view, not a market, and they will move a lot by the morning. Only one race, the Ffos Las beginners' chase, is priced widely enough for us to work out fair chances (the market with the bookmakers' margin taken out). Dundalk had not been priced at all. Everything here comes from The Racing API racecards and bookmaker feed.</p>", "<div class=\"update\"><h3>Updated 07:37 Kampala (04:37 UK): non-runners, going and the morning market</h3><p><b>Going:</b> Ffos Las and Hexham are now <b>good, good to soft in places</b> (GoingStick 5.8 at Hexham), a little softer than the good ground declared on Tuesday. Kempton is unchanged, Polytrack standard to slow.</p><p><b>Non-runners:</b> Kalo Athena (Hexham 1.33), My Gift To You (Hexham 2.38), Winston's Warrior (Kempton 8.00) and Navy Guitar (Dundalk 4.30). Hexham's 1.33 is now 10 runners and the 2.38 is 7.</p><p><b>The main moves overnight</b> (median of the bookmakers pricing each horse this morning):</p><ul><li><b>Hexham 2.38:</b> <b>President Scottie</b> has been backed from about 4/1 into about 9/4, and is now joint-favourite with <b>Wal Buck's</b> (also about 9/4, from 15/8).</li><li><b>Ffos Las 2.18:</b> <b>Wannabe</b> is now a clear favourite at about 2/1; <b>Walk Tall</b> is about 3/1. On Tuesday they were joint favourites at around 7/2.</li><li><b>Kempton 6.00:</b> <b>Divine Rhythm</b> has shortened from about 5/2 to about 6/4.</li><li><b>Kempton 7.00:</b> <b>Palladas</b> is now about 6/5 (from 13/8) and <b>Princling</b> about 9/4.</li><li><b>Ffos Las 3.18:</b> <b>For Old Times Sake</b> is now favourite at about 2/1, ahead of Wispit Twob (about 3/1).</li></ul><p><b>Dundalk</b> is now priced. The favourites are around 6/4 or bigger in every race except the 5.07 fillies' maiden, where <b>Rhapsodic</b> is about 4/6, and the 3.30 nursery, where <b>Rock On Johnny</b> is about evens.</p><p>The prices above are from The Racing API's racecards at about 07:37 Kampala. Prices move all morning; check before you bet.</p></div>", "<h2>The race of the day: Ffos Las, 1.48</h2><h3>Beginners' chase, 2m (5 runners, Class 3)</h3><p>The best race on Wednesday on official ratings, and the only one the whole market has priced. Two smart hurdlers make their debuts over fences. <b>Old Park Star</b> (Nicky Henderson, Nico de Boinville) is rated 155 and has won his last four races: his form figures read /1111. <b>Sober Glory</b> (Philip Hobbs and Johnson White, Ben Jones) is rated 153, form 41124. Both have been off since the spring, Old Park Star for 253 days and Sober Glory for 222.</p><p>About 19 bookmakers price the race. With their margins removed, the market makes <b>Old Park Star about 56%</b> (fair price about 1.79) and <b>Sober Glory about 35%</b> (about 2.84). Alan King's <b>Baron Noir</b> (rated 146) is the only other horse with a realistic chance at around 12/1. The best prices from the big UK firms on Tuesday evening, 1.73 and 2.63, were both a little <b>below</b> those fair prices: the market is fair here, not generous.</p><p>Novice chasers are the least predictable favourites in jump racing. A horse rated 155 over hurdles has the engine, but jumping fences at speed is a new skill, and one bad mistake ends the race. The market's 56% means Old Park Star gets beaten more than four times in ten.</p>", "<h2>Hexham: Go North qualifiers on good ground</h2><p>Hexham has six races over jumps and a closing bumper. The trainer to note is <b>Lucinda Russell and Michael Scudamore</b>: nine winners from 42 runners in the last 14 days, with four runners here. <b>Jamie Snowden</b> (7 from 22) has two.</p>", "<h3>12.33 · Novices' hurdle, 2m (9 runners)</h3><p>Snowden's <b>Lord du Gouet</b> (Gavin Sheehan) is the early favourite at around 8/13. His form reads 13-2, and he ran 28 days ago. Sandy Thomson's <b>Eagle Warrior</b>, second last time, is next at around 7/2.</p>", "<h3>1.03 · Beginners' chase, 3m (4 runners, Class 3)</h3><p>A small field but a good one. Russell and Scudamore's <b>Derryhassen Paddy</b> (Derek Fox) is top-rated on 142 and around evens. Snowden's <b>Julius des Pictons</b> (135) has not run for 593 days but won three of his five races before that; he is around 13/8. Skelton's <b>Fishery Lane</b> (131) completes the serious trio.</p>", "<h3>1.33 and 2.08 · Handicap hurdles, 2m4f (two divisions)</h3><p>Mark Walford's <b>De Deli Counter</b> won 12 days ago and heads the first division at around 11/4. In the second, Susan Corbett's <b>Ratemenow</b>, second last time out, is the top-rated runner on 97 and around 2/1. Both divisions are Class 5 races of 10 and 11 runners, where the favourite wins far less often than in a novice race.</p>", "<h3>2.38 · Long-distance handicap chase, 3m7f (8 runners)</h3><p>Almost four miles, and a qualifier for the Go North Red Rum series. Russell and Scudamore's <b>Wal Buck's</b> (form 2144-2) is around 15/8, with Alan King's <b>Betterforeveryone</b> at around 5/2. James Owen's <b>My Gift To You</b> is top-rated on 120. Stamina will decide it.</p>", "<h3>3.08 and 3.38 · Handicap chase and bumper</h3><p>The 3.08 is a 12-runner Class 5 handicap with no clear favourite: <b>Scairp Dubh</b> (around 4/1) and Micky Hammond's <b>Halpha Soleil</b> (around 9/2, second in each of his last two) head the betting. The card closes with a seven-runner bumper. David Pipe's <b>Max Potential</b> and Skelton's newcomer <b>Rio Blue</b> are close in the betting at around 11/10 and 11/8.</p>", "<h2>Ffos Las: the rest of the card</h2><p>Seven races in all. <b>Dan Skelton</b> is again the trainer in form, with 19 winners from 80 runners in 14 days (about 24%). He has twelve runners across Wednesday's cards.</p>", "<h3>12.48 and 1.18 · Maiden hurdles, 2m (two divisions)</h3><p>In the first, David Pipe's <b>No Walkover</b> is around 4/6 on his return from 284 days off; his form reads 2/118. Joe Tizzard's <b>Bob Bishop</b> (off for 574 days) is next. Note that Pipe's yard has had no winners from 11 runners in the last 14 days. In the second, Skelton's <b>Diamond Street</b> (Harry Skelton) is around 10/11, with Philip Hobbs and Johnson White's <b>Quickkick</b>, second 27 days ago, at around 7/2.</p>", "<h3>2.18 · Novices' handicap chase, 2m7f (15 runners)</h3><p>The biggest field of the day at Ffos Las. Tizzard's <b>Walk Tall</b> (Harry Cobden), top-rated on 100, and Tom Lacey's <b>Wannabe</b> are joint favourites at around 7/2. Both unseated their riders last time. Lacey has had five winners from 11 runners in two weeks.</p>", "<h3>2.48 to 3.48 · Maiden hurdle and two handicaps</h3><p>In the 2.48 maiden hurdle, Ben Pauling's <b>Merry Away</b> is around 10/11. In the 3.18 novices' handicap hurdle, Martin Keighley's <b>Wispit Twob</b>, who won last time (form 0P-P81), is around 3/1 under Sean Bowen. Fergal O'Brien's <b>Unleash The Beast</b> heads the 3.48 handicap chase at around 7/4, top-rated on 98 but returning from 270 days off.</p>", "<h2>Kempton: the evening all-weather card</h2><p>Eight races from 4.25pm to 8.30pm on Polytrack, described as standard to slow. Two of them are Class 2.</p>", "<h3>4.25 and 5.00 · Novice stakes for two-year-olds, 6f</h3><p>James Fanshawe's <b>Tredegar Square</b> (rated 77, form 15) and Ed Walker's <b>Brave Country</b> (75, form 23) head the first, around 13/8 and 2/1. In the second, Marco Botti's <b>Angel Footsteps</b>, second on the only previous start, is around 5/6, with the Gosdens' <b>Amandari</b> next.</p>", "<h3>5.30 and 6.00 · Nurseries</h3><p>The 5.30 has five runners; George Boughey's <b>Divine Whisper</b> is top-rated on 86 and around 15/8. In the 6.00 over a mile, William Haggas' <b>Divine Rhythm</b> (form 34) is around 5/2.</p>", "<h3>6.30 · Juddmonte EBF maiden, 1m (14 runners, Class 2)</h3><p>Andrew Balding's <b>Sterling Cooper</b> (third last time) and the Gosdens' <b>Lady Margo</b> (fourth on her debut 21 days ago) head the betting at around 5/2 and 7/2. Maidens like this can produce smart horses for next season, so watch the market for any late support.</p>", "<h3>7.00 · London Mile Series qualifier, 1m handicap (14 runners, Class 2)</h3><p>The best handicap of the evening. Charlie Appleby's <b>Palladas</b> (rated 95, form 114) is favourite at around 13/8. Haggas' <b>Princling</b> (90) is next at around 7/2. The top-rated runner is <b>Ebt's Guard</b> on 104, who has been placed in his last three races without winning, at around 6/1. In a 14-runner handicap the early favourite at 13/8 looks short, and prices will spread out as more firms price it.</p>", "<h3>7.30 to 8.30 · The late handicaps</h3><p>In the 7.30 London Stayers' qualifier over two miles, Roger Varian's <b>Zachary Hickes</b>, who won last time (form 641231), is around 5/4. The 8.00 over seven furlongs is wide open, with Fanshawe's <b>Enacting</b> heading a 14-runner field at around 7/2. Haggas' <b>Crown Of Crowns</b> (form 41) is around 2/1 in the 8.30. <b>Snooker McCrew</b> is the one to watch for form: he has won four in a row.</p>", "<h2>Dundalk</h2><p>Dundalk's eight races run from 2.00pm to 5.40pm, five of them with 16 or 17 runners. Bookmakers had not priced the card on Tuesday evening. On ratings, the stand-out runners include <b>Golddream</b> (75) in the 4.30 handicap and <b>Stooked</b> (73) in the 4.00 maiden. Big fields at Dundalk are among the hardest betting races of the week; each-way terms matter more than the win price.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Early prices taken at about 7pm on Tuesday 17 November (Kampala), 4pm UK. Fair chances for the Ffos Las 1.48 come from about 19 bookmakers with the margin removed. Trainer form is winners and runners in the 14 days to Tuesday. All times are UK. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133 (UK), 1-800-GAMBLER (US).</p>"], "sources": [["The Racing API: Ffos Las, Hexham, Kempton and Dundalk racecards, ratings, trainer form and early bookmaker prices for 18 Nov 2026", "https://www.theracingapi.com"], ["The Racing API: racecards for Wednesday 18 November (morning update)", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]], "updated": "2026-11-18T07:37:00+03:00"}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football", "Basketball"], "id": "asian-handicaps-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Betting basics", "title": "Asian handicaps explained: half, whole and quarter lines, and what each one pays", "dek": "Asian handicaps take the draw out of football betting. Here is how every line works, from 0 to −1.25, with the exact payout for each result and how to turn a handicap price into a chance.", "date": "2026-11-17T12:22:00+03:00", "read": 7, "sources": [["Sports Mole: Asian handicap betting guide", "https://www.sportsmole.co.uk/football/features/asian-handicap-betting-guide_386256.html"], ["SoccerNews: Asian handicap betting explained", "https://www.soccernews.com/asian-handicap-betting/401985/"]], "body": ["<p>They are Asian handicaps, and they are the most-traded football market in the world. They look confusing, but there are only three kinds of line, and once you know which kind you are looking at, you can work out exactly what any result pays.</p>", "<h3>Kind 1: half-goal lines (−0.5, +0.5, −1.5, +1.5)</h3><p>These are the simplest, because a half goal can never be cancelled out, so there is no draw and no refund. The bet either wins in full or loses in full.</p><ul><li><b>−0.5:</b> your team must win. It is the same bet as a straight win, often at a slightly different price.</li><li><b>+0.5:</b> your team must not lose. A win or a draw wins the bet: the same as 'double chance'.</li><li><b>−1.5:</b> your team must win by two goals or more.</li><li><b>+1.5:</b> your team can lose by one goal and the bet still wins.</li></ul>", "<h3>Kind 3: quarter lines (±0.25, ±0.75, ±1.25)</h3><p>Quarter lines are the ones that confuse people, but there is a simple trick: <b>a quarter line is two bets in one</b>. Your stake is split in half, and each half goes on the two nearest lines either side.</p><ul><li><b>−0.25</b> = half on 0 (draw no bet) + half on −0.5 (to win).</li><li><b>+0.25</b> = half on 0 + half on +0.5 (win or draw).</li><li><b>−0.75</b> = half on −0.5 + half on −1.</li><li><b>+0.75</b> = half on +0.5 + half on +1.</li><li><b>−1.25</b> = half on −1 + half on −1.5.</li><li><b>+1.25</b> = half on +1 + half on +1.5.</li></ul><p>Settle each half separately and add them up. That is where the 'half win' and 'half loss' results come from.</p>", "<h3>The full payout table, for £10 at 2.00</h3><p>Here is what £10 at evens (2.00) returns on each quarter line, by result. 'Margin' is your team's goals minus the opponent's.</p><ul><li><b>−0.25</b>: win = £20 back. Draw = half loses, half returned: <b>£5 back</b>. Defeat = £0.</li><li><b>+0.25</b>: win = £20. Draw = half wins, half returned: <b>£15 back</b>. Defeat = £0.</li><li><b>−0.75</b>: win by 2+ = £20. Win by exactly 1 = half wins, half returned: <b>£15</b>. Draw or defeat = £0.</li><li><b>+0.75</b>: win or draw = £20. Lose by exactly 1 = half loses, half returned: <b>£5</b>. Lose by 2+ = £0.</li><li><b>−1.25</b>: win by 2+ = £20. Win by exactly 1 = half returned, half loses: <b>£5</b>. Draw or defeat = £0.</li><li><b>+1.25</b>: win, draw = £20. Lose by exactly 1 = half wins, half returned: <b>£15</b>. Lose by 2+ = £0.</li></ul><p>At other prices the maths is the same: a <b>half win</b> returns half your stake at the full price plus the other half back; a <b>half loss</b> returns half your stake and loses the rest.</p>", "<h3>Asian handicap v European handicap</h3><p>Some bookmakers also offer a <b>European (three-way) handicap</b>, written like 'Iceland −1 (three-way)'. It keeps the draw as a separate outcome: with Iceland −1, a one-goal Iceland win is the 'handicap draw', and a bet on Iceland loses. No stake comes back. Always check which one you are betting: the same number can mean a push in one market and a loss in the other.</p>", "<h3>Handicaps in basketball and handball</h3><p>The same idea works in points. A basketball line such as <b>Zalgiris −9.5</b> means Zalgiris must win by 10 or more; there is no draw because of the half point. Whole lines push in the usual way: <b>Botevgrad +2</b> returns the stake if they lose by exactly two. In basketball, <b>overtime counts</b> for handicaps and totals at most UK bookmakers; in handball, check the market, because some are settled at the end of normal time.</p>", "<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li><b>Treating −0.75 like −1.</b> On −0.75, a one-goal win still pays half at the full price. On −1, it is only a refund.</li><li><b>Forgetting extra time does not count.</b> A cup tie that is 1-1 after 90 minutes is a draw for handicap purposes, whatever happens after.</li><li><b>Quarter lines in accas.</b> A half-won leg is not a winner: most bookmakers count it at a reduced price, (1 + price) ÷ 2, so the acca pays less than you expect. A half-lost leg keeps the acca alive at half the price, 0.5. Check your bookmaker's rules.</li><li><b>Mixing up the sides.</b> −1.25 for one team is the same bet as +1.25 for the other; make sure the team name and sign match what you meant.</li></ul>", "<h3>Which line should you choose?</h3><p>The same match is usually offered on several lines at once, each at a different price. Moving from −0.5 to −0.75 to −1 makes your bet harder to win, so the price rises; moving the other way makes it easier and the price falls. None is better in itself: the question is always whether the price is above the fair chance for <b>that</b> line.</p><p>As a rule of thumb, the line closest to 50/50 is where the market is most accurate, because that is where most of the money goes. The further you move from it, the more the bookmaker tends to widen its margin. If you like a favourite but fear a one-goal win, a quarter line such as −0.75 gives you half the payout in that case instead of nothing; if you like an underdog but fear a narrow defeat, +0.75 or +1 cushions it.</p><p>One last tip: compare the Asian handicap price with the matching three-way price. A team at −0.5 should pay almost exactly the same as the team to win; if one is clearly bigger, that is the better bet for the same outcome.</p><h3>The quick reference</h3><ul><li><b>.5 lines</b> (−0.5, +1.5): win or lose, nothing in between.</li><li><b>Whole lines</b> (0, −1, +2): land on the number and your stake comes back.</li><li><b>Quarter lines</b> (.25, .75): your stake is split across the two lines either side, so one result per line can pay half.</li></ul>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Settlement rules vary slightly between bookmakers: always read the rules page for the market before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"]}, {"topics": ["Licensing and regulation", "Enforcement"], "sports": [], "id": "ukgc-illegal-gambling-money-1711", "section": "news", "kicker": "News · UK regulator", "title": "The UK regulator is going after the money behind illegal betting sites: what it means for you", "dek": "7.8 million web addresses referred, £40 million in suspected criminal funds flagged and 1,000+ cease and desist notices. The Gambling Commission is now targeting payments, adverts and suppliers, and its new survey shows who uses illegal sites.", "date": "2026-11-17T12:22:00+03:00", "read": 3, "sources": [["UK Gambling Commission: Commission ramps up action against illegal gambling profits and assets (9 Nov 2026)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/commission-ramps-up-action-against-illegal-gambling-profits-and-assets"], ["UK Gambling Commission: New survey data to help tackle illegal gambling and protect consumers (5 Nov 2026)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/new-survey-data-to-help-tackle-illegal-gambling-and-protect-consumers"], ["UK Gambling Commission: latest news", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/latest"], ["BetMeisters.news: How to check a gambling licence", "article.html#how-to-check-gambling-licence"]], "body": ["<p>Britain's Gambling Commission says its fight against illegal gambling websites is moving from the sites themselves to the <b>money, assets and business models</b> behind them. The regulator announced the change on 9 November, at the start of the first international Illegal Gambling Awareness Week, and backed it with a set of numbers that show how fast the problem, and the response, has grown.</p>", "<h3>What the Commission is doing</h3><p>Taking down a website on its own does little: an illegal operator can reappear on a new address within hours. So the Commission says it will now focus on what keeps those operators running:</p><ul><li><b>Payments:</b> disrupting the ways illegal sites take money from customers.</li><li><b>Advertising:</b> making it harder for them to reach British players.</li><li><b>Suppliers:</b> cutting off access to the games and sportsbooks they rent from other companies.</li><li><b>Criminal funds:</b> referring suspected proceeds to partner agencies so they can be frozen, and working with police to seize cash from illegal land-based gambling.</li></ul><p>Sue Young, the Commission's Executive Director of Operations, put it bluntly: \"If you run an illegal gambling operation, we are coming after you, your business and your money.\"</p>", "<h3>The numbers</h3><p>In the 2026/27 financial year so far, the Commission says it has:</p><ul><li>issued <b>more than 1,000</b> cease and desist notices to illegal operators;</li><li>referred <b>7.8 million</b> web addresses to search engines, and had <b>3 million</b> removed from search results;</li><li>referred <b>2,200</b> websites for disruption action;</li><li>referred <b>more than £40 million</b> in suspected criminal funds to partner agencies since January.</li></ul><p>The jump in the second quarter is striking: URL referrals rose from <b>147,721</b> in April to June to <b>7.7 million</b> in July to September, an increase of more than 5,000%. That says more about the Commission's new tools than about a sudden explosion of sites, but it shows the scale of what is out there.</p>", "<h3>Who uses illegal sites: the new survey data</h3><p>Four days earlier, on 5 November, the Commission published the first estimates from new questions in its Gambling Survey for Great Britain, designed to show whether people are likely to be using an illegal site, whether they realise it or not.</p><ul><li>Across the whole adult population, <b>94.4%</b> showed no sign of using an illegal gambling website.</li><li><b>0.6%</b> had used one in the past four weeks, and a further <b>0.4%</b> in the past year.</li><li>Among people who gamble online, those figures rise to <b>2.5%</b> in the past four weeks and a further <b>1.5%</b> in the past year.</li><li>Use was higher among men than women, and in the past year most common among <b>18 to 24-year-olds</b>.</li></ul><p>Sue Young's warning was that \"those consumers who use illegal operators are more likely to experience harm\". The Commission will track the figures over time on an updated illegal gambling data dashboard.</p>", "<h3>Why it matters if you bet</h3><p>An unlicensed site is not just a legal technicality. If it refuses to pay you, there is no regulator to complain to and no independent dispute service. It does not have to check your age, offer deposit limits or join GAMSTOP, and your money is not protected if it disappears. Illegal sites often market themselves on exactly the things UK rules restrict: huge bonuses, no checks and no limits.</p><p>The quickest protection is to check the licence before you sign up. Every site licensed to take bets from people in Great Britain is listed on the Gambling Commission's public register, with its account number and the exact web addresses it is allowed to use. Our <a href=\"article.html#how-to-check-gambling-licence\">step-by-step guide to checking a licence</a> shows how, and every BetMeisters.news review starts with that check.</p>", "<h3>What happens next</h3><p>The Commission says it will work more closely with police, technology companies and the financial sector on intelligence sharing and early warnings. With the money now the target, the next visible results are likely to be payment blocks and asset freezes rather than website take-downs. We will report them as they are published.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">Need help? GambleAware's helpline is free and open 24 hours: 0808 8020 133 (England and Scotland) or 0808 2819 265 (Wales). Complaints about a gambling business go to the Gambling Commission through its contact page. 18+.</p>"]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Tennis", "Basketball", "Handball", "Ice hockey"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "other-sports-tuesday-1711", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Other sports · Tue 17 November", "title": "Tuesday's other sports: Fils v Jodar in Turin, a full Euroleague night and the NBA, NHL and college basketball overnight", "dek": "ATP Finals singles and doubles, ten Euroleague games, the EHF European League and a busy US night. The sharp market's numbers for each, plus running in these sports.", "date": "2026-11-17T12:22:00+03:00", "read": 3, "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan at 12:02 EAT (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"], ["Land of Basketball: NBA games on 17 November 2026", "https://www.landofbasketball.com/nba_daily_summary/20261117.htm"]], "body": ["<p>A full afternoon and evening of tennis and European basketball, then the US night. All chances below are from the sharp Pinnacle market with the bookmaker margin removed, taken at 12:02 Kampala time, so they will move. Times are Kampala (EAT); UK is three hours behind.</p>", "<h2>NBA overnight</h2><ul><li><b>Raptors v Knicks (03:30):</b> Knicks 57.8%, −3, total 217.</li><li><b>Spurs v Pistons (04:00):</b> Spurs 78.4%, −8.5, total 222.</li><li><b>Clippers v Jazz (06:30):</b> Clippers 69.2%, −6, total 226.</li><li><b>Lakers v Hornets (07:00):</b> Lakers 74.3%, −8, total 218.5.</li></ul><p>Portland v Minnesota is also on the schedule; it had no sharp line in our scan.</p>", "<h2>NHL and college basketball</h2><p>The pick of the NHL night is <b>Florida Panthers v Edmonton Oilers (03:00)</b>, a near coin flip at 53% v 47%. Tampa Bay are 59.5% against Washington and Minnesota 64.7% against Nashville. In college basketball, <b>Duke v Illinois (03:00)</b> has Duke at 75.5% and −7.5, and <b>Tennessee v Michigan State (05:00)</b> has Michigan State 58.6%. Early-season college schedules also bring huge spreads: Miami are −42 against Rider and Vanderbilt −43.5 against Eastern Illinois.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle prices with the margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at 12:02 Kampala time on Tuesday 17 November, and will move. Times are Kampala (EAT). No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"]}, {"topics": ["Bonuses, rewards and VIP", "Sports betting"], "sports": [], "id": "free-bets-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Bonuses and promotions", "title": "Free bets explained: stake not returned, expiry and what a free bet is really worth", "dek": "A £10 free bet is not worth £10. Here is how free bets work in the UK, what the terms mean, the rules bookmakers have to follow, and the simple maths that shows what one is really worth.", "date": "2026-11-17T07:38:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>\"Bet £10, get £30 in free bets.\" It is the most common offer in British betting, and one of the most misunderstood. A free bet is a real thing with real value, but it is almost never worth its face value, and the terms attached to it decide how much you actually get. This guide explains how free bets work, the UK rules that protect you, and how to work out what a free bet is really worth.</p><p>One thing first: a free bet is still gambling. Only use offers on bets you were going to make anyway, and only with money you can afford to lose. 18+.</p>", "<h3>What a free bet is</h3><p>A free bet is a token that lets you place a bet without staking your own money. If the bet wins, you receive the winnings. If it loses, you lose nothing of your own. That sounds like a straight gift, but there is one big catch, and it is in almost every UK free bet: <b>the stake is not returned</b>.</p>", "<h3>Stake not returned (SNR): the term that matters most</h3><p>With a normal £10 bet at 3.00, a win returns £30: £20 profit plus your £10 stake back. With a £10 free bet at 3.00, a win usually returns <b>£20</b>, because the £10 free bet stake is kept by the bookmaker. You get the profit only.</p><p>This is why the price you choose matters so much more with a free bet than with cash. At short odds, SNR wipes out most of the value:</p><table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>Decimal odds</th><th>Cash bet: return on a £10 win</th><th>Free bet (SNR): return on a £10 win</th><th>Share of the cash return you keep</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1.50</td><td>£15</td><td>£5</td><td>33%</td></tr><tr><td>2.00</td><td>£20</td><td>£10</td><td>50%</td></tr><tr><td>4.00</td><td>£40</td><td>£30</td><td>75%</td></tr><tr><td>10.00</td><td>£100</td><td>£90</td><td>90%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A few offers do return the stake. They are rare, and the offer terms will say so clearly. If the terms do not mention the stake, assume it is not returned.</p>", "<h3>What a free bet is really worth</h3><p>The fair way to value any bet is its <b>expected value</b>: what it pays on average if you placed it many times. For a free bet with the stake not returned, the formula is simple:</p><p><b>Value of a free bet = free bet amount × chance of winning × (decimal odds − 1)</b></p><p>If the odds were perfectly fair (no bookmaker margin), the chance of winning would be exactly 1 ÷ odds, and the formula becomes <b>free bet amount × (1 − 1 ÷ odds)</b>. At fair odds of 2.00 a £10 free bet is worth £5; at fair odds of 10.00 it is worth £9. The longer the odds, the closer the free bet gets to its face value, but it never reaches it.</p><p>Real odds include a margin. In BetMeisters' price snapshot on Tuesday morning, the average margin on main win markets at four big UK bookmakers was between about 7.7% and 8.8%, across more than 400 selections. Taking a margin of 8%, the value of a £10 free bet at different prices works out like this:</p><table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>Decimal odds</th><th>Chance after the 8% margin</th><th>Value of a £10 free bet (SNR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1.50</td><td>61%</td><td>£3.07</td></tr><tr><td>2.00</td><td>46%</td><td>£4.60</td></tr><tr><td>4.00</td><td>23%</td><td>£6.90</td></tr><tr><td>10.00</td><td>9.2%</td><td>£8.28</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So a £10 free bet is worth roughly £3 to £8, depending on how you use it. Two practical lessons follow. First, using a free bet on a short-priced favourite is the worst way to use it: you are very likely to win, but you win very little. Second, longer odds get more of the face value, but they also mean you will usually lose. Over a single bet, the outcome is down to luck either way. The maths only tells you the average.</p>", "<h3>The qualifying bet: what the offer costs you</h3><p>Most sign-up offers ask you to place a <b>qualifying bet</b> with your own money first: \"bet £10, get £30\". That qualifying bet carries the normal bookmaker margin. At odds of 2.00 with an 8% margin, a £10 qualifying bet loses about 80p on average. Weigh that against the value of the free bets you receive, and remember that the qualifying bet usually has its own minimum odds.</p><p>The bigger risk is not the 80p. It is that an offer gets you to open an account and bet when you would not otherwise have done. If a sign-up offer changes what you would have done, it is costing you more than the maths shows.</p>", "<h3>The terms to check before you claim</h3><p>The UK advertising rules say the significant conditions of an offer must be clear in the advert itself or one click away, not buried in small print. In practice, look for these:</p><ul><li><b>Eligibility:</b> new customers only, one per person or household, UK and Ireland only, 18+.</li><li><b>Qualifying bet:</b> the minimum stake, the minimum odds (often 1/2 or evens), and which markets count. Some offers exclude each-way bets, bet builders or cashed-out bets.</li><li><b>Payment method:</b> some offers exclude deposits made with certain e-wallets. This has caught out many people, and the advertising regulator has ruled against offers that hid it.</li><li><b>Expiry:</b> free bets usually expire within a set number of days. An unused free bet is worth nothing.</li><li><b>Minimum odds and market limits on the free bet itself:</b> many free bets must be used at odds of evens or bigger, or only on certain sports.</li><li><b>How it is split:</b> \"£30 in free bets\" is often three £10 tokens, sometimes for different sports. You may not be able to combine them.</li><li><b>Stake returned or not:</b> assume not, unless the terms say otherwise.</li><li><b>Wagering on winnings:</b> sports free bet winnings are normally paid as cash you can withdraw. If an offer adds wagering requirements to the winnings, see the 10x cap below.</li></ul>", "<h3>The UK rules that protect you</h3><p>Several rules from the UK regulators apply directly to free bets.</p><p><b>\"Free\" must mean free.</b> The Committee of Advertising Practice's guidance says an offer should not be called \"free\" if the customer must risk their own money to qualify, and \"risk-free\" should not be used unless the customer genuinely cannot lose their own money. That is why adverts say \"bet £10, get £30 in free bets\" rather than \"get £30 free\".</p><p><b>Wagering is capped at 10 times.</b> Since <b>19 January 2026</b>, the Gambling Commission caps wagering requirements on gambling promotions at ten. A bonus can no longer require you to re-stake it more than 10 times before you can withdraw. The Commission's own example: a £10 bonus with a 50x requirement used to mean £500 of play. Our <a href=\"article.html#uk-bonus-rules-10x-cap\">guide to the 10x cap</a> explains it in full.</p><p><b>No mixed-product offers.</b> From the same date, operators cannot offer a promotion that requires you to gamble on two or more types of product, such as \"bet on the football and get free spins on slots\". The Commission called these potentially harmful.</p><p><b>Restrictions cannot take away an offer you have started.</b> The Gambling Commission's guidance on free bets and account restrictions says that if you have already started placing qualifying bets for a free bet offer, the bookmaker must not then enforce an account restriction (for example, cutting your maximum stake) in a way that stops you getting the free bets or their full value. A restriction notice must also say that it does not affect promotions you have already qualified for.</p>", "<h3>Free bets for existing customers</h3><p>Free bets are not only for new accounts. Existing customers often see \"bet £20 on the weekend football, get a £5 free bet\" offers, acca insurance paid as a free bet, or free bets for a losing streak. The same maths applies: value each one with the formula above, check the minimum odds and expiry, and ask whether the offer is changing what you bet on. A reward that pushes you to bet more often, or on markets you would not normally touch, is working for the bookmaker, not for you.</p>", "<h3>Common mistakes with free bets</h3><ul><li><b>Treating the free bet as cash.</b> A £10 free bet is worth roughly £3 to £8, not £10.</li><li><b>Using it on a short favourite.</b> At 1.50 a £10 free bet is worth about £3. The same free bet at 4.00 is worth more than twice as much.</li><li><b>Missing the expiry date.</b> An expired free bet is worth nothing.</li><li><b>Ignoring the qualifying odds.</b> A qualifying bet below the minimum odds does not count, and you will not get the free bets.</li><li><b>Chasing offers across many sites.</b> Opening account after account for sign-up offers can quickly turn into betting far more than you meant to.</li></ul>", "<h3>FAQs</h3><p><b>Is a free bet really free?</b> Using it costs you nothing, but getting it usually needs a qualifying bet with your own money, and its value is below its face value because the stake is not returned.</p><p><b>Can I withdraw a free bet?</b> No. You can only bet with it. Winnings from a sports free bet are normally paid as withdrawable cash, minus the stake.</p><p><b>What odds should I use a free bet at?</b> There is no right answer for one bet, but on average a free bet keeps more of its value at bigger odds. On short odds most of the value is lost to stake-not-returned.</p><p><b>Does the 10x wagering cap apply to sports free bets?</b> The Gambling Commission's announcement applies the cap to promotions that come with wagering requirements. Most sports free bets have no wagering requirement on winnings at all; if one does, it cannot be more than 10x.</p><p class=\"note\">18+. BetMeisters.news has no affiliate links and is not paid for any offer mentioned. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["UK Gambling Commission: Gambling promotions to be safer and simpler (10x wagering cap and mixed-product ban, from 19 January 2026)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/gambling-promotions-to-be-safer-and-simpler"], ["UK Gambling Commission: Free bets and account restrictions in relation to sports betting", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/authorities/codes-of-practice/guide/page/free-bets-and-account-restrictions-in-relation-to-sports-betting"], ["ASA / CAP: Gambling, betting and gaming: free bets and bonuses", "https://www.asa.org.uk/advice-online/gambling-betting-and-gaming-free-bets-and-bonuses.html"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Basketball", "NFL", "Ice hockey"], "previewType": "other-sports", "id": "us-sports-tonight-1611", "section": "previews", "kicker": "US sports tonight · NBA, NFL, NHL, college basketball", "title": "US sports overnight: Ravens v Chargers on Monday Night Football, eight NBA games and a big college slate", "dek": "Monday night in the US runs through the Kampala small hours. The sharp market's spreads and totals for the NFL, NBA, NHL and college basketball, plus the two running.", "date": "2026-11-16T22:34:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>The US sports night starts as Kampala goes to bed. College basketball tips off from 2am, the NBA from 3am, the NHL from 3.30am, and <b>Monday Night Football</b> kicks off at 4.15am. Below is what the sharpest market makes of the main games, with the bookmaker margin removed. US betting works mostly on <b>spreads</b> (a points handicap) and <b>totals</b> (over or under a number of points), so that is what we show. A spread priced at about 50% on each side means the market thinks the line is set right.</p>", "<h2>NFL · Monday Night Football</h2>", "<h3>Baltimore Ravens v Los Angeles Chargers · 04:15</h3><p>The Ravens are <b>4.5-point favourites</b> at home, and the market is split almost exactly down the middle on that line: 50.2% for Baltimore to cover, 49.8% for the Chargers. The total is low at <b>39 points</b>, with the over at 50.7%. A total that low says the market expects defences to have the upper hand. When a spread sits on 4.5, the key numbers in American football (3 and 7, the value of a field goal and a converted touchdown) matter: a Ravens win by exactly 4 or by 3 loses on the spread, and a win by 5 or more covers.</p>", "<h2>NHL</h2><p><b>New York Rangers v Montreal Canadiens (03:30):</b> Montreal are favourites away from home. The puck line (a 1.5-goal handicap) has the Rangers +1.5 at about 71.5%, so the market makes a Montreal win by two goals or more about 28.5%. Over 5.5 goals is 56%. <b>Utah Mammoth v Vancouver Canucks (05:30):</b> Utah −1.5 is 55.3%, a big number for a puck line, and the total is set at 6.5.</p>", "<h2>College basketball</h2><p>College basketball's opening weeks bring huge spreads, because big programmes schedule home games against much smaller schools. Tonight Maryland are −34.5 against Alcorn State, Minnesota −34 against Western Illinois, Ohio State −33 against Prairie View A&amp;M, Texas A&amp;M −33 against Southeastern Louisiana and UCLA −31.5 against Long Beach State. Even at those margins the market prices each spread close to 50/50.</p><p>The closer games: <b>Missouri State v Abilene Christian</b> (−5.5, exactly 50/50), <b>Tulsa v Western Kentucky</b> (−6.5) and <b>Drake v Tarleton State</b> (−9). Kent State v Eastern Kentucky has the highest college total at 162.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle prices with the margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at about 10pm Kampala time on Monday 16 November, and will move. Times are Kampala (EAT). No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan, 16 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "event", "id": "preview-liverpool-man-utd-gw11", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Advance preview · Premier League Gameweek 11", "title": "Liverpool v Manchester United: fourth against eighteenth, and what the market makes of the biggest game of Gameweek 11", "dek": "Sunday 22 November, Anfield, 16:30 UK. Liverpool are 4th, United in the relegation zone with one league win all season, and Mauricio Pochettino takes charge of United for the first time. The early sharp market makes Liverpool 54%, and it expects goals. A full advance preview, with the numbers, the rest of the weekend and what to watch before kick-off.", "date": "2026-11-16T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>The Premier League returns from the international break on Saturday 21 November, and the biggest fixture of <b>Gameweek 11</b> comes on Sunday afternoon: <b>Liverpool v Manchester United</b> at Anfield, kick-off 16:30 UK (19:30 in Kampala). It is one of English football's great rivalries. This season it is also a meeting of two clubs heading in very different directions.</p><p>This is an <b>advance preview</b>, written six days before kick-off. Team news, injuries and prices will change after the international games this week. We will update the numbers in our weekend football preview on Thursday and in the daily preview on the day. Everything below is sourced, and the prices are the sharp market's early view.</p>", "<h2>Where the two clubs stand</h2><p>After 10 games, <b>Liverpool are fourth on 18 points</b>, nine behind leaders Arsenal (27). Brighton are second on 22 and Manchester City third on 21. Liverpool's last two league games show both sides of their season: a 1-0 home defeat by Arsenal at Anfield on 1 November, then an away win at Crystal Palace a week later that lifted them back into the top four.</p><p><b>Manchester United are 18th on 8 points</b>, inside the relegation zone, one point behind Coventry City. Their only league win of the season was 5-2 against Ipswich on 29 August. Their last game before the break was a 4-4 draw at home to Aston Villa on 8 November. United were 4-2 down and scored twice in stoppage time, through Joshua Zirkzee and a 96th-minute Patrick Dorgu header. Before that, they lost 3-0 at home to Bournemouth, 2-0 to Chelsea, and 4-1 at Como in the Champions League. Reuters reported that some supporters unfurled banners against the ownership during the Villa game, and that manager <b>Michael Carrick</b> would not discuss his own future.</p><p><b>Since then, United have changed manager.</b> On Thursday 12 November the club dismissed Carrick, 15 games into the two-year contract he signed in May, and confirmed <b>Mauricio Pochettino</b> as head coach on a two-and-a-half-year deal. Pochettino left his job as head coach of the United States men's national team to take it. Sunday at Anfield will be his first game in charge, with only the international-break week to work with the players who are not away with their countries.</p>", "<h2>What the market says</h2><p>On Monday evening, Pinnacle's prices (margin removed) gave:</p><ul><li><b>Liverpool win: 54.2%</b> (fair price about 1.85)</li><li><b>Draw: 22.8%</b> (about 4.39)</li><li><b>Manchester United win: 23.0%</b> (about 4.35)</li></ul><p>That is a clear but not overwhelming home favourite. For comparison, the market makes Manchester City 68.6% against Fulham and Tottenham 62.1% against Ipswich in the same gameweek. The market is still giving United almost a one-in-four chance of winning. That reflects the gap in quality between United's squad and their league position, and the fact that derbies are less predictable than league tables suggest.</p><p>On the Asian handicap, the main line is <b>Liverpool −0.75</b>, priced close to even money (about 49% for Liverpool to cover). That line is a half-and-half bet: half the stake on Liverpool −0.5 and half on −1. A one-goal Liverpool win pays half, a two-goal win pays in full, and a draw or United win loses.</p>", "<h3>Goals</h3><p>The goals market is the most interesting part. Over 2.5 goals is about <b>64.7%</b>, the highest of any Gameweek 11 game, just ahead of Manchester City v Fulham (64.3%). Over 3.5 goals is about <b>43.7%</b>, the highest of the round. That reflects both teams' recent games. Liverpool v Arsenal was tight, but United have just conceded four at home to Villa and three at home to Bournemouth, and scored four themselves in the Villa game. A new head coach with little training time adds uncertainty in both directions.</p><p>For anyone thinking about 'both teams to score', United's defensive record and their late goals against Villa point in the same direction: the market expects goals at both ends.</p>", "<h2>The other side of the weekend</h2><p>Gameweek 11 has ten games across Saturday, Sunday and Monday. The early sharp market makes these the key ones:</p><ul><li><b>Newcastle v Arsenal</b> (Sat 17:30 UK): the leaders travel as clear favourites, <b>Arsenal 61.0%</b>, Newcastle 16.7%. Arsenal have won nine of ten league games. Newcastle are 12th. We will preview this one in full on Tuesday.</li><li><b>Manchester City v Fulham</b> (Sat 12:30): City 68.6%, over 2.5 goals 64.3%.</li><li><b>Hull v Brighton</b> (Sun 14:00): second-placed Brighton 63.8% away at Hull.</li><li><b>Chelsea v Leeds</b> (Sat 15:00): Chelsea 50.7%. Leeds are ninth and level on points with eighth-placed Brentford.</li><li><b>Coventry v Crystal Palace</b> (Sat 15:00): a relegation six-pointer. Palace are bottom on 7 points and Coventry 17th on 9. The market makes it Coventry 39.5%, Palace 33.8%.</li><li><b>Brentford v Everton</b> (Mon 20:00): Brentford 46.1%, Everton 27.9%.</li></ul><p>The other three games are Aston Villa v Sunderland, Bournemouth v Nottingham Forest and Tottenham v Ipswich.</p>", "<h2>What to watch before Sunday</h2><ul><li><b>International fitness.</b> Players from both squads are away with their countries this week. Injuries picked up this week often move prices on Thursday and Friday. We do not report team news until it is confirmed by the clubs or reputable outlets.</li><li><b>The managers' press conferences</b> on Friday are the first reliable guide to fitness, and Pochettino's first pre-match press conference may give the first clues to how he will set United up.</li><li><b>Price movement.</b> If Liverpool shorten below about 1.80 before team news, the market is reacting to information. If they drift, the same applies the other way.</li><li><b>The goals line.</b> If the 2.5 line moves to 3.0 (the market's way of saying 'even more goals expected'), the over 2.5 price will shorten.</li></ul>", "<h2>The short version</h2><p>Liverpool are clear favourites at about 54%. The draw and a United win are almost equally likely at about 23% each. The market expects goals: nearly two chances in three of three or more. United's league position makes them look weaker than the market thinks they are, and that gap is what makes this fixture worth watching closely as the week goes on.</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle's prices with the margin removed, taken at about 7.15pm Kampala time on Monday 16 November, six days before kick-off. Table and results are as reported after Gameweek 10. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["Premier League table after 10 games (WithinNigeria, 9 Nov 2026)", "https://www.withinnigeria.com/2026/11/09/premier-league-table-after-10-games-arsenal-top-on-27-points-brighton-second-as-man-united-fall-into-the-relegation-zone/"], ["Reuters via TimesLIVE: Pressure still on Man United boss Carrick despite dramatic Villa draw (9 Nov 2026)", "https://www.timeslive.co.za/sport/soccer/2026-11-09-pressure-still-on-man-united-boss-carrick-despite-dramatic-villa-draw/"], ["ESPN: Man United confirm Mauricio Pochettino as Michael Carrick replacement (13 Nov 2026)", "https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/50469660/man-united-mauricio-pochettino-manager-michael-carrick"], ["Premier League 2026-27 Matchday 11 fixtures (FIFPlay)", "https://www.fifplay.com/premier-league-2026-2027-matchday-11/"], ["Pinnacle prices, 16 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "daily-football", "updated": "2026-11-17T07:35:00+03:00", "id": "football-tuesday-1711", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Daily football · Tuesday 17 November", "title": "Tuesday's football: Denmark v Norway for a quarter-final, Wales v Portugal and a full AFCON qualifying night", "dek": "The last Nations League group games, 18 Africa Cup of Nations qualifiers including Botswana v Uganda, Japan v Paraguay and Asian friendlies. Every big game with its chance on the sharp market, plus running.", "date": "2026-11-16T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 7, "body": ["<p>Tuesday is the busiest international night of the month. In Europe the <b>Nations League</b> group stage ends, with a quarter-final place at stake in Copenhagen. In Africa, there are <b>Africa Cup of Nations qualifiers</b> all day, from early afternoon to late evening. In Asia, Japan play Paraguay and Australia go to Jordan.</p><p>The chances below come from <b>Pinnacle</b>, the sharpest bookmaker in the market, with its margin removed. They show what the market thinks, not what we think, and they are a good guide to what is likely. A chance is not a prediction: a 55% favourite fails to win almost half the time. All times are Kampala time (EAT), with UK time in brackets.</p>", "<p class=\"note\"><b>Updated 07:35 Kampala time, Tuesday.</b> Prices re-checked at Pinnacle this morning. Most chances moved by a point or less. The bigger moves: Botswana up from 34.8% to 37.4% against Uganda, Bosnia and Herzegovina down from 52% to 48% against Romania, and Congo Republic up from 41% to 44% against Comoros. Lesotho v Gabon (Gabon 65%) and Mauritania v Burkina Faso (Burkina Faso 37%) now have markets and are added to the table. Line-ups are not out yet; they are usually confirmed about an hour before kick-off.</p>", "<h2>Nations League: the final night</h2>", "<h3>Denmark v Norway · 22:45 (19:45 UK)</h3><p>The game of the night. Going into the final round, UEFA's own table has <b>Portugal 12 points</b> and already through, <b>Denmark 9</b>, <b>Norway 6</b> and <b>Wales 3</b>. Denmark go through to the quarter-finals if they avoid defeat. Norway need to win. A Danish win, combined with a Portugal defeat in Wales, would even lift Denmark to first place.</p><p>The market sees a close game: <b>Denmark 40.9%</b>, the draw <b>23.9%</b>, <b>Norway 35.2%</b>. So the result Denmark need (a win or a draw) is about a <b>65%</b> chance, and Norway's route through is about one in three. Goals are expected: over 2.5 goals is about <b>65%</b>, and over 3.5 about 43%. Norway have to attack, and Denmark may need to answer, so that makes sense.</p>", "<h3>Wales v Portugal · 22:45 (19:45 UK)</h3><p>Portugal are through. Wales are confirmed fourth and will go into the League A/B play-offs whatever happens. Portugal finish first if they avoid defeat. The market makes <b>Portugal 67.8%</b>, the draw 18.3% and <b>Wales 13.9%</b>. With little at stake for either side, team selection could change things: rotated line-ups in dead rubbers are one reason favourites can disappoint in the last round of a group.</p>", "<h3>League B: Poland v Sweden, Ireland v Kosovo and more · 22:45</h3><p>Four League B games kick off at the same time. <b>Poland v Sweden</b> is the closest: Poland 41.2%, the draw 25.9%, Sweden 32.9%, with over 2.5 goals about 58%. <b>Ireland v Kosovo</b> is expected to be tight and low-scoring: Ireland 45.4%, the draw 29.8%, and under 2.5 goals about 61%. <b>Israel v Austria</b> has Austria as clear favourites at 54.2%. In <b>Hungary v Northern Ireland</b>, Hungary are about 50%, and the market makes under 2.5 goals the likeliest outcome at about 63%. <b>Ukraine v Georgia</b> (Ukraine 44%) and <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina v Romania</b> (Bosnia 48%, down from 52% overnight) complete the evening.</p>", "<h2>Africa Cup of Nations qualifiers</h2><p>Eighteen AFCON qualifiers are on Tuesday's card. They fall into three time slots: 16:00 Kampala (13:00 UK), 19:00 (16:00 UK) and 22:00 (19:00 UK), with Niger v Morocco at 23:00.</p>", "<h3>The 19:00 games</h3><p><b>Kenya v South Africa</b> stands out. Bafana Bafana are 52.4% favourites away to Kenya, Kenya 17.8% and the draw 29.8%, and under 2.5 goals is 64%. <b>Ivory Coast</b> are 56.2% away to Gambia, <b>Algeria</b> 56.9% away to Togo, <b>DR Congo</b> 70.5% away to Sierra Leone, and <b>Tunisia</b> 42.7% away to Libya, where the draw is 32.2%.</p>", "<h2>Asia and the rest</h2><p><b>Japan v Paraguay</b> (16:10 Kampala): Japan are 58.1% favourites, the draw 24.1%. Goals are a coin toss at 49% for over 2.5. Some bookmakers' early goals prices for this game were far from the sharp line; that usually means a stale price that will be corrected quickly. <b>Qatar v South Korea</b> (17:00): Korea 65.6%. <b>Jordan v Australia</b> (19:00): Australia 53.8% away. <b>Iraq v Iran</b> (19:15): Iran 49.8%, and under 2.5 goals 62%. In the King's Cup, <b>Thailand</b> are 51.2% against Tajikistan, and in the SAFF Championship knockouts <b>India v Pakistan</b> (17:00) has India at 45.9%.</p><p>At 04:00 Kampala time on Tuesday, Canada play Guatemala in the CONCACAF Nations League (Canada 83.9%). In England, the EFL Trophy has <b>Luton v Peterborough</b> at 22:00 (Luton 61.8%, over 2.75 goals 66%).</p>", "<p class=\"note\">18+. Chances are from Pinnacle prices with the margin removed, from BetMeisters' scan at about 7pm Kampala time on 16 November. Prices and chances move; check before you bet. No affiliate links. Please gamble responsibly: BeGambleAware.org or 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["UEFA: Nations League quarter-finals, who is through and who can join them (16 Nov 2026)", "https://www.uefa.com/uefanationsleague/news/02ab-22096ba64f4d-f179d6d05013-1000--nations-league-quarter-finals-who-is-through-who-can-joi/"], ["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan, 16 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "previewType": "racing-card", "updated": "2026-11-17T07:35:00+03:00", "id": "racing-card-1711", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Racing card · Tuesday 17 November", "title": "Tuesday's racing: Skelton's Carlisle raid, Southwell under lights and a big-field Limerick card", "dek": "Three meetings and 25 races. Dan Skelton sends six to Carlisle, with Bossman Jack and Turbocharged odds-on. Southwell races into the evening and Limerick has five 17-runner fields. Every race, with the early market and the angles that matter.", "date": "2026-11-16T19:11:00+03:00", "read": 8, "body": ["<p>Tuesday's racing in Britain and Ireland is spread across three meetings: <b>Carlisle</b> over jumps in the early afternoon, <b>Limerick</b> over hurdles at the same time, and <b>Southwell</b> on the all-weather from 4.30pm into the evening. That is 25 races in all, from 11.50am to 8.30pm UK time (2.50pm to 11.30pm in Kampala).</p><p>The prices below are <b>early prices</b>, taken on Monday evening from the main UK and Irish bookmakers. At this stage only a handful of firms price each race, so the market is thin and will move a lot by the morning. We show the favourite and the best early price, and we explain where the form and the trainers stand. We add fair chances (the market with the bookmakers' margin taken out) once at least six bookmaker groups price a race, which usually happens by mid-morning. Our figures come from The Racing API racecards and its bookmaker feed.</p>", "<p class=\"note\"><b>Updated 07:35 Kampala time, Tuesday.</b> The market has filled out overnight: 16 to 19 bookmaker groups now price most races (Carlisle's 1.25 still has only two). The main moves: at Carlisle, <b>Doubly Blessed</b> (12.50) has been backed from about 5/4 into 4/5, a fair chance of about 50%; <b>Bossman Jack</b> (12.20) is about 8/13, a fair chance of 58%, with Kaka's Cousin now second favourite at 5/1; <b>Coconut Man</b> is joint favourite with They're Chancers at 3/1 in the 11.50; <b>Doyen du Bar</b> is now a clear 2/1 favourite in the 2.25 (27%); and in the 3.30 <b>Air Apparent</b> (about 15/8) has edged ahead of Mr Lincoln (2/1). At Limerick, <b>Detroit Maverick</b> (12.40) is about 10/11, a fair chance of 44%. At Southwell, <b>Hayate</b> in the 8.00 is the evening's shortest favourite at about 8/15 (60%). Several Limerick races list more declared horses than the field size, so some will not run: check the final field before you bet.</p>", "<h2>Carlisle: the Skelton show</h2><p>Carlisle stages eight races over hurdles and fences. The story of the day is <b>Dan Skelton</b>. His yard is in red-hot form: 19 winners from 78 runners in the last 14 days, a strike rate of about 24%, the best of any trainer at the three meetings. He sends runners to six of the eight races, with Harry Skelton riding most of them, and three of them are favourites in the early betting.</p>", "<h3>11.50 · Maiden hurdle, 2m3f (15 runners, Class 4)</h3><p>A big field of maidens opens the card. <b>They're Chancers</b> (Nicky Richards, Danny McMenamin) is the early favourite at up to 3/1. His form figures read 1/2 and he has not been seen for 323 days, so fitness is a question. <b>Coconut Man</b> (Skelton) is next at around 9/2, and Lucinda Russell and Michael Scudamore's <b>Ar Ais Aris</b>, second last time out 33 days ago, is a similar price. Russell and Scudamore are also in good form, with 9 winners from 37 runners in 14 days. With 15 runners, many of them unexposed, this is a race for small stakes at most.</p>", "<h3>12.20 · Novices' chase, 2m3f (6 runners, Class 3)</h3><p>The best race on the card on official ratings. <b>Bossman Jack</b> is rated 148, 14lb clear of the rest of the field, and is odds-on at around 4/6. His last-season form figures read 31161. The catch is the usual one with novice chasers: jumping fences at speed is a new skill, and a horse that falls or makes a bad mistake loses regardless of his rating. He has also been off for 220 days. His stablemate <b>A Pai de Nom</b> (rated 134) is second favourite at around 13/2, with <b>Kalkbrenner</b> (Ben Pauling, 134) at 7/1. At 4/6 the market makes Bossman Jack a strong favourite, but not a certainty.</p>", "<h3>12.50 and 1.25 · Novices' hurdles, 2m1f (two divisions)</h3><p>The race is split into two divisions, and Skelton has the favourite in both. In the first, <b>Doubly Blessed</b> (form 12) is around 5/4, with Donald McCain's <b>Throatlash</b>, rated 117, who won three in a row before a fall, next at 9/2. In the second, <b>Turbocharged</b> is the shortest-priced runner of the day at around 4/9. He won the second of his two starts last season. The main danger on ratings is <b>Bohemond Antioch</b> (McCain, rated 113) at around 6/1.</p><p>Both divisions are qualifiers for the 'Go North' Night Nurse series, so local trainers have a reason to run their better young horses here. Odds-on novices are often the most reliable favourites in jump racing, but 4/9 still means the market expects him to get beaten roughly three times in ten.</p>", "<h3>1.55 · Handicap hurdle, 3m1f (12 runners, Class 5)</h3><p>The marathon of the card, and a genuinely open race. Max Comley's <b>King Berry</b> is favourite at around 7/2, with <b>Coventry</b> (Lisa Harrison) next. Coventry has won two of his last three races, form 171, and ran 17 days ago. <b>Half Track</b> and <b>Ebselysees</b>, the top-weight on 100, are both around 9/2 to 8/1. Over three miles in November at Carlisle, stamina will count.</p>", "<h3>2.25 · Handicap hurdle, 2m1f (17 runners, Class 4)</h3><p>A full field. Skelton's <b>Razors Edge</b> (rated 113, off for 323 days) and Nicky Richards' 10-year-old <b>Doyen du Bar</b> (rated 122, last-time-out winner) share favouritism at around 3/1. The top-rated pair, Doyen du Bar and <b>Mick Collins</b> (122), carry the most weight. In a 17-runner handicap the favourite wins far less often than in a novice race, so the each-way terms matter as much as the win price.</p>", "<h3>3.00 · Handicap hurdle, 2m1f (17 runners, Class 5)</h3><p>The most open race of the day: no horse is shorter than about 6/1 in the early betting. Skelton's <b>Holeheartedly</b> heads the market despite form of 6088-7, which suggests the market expects a big step forward. Rebecca Menzies' <b>Bruce Banner</b> (second eight days ago) and <b>Raffles Wonder</b> are next, both around 13/2.</p>", "<h3>3.30 · Conditional jockeys' handicap chase, 2m (6 runners, Class 5)</h3><p>The card closes with a small-field chase for conditional riders. Iain Jardine's <b>Mr Lincoln</b> won last time and is favourite at around 7/4. Seamus Mullins' <b>Air Apparent</b>, also a last-time-out winner and top-rated on 105, is around 11/4.</p>", "<h2>Limerick: big fields, yielding ground</h2><p>Limerick's eight races are all over hurdles or in a bumper, on <b>yielding</b> ground. Five of them have 17 runners, so expect big prices and generous each-way terms. <b>Gordon Elliott</b> is the trainer to note: 12 winners from 73 runners in 14 days.</p>", "<h3>12.10 and 12.40 · Maiden hurdles</h3><p>In the 12.10 (2m2½f), <b>Poet's Duke</b> (Paul Nolan) is favourite at around 7/4 despite form of 75-07, with <b>Con Did It</b> and Elliott's <b>Western Note</b> next. In the 12.40, an opportunity race for less experienced riders, Elliott's <b>Detroit Maverick</b> is the day's strongest Irish favourite at around 8/11. He has been placed in four of his last five starts without winning, so he is consistent rather than proven.</p>", "<h3>1.10 and 1.40 · Handicap hurdles, 2m</h3><p>The 1.10 is a mares' handicap. <b>Cathryns Ruby</b>, a last-time-out winner and top-rated on 108, is around 5/2, with <b>Jumbos Jet</b> (3/1) and <b>Galileo Springs</b> next. In the 1.40, <b>The Monkey Pole</b> runs again just five days after his last race and is favourite at around 7/4.</p>", "<h3>2.10, 2.40 and 3.15 · Staying handicap hurdles</h3><p>Three 17-runner handicaps that will decide many accumulators. The two divisions over 2m5f have no runner shorter than 7/2. <b>The Cobblers Wife</b> (Anthony Mullins) and <b>Ardera Gun</b> head the first. <b>Kayla's Friend</b> and Elliott's <b>Jonzac</b> head the second. In the 3m 3.15, Emmet Mullins' <b>Cock And Bull</b> is favourite at around 7/4. Mullins has two winners from five runners in the last 14 days.</p>", "<h3>3.50 · Ladies' pro/am bumper, 2m</h3><p>A flat race for jumps-bred horses, ridden by amateur and professional women riders. Gavin Cromwell's <b>Marble Hill</b> (form 2-3) and Elliott's <b>Todo Bien</b> are close in the betting at around 7/4 and 9/4.</p>", "<h2>Southwell: evening all-weather</h2><p>Southwell's Tapeta track takes over from 4.30pm with nine races. The going is <b>standard</b>, and every race is a rider-restricted race, so many of the jockeys claim weight. All-weather evening cards tend to have well-handicapped, recently raced horses, and the market is usually accurate.</p>", "<h3>4.30 and 5.00 · Sprints over 4½ furlongs</h3><p>The 4.30 is a 14-runner handicap over Southwell's extended five furlongs. Geoff Oldroyd's <b>Managing Director</b> is favourite at around 4/1, and nobody else is shorter than 13/2. The 5.00 is a five-runner nursery for two-year-olds. Richard Hannon's <b>Urumita</b> won last time and is around 2/1. <b>Undercover Affair</b>, also a recent winner, is top-rated on 82.</p>", "<h3>5.30 and 6.00 · Middle-distance races, 1m4f</h3><p>In the 5.30 handicap, Andrew Balding's <b>Fahidi</b> (won last time, claiming apprentice) is favourite at around 7/4. The 6.00 is a fillies' novice race. <b>Maneater</b> (Richard Spencer), second last time out, is around 3/1, but the Crisford-trained <b>Danseuse</b> and Harry Charlton's <b>Bintnaseefa</b> are close behind. Novices with only one or two runs can improve a lot, so this one is less predictable than its small field suggests.</p>", "<h3>6.30 to 8.30 · The evening handicaps and a novice</h3><p>At 6.30, Michael Herrington's <b>Anthropologist</b> has won his last two races and is around 3/1. Karl Burke's <b>Moving Shadow</b> is next. The two 7f divisions at 7.00 and 7.30 are open, except that Edward Bethell's <b>Edmond Halley</b>, a dual last-time-out winner, is around 6/4 in the second. The 8.00 novice has the best two-year-olds of the evening. Roger Varian's <b>Hayate</b>, already rated 90 after winning last time, is around 11/10, with William Haggas' unbeaten <b>Miss Deeds</b> (won on her only start) at around 7/4. The card closes with a 14-runner sprint handicap headed by <b>Methgal</b> at around 3/1.</p>"], "sources": [["The Racing API: Carlisle, Limerick and Southwell racecards, ratings, trainer form and early bookmaker prices for 17 Nov 2026", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "previewType": "racing-card", "id": "racing-card-wolverhampton-1611", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Racing card · Wolverhampton (AW)", "title": "Wolverhampton tonight: the nine-race card, and where the favourites are short", "dek": "Monday evening on the all-weather: two Appleby hotpots in the novices, a busy handicap at 7pm and an evens favourite to close. Here is what the market says, race by race.", "date": "2026-11-16T17:41:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>Wolverhampton's Tapeta track hosts a nine-race card tonight, from 4.30pm to 8.30pm UK time (7.30pm to 11.30pm in Kampala). The going is standard. Every race is restricted to apprentice or conditional-type riders, so many of the jockeys carry claims. Below, for each race, we show the favourites and what the betting makes their chance once the bookmakers' margin is taken out.</p><p>A quick reminder of how to read the numbers: a horse at <b>2.00 (evens)</b> needs to win half the time to be fair value. If the market makes it 46%, evens is slightly short. If it makes it 55%, evens is generous.</p>", "<h3>4.30 · Class 5 nursery handicap, 6 furlongs (9 runners)</h3><p>A wide-open start. Lord Ragnar (Ed Dunlop) and Go Go Girl (Clive Cox) are both around 16% in the market, with Harry's Pop close behind. Nobody is shorter than about 4/1. When a race is this level, small price differences between bookmakers matter more than usual.</p>", "<h3>5.00 · Class 4 fillies' novice, 6 furlongs (13 runners)</h3><p><b>Desert Romance</b> (Charlie Appleby) is the clear favourite around 4/5, and our fair line makes her about a 52% chance, so the best price of 1.83 is a touch short of fair. Threelittleangels (Roger Teal) is the danger at around 5/2. Desert Romance is also a leg in one of the Original Combo Meister's trebles tonight.</p>", "<h3>5.30 · Class 3 novice, 8½ furlongs (9 runners)</h3><p>The shortest price of the night: <b>Leading Role</b>, another Appleby runner, at around 3/10. The market makes him about 72%, which means 1.30 is fair at best. Two John and Thady Gosden runners, Afif and Ever Since, are next in at 7/1 and 8/1.</p>", "<h3>6.00 · Class 3 novice, 8½ furlongs (9 runners)</h3><p><b>Al Lahbab</b> (Appleby again) is favourite around 8/11 to 4/6. Our fair line makes him about 55%, so the prices around 1.73 are close to fair. Kings Medal (Gosden) is second favourite around 7/2 to 4/1. Al Lahbab is a leg in the Original Combo Meister's double tonight.</p>", "<h3>6.30 · Class 3 handicap, 5 furlongs (11 runners)</h3><p>A competitive sprint with no clear favourite: Knebworth, Tan Rapido and Atomic Force are all between 4/1 and 13/2. Five-furlong sprints at Wolverhampton often turn on the draw and the break.</p>", "<h3>7.30 · Class 5 handicap, 8½ furlongs (12 runners)</h3><p>R P McMurphy (Michael Appleby) is a narrow favourite around 4/1 in a big, open handicap. The market makes nobody better than about 17%.</p>", "<h3>8.00 · Class 5 handicap, 9½ furlongs (12 runners)</h3><p><b>Ted Le Saux</b> (Andrew Balding) is favourite around 2/1 to 9/4. Our fair line makes him about 29%, which is nearer 5/2, so the shorter prices look a little skinny. Sea The Light and Felix Gem are next at 6/1 and 13/2.</p>", "<h3>8.30 · Class 6 handicap, 1m4f (11 runners)</h3><p><b>Saxophonic</b> (David Simcock) closes the card as an evens favourite. He has won two of his last three, and the market makes him about 47%, so evens is about fair rather than value. Havachoc is next at 7/1.</p>"], "sources": [["The Racing API: Wolverhampton racecards and bookmaker prices, 16 Nov 2026", "https://www.theracingapi.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "previewType": "daily-football", "id": "football-tonight-1611", "section": "previews", "kicker": "Daily football · UEFA Nations League", "title": "Nations League tonight: Germany's must-win, Scotland in Switzerland and Greece v Serbia", "dek": "The final round of the Nations League group stage, game by game, with the chances the sharp market gives each side.", "date": "2026-11-16T17:41:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>Monday night brings the last round of Nations League group games. The chances below come from Pinnacle, the sharpest bookmaker in the market, with its margin removed. They are the market's view, not ours, but they are a good guide to what is likely.</p>", "<h3>Switzerland v Scotland (19:45 UK)</h3><p>Switzerland are favourites at home, about <b>53%</b>, with the draw 25% and Scotland <b>22%</b>. The goals market is evenly balanced: over 2.5 goals is about a coin toss at 51%. For Scotland, a point would be a decent night away from home.</p>", "<h3>Estonia v Bulgaria (17:00 UK)</h3><p>A tight one in Tallinn. Estonia are slight favourites at about <b>50%</b>, the draw 29% and Bulgaria 22%. The market expects a low-scoring game: under 2.5 goals is about <b>62%</b>.</p>", "<h3>Lithuania v Liechtenstein and Iceland v Luxembourg (17:00 UK)</h3><p>Two big favourites. Lithuania are about 89% to beat Liechtenstein and Iceland about 82% to beat Luxembourg. At those chances the interest is in the margin, not the result: Lithuania winning by three or more is about 41% on the market, and Iceland by two or more about 57%.</p>"], "sources": [["Pinnacle prices via BetMeisters' scan, 16 Nov 2026 (margin removed)", "https://www.pinnacle.com"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football", "Horse racing"], "id": "betting-odds-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Betting basics", "title": "Betting odds explained: fractional, decimal and American odds, and what they mean as a chance", "dek": "Every price is a chance in disguise. Here is how to read fractional, decimal and American odds, convert between them, read each-way terms and price moves, and spot how much margin a bookmaker is charging.", "date": "2026-11-16T17:41:00+03:00", "read": 8, "body": ["<p>Odds look like three different languages: <b>5/2</b> in a betting shop, <b>3.50</b> on an exchange, <b>+250</b> on an American app. They all say the same two things: how much you win if your bet comes in, and what chance the bookmaker is putting on it happening. Once you can read odds as a chance, you can tell a fair price from a poor one. That is the single most useful skill in betting.</p>", "<h3>Fractional odds: the British way</h3><p>Fractional odds show your <b>profit</b> relative to your stake, without the stake. At 5/2 you win £5 for every £2 staked, so a £10 bet wins £25 profit and returns £35 in total, exactly the same as decimal 3.50.</p><ul><li>Profit = stake × (top number ÷ bottom number)</li><li>To convert to decimal: divide the top by the bottom and add 1. So 5/2 = 2.5 + 1 = 3.50.</li></ul><p>Odds-on prices are written the other way round: <b>4/6</b> means you win £4 for every £6 staked, which is decimal 1.67. 'Evens' is 1/1.</p>", "<h3>American odds: plus and minus</h3><p>American (moneyline) odds use £100 or $100 as the base.</p><ul><li><b>Positive odds</b> (+250) show the profit on a 100 stake. +250 means 100 wins 250, which is decimal 3.50.</li><li><b>Negative odds</b> (−150) show how much you must stake to win 100. −150 means you stake 150 to win 100, which is decimal 1.67.</li></ul><p>To convert: for positive odds, decimal = 1 + (odds ÷ 100). For negative odds, decimal = 1 + (100 ÷ the number without the minus sign).</p>", "<h3>Quick conversion table</h3><table class=\"rank\"><thead><tr><th>Fractional</th><th>Decimal</th><th>American</th><th>Implied chance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1/4</td><td>1.25</td><td>−400</td><td>80%</td></tr><tr><td>1/2</td><td>1.50</td><td>−200</td><td>66.7%</td></tr><tr><td>4/6</td><td>1.67</td><td>−150</td><td>60%</td></tr><tr><td>Evens (1/1)</td><td>2.00</td><td>+100</td><td>50%</td></tr><tr><td>6/4</td><td>2.50</td><td>+150</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>2/1</td><td>3.00</td><td>+200</td><td>33.3%</td></tr><tr><td>5/2</td><td>3.50</td><td>+250</td><td>28.6%</td></tr><tr><td>4/1</td><td>5.00</td><td>+400</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>9/1</td><td>10.00</td><td>+900</td><td>10%</td></tr></tbody></table>", "<h3>Turning odds into a chance</h3><p>This is the part most punters skip. Every price implies a probability:</p><p><b>Implied chance = 1 ÷ decimal odds</b></p><p>So 2.00 implies 50%, 4.00 implies 25% and 1.25 implies 80%. If you think something happens more often than the price implies, the price is good value for you. If it happens less often, the bookmaker has the better of it.</p><p>A worked example: a team is priced at 2.50, which implies 40%. If the honest chance of that team winning is 45%, then over many bets like this you would expect to win: 45% of the time you collect 2.50, which averages 1.125 back for every 1 staked, a 12.5% edge. If the true chance is only 35%, you average 0.875 back, a 12.5% loss.</p>", "<h3>The overround: how bookmakers make money</h3><p>Add up the implied chances for every outcome in a market and you will get more than 100%. The extra is the bookmaker's margin, also called the overround or 'vig'.</p><p>Take a football match priced Home 2.10, Draw 3.40, Away 3.60. The implied chances are 47.6% + 29.4% + 27.8% = <b>104.8%</b>. That 4.8% is the margin: the bookmaker's built-in edge over everyone betting on that market.</p><p>Margins vary a lot. On BetMeisters' own price data for 16 November 2026, the average margin on main markets ran from about <b>8.5% at bet365 to about 10.2% at Paddy Power</b> across the four big UK firms we track. Sharp bookmakers and exchanges run much lower margins, which is why we use them to work out fair prices.</p>", "<h3>Odds-on, evens and odds-against</h3><p>Three phrases you will hear all the time:</p><ul><li><b>Evens</b> (1/1, decimal 2.00, American +100) means you win the same as you stake. The implied chance is exactly 50%.</li><li><b>Odds-against</b> means the price is bigger than evens: 3/1, 4.00, +300. You win more than you stake, and the implied chance is under 50%.</li><li><b>Odds-on</b> means the price is shorter than evens: 1/2, 1.50, −200. You win less than you stake, and the implied chance is over 50%. Fractional odds-on prices are often said backwards in a betting shop: 1/2 is called \"two to one on\".</li></ul><p>Odds-on does not mean safe. A 1/2 shot is about a 67% chance before the margin, so it loses about one time in three. String three of them together in a treble and the chance of all three winning is about 30%.</p>", "<h3>Each-way odds: the place fraction</h3><p>In horse racing and golf you will often see a price like <b>10/1 each way, 1/5 three places</b>. An each-way bet is two bets of the same size: one to win, and one to finish in the places. The place part pays at a fraction of the win odds.</p><p>Take a £5 each-way bet (£10 in total) at 10/1, with a 1/5 place fraction and three places paid:</p><ul><li>If the horse <b>wins</b>, the win part returns £55 (£50 profit plus the £5 stake) and the place part returns £15 (10/1 × 1/5 = 2/1, so £10 profit plus £5). Total return £70.</li><li>If it finishes <b>second or third</b>, the win part loses and the place part returns £15. Total return £15, so £5 profit on £10 staked.</li><li>If it finishes <b>fourth or worse</b>, both parts lose.</li></ul><p>The number of places and the fraction depend on the race type and the number of runners, and bookmakers sometimes offer extra places on big races. Those terms decide whether an each-way bet is good value, so always check them, not just the win price.</p>", "<h3>Early prices, starting price and why odds move</h3><p>Odds are not fixed. They move as money comes in, as team news or non-runners are announced, and as bookmakers react to each other.</p><ul><li>An <b>early price</b> is a price you take before the day or before the market settles. Thin early markets move the most.</li><li>In UK and Irish racing, the <b>starting price (SP)</b> is the official price at the off. If you bet \"at SP\" you get whatever that turns out to be.</li><li>Some bookmakers offer <b>best odds guaranteed</b> on racing: if you take a price and the SP is bigger, you are paid at the SP.</li><li>When a horse is withdrawn late, bookmakers can apply a <b>Rule 4 deduction</b> to winning bets on the rest of the field at earlier prices, because the race became easier to win.</li></ul><p>A move in the price is a move in the market's chance. When a horse goes from 5/4 (44% implied) to 4/5 (56% implied), the market now rates it much more highly. Watching how far and how fast a price moves tells you what the rest of the market thinks.</p>", "<h3>Odds on handicaps and totals</h3><p>The same rules apply to every market, not just who wins. On a football total of <b>Over 2.5 goals at 1.80</b>, the implied chance is 1 ÷ 1.80 = 55.6%. On a basketball spread of <b>−4.5 at 1.91</b> (American −110), the implied chance is 52.4%. Two-way markets like these are usually priced near 1.91 on each side, which is a margin of about 4.8%. That is why American bettors talk about needing to win about 52.4% of bets at −110 just to break even.</p><p>Some lines can land exactly. On a whole-number line such as <b>Over 3 goals</b> or <b>−2 points</b>, a result of exactly three goals or a two-point win returns your stake. Quarter lines like Over 2.25 split your stake across two lines, so you can win or lose half. Our guide to Asian handicaps covers these in detail.</p>", "<h3>Common mistakes with odds</h3><ul><li><b>Confusing return and profit.</b> Decimal odds include your stake; fractional odds do not.</li><li><b>Thinking a short price is a safe bet.</b> A 1.25 shot still loses one time in five.</li><li><b>Ignoring the margin.</b> Two bookmakers can both have a favourite at 'about 2/1', but 2.10 against 2.25 is a big difference over hundreds of bets.</li><li><b>Chasing long odds.</b> Big prices are exciting, but they usually carry the biggest margins.</li></ul>"], "sources": [["Shin, H. S. (1993), 'Measuring the incidence of insider trading in a market for state-contingent claims', The Economic Journal", "https://doi.org/10.2307/2234526"]], "updated": "2026-11-17T07:38:00+03:00"}, {"topics": ["Enforcement", "Licensing and regulation", "General news"], "sports": [], "id": "uk-enforcement-roundup-nov-2026", "section": "news", "kicker": "Enforcement · UK regulator", "title": "Paddy Power, Betfair and William Hill: what the Gambling Commission's register shows", "dek": "A £2 million settlement for the Betfair and Paddy Power licences, and a self-reported fine for William Hill. Here is what each action was for, and how it feeds into our reviews.", "date": "2026-11-16T17:12:00+03:00", "read": 6, "body": ["<p>The UK Gambling Commission publishes every fine, settlement and warning on its public register. We check that register for every operator we review, because a bookmaker's regulatory record says more about how it treats customers than its advertising does. Here are the most recent actions for three of the biggest UK brands, and what they mean.</p>", "<h3>Paddy Power and Betfair: £2 million settlement (17 December 2025)</h3><p>The licence behind <b>paddypower.com</b> and <b>betfair.com</b> is held by <b>PPB Entertainment Limited</b> (account 39426). The register records a settlement dated 17 December 2025, made up of a payment in lieu of a financial penalty, a public statement and the Commission's costs.</p><p>The total was <b>£2 million</b>, shared across four licensees: PPB Entertainment Limited, PPB Counterparty Services Limited, Betfair Casino Limited and TSE Malta LP. The register does not split the figure between them.</p><p>The failings were in <b>customer interaction</b>, the rules that require operators to spot customers at risk of harm and act on it. The register cites breaches of paragraphs 1, 4, 7, 8 and 11 of Social Responsibility Code Provision 3.4.3. The action followed a 2024 compliance assessment of the Betfair and Paddy Power remote licences.</p>", "<h3>William Hill: £82,687 fine (7 August 2025)</h3><p>William Hill's online site, <b>williamhill.com</b>, runs under <b>WHG (International) Limited</b> (account 39225), now part of evoke plc. The register shows one action: a financial penalty of <b>£82,687</b> dated 7 August 2025.</p><p>It was for a failure on <b>identifying individual remote customers</b>, a breach of paragraph 3 of Social Responsibility Code Provision 3.9.1. The register notes that the company <b>self-reported</b> the breach and co-operated with the investigation. That matters: a self-reported, relatively small fine is a very different signal from a large settlement uncovered by the regulator.</p>", "<h3>Ladbrokes and Coral: no actions on the register now</h3><p>The online licence for <b>ladbrokes.com</b> and <b>coral.co.uk</b> is held by <b>LC International Limited</b> (account 54743), part of Entain. Its register page currently shows <b>no regulatory actions</b>.</p><p>That does not mean a spotless history. In August 2022 the Commission announced that Entain would pay <b>£17 million</b> for social responsibility and anti-money-laundering failures, of which <b>£14 million</b> related to LC International. It was the largest penalty of its kind at the time. Under our scoring method, the regulatory record looks back three years, so the 2022 settlement falls outside the scored window. We still mention it in the review, because readers deserve the full picture.</p>", "<h3>How this goes into our reviews</h3><p>Every BetMeisters.news review scores <b>regulatory record</b> as part of the Licence module, on a simple scale: no actions in three years scores 10, one minor fine that has been fixed scores 5, and a major sanction or suspension scores 0.</p><p>We link straight to the register entry in every review, so you can check it yourself. That is the point: you should not have to take our word for it, or the bookmaker's.</p>", "<h3>How to check any operator yourself</h3><p>Search the operator's company name or account number on the Gambling Commission's public register. The <b>Regulatory actions</b> tab lists fines and settlements, and the <b>Domain names</b> tab shows which websites the licence covers. Our <a href=\"article.html#how-to-check-gambling-licence\">step-by-step guide to checking a licence</a> walks through it.</p><p class=\"note\">BetMeisters.news earns commission and fees from some operators. That never changes a score, a ranking or what we report from the register.</p>"], "sources": [["Gambling Commission register: PPB Entertainment Limited (39426), regulatory actions", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/public-register/business/detail/actions/39426"], ["Gambling Commission register: WHG (International) Limited (39225), regulatory actions", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/public-register/business/detail/actions/39225"], ["Gambling Commission register: LC International Limited (54743)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/public-register/business/detail/actions/54743"], ["Gambling Commission: Entain to pay £17 million for regulatory failures (17 Aug 2022)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/entain-to-pay-gbp17-million-for-regulatory-failures"]]}, {"topics": ["Licensing and regulation"], "id": "how-to-check-gambling-licence", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Licences", "title": "How to check a gambling licence: a step-by-step guide for UK, Malta, Curaçao and beyond", "dek": "A logo in the footer proves nothing. Here is how to check, in five minutes, whether a betting or casino site really holds the licence it claims, and what each licence actually protects.", "date": "2026-11-16T16:21:00+03:00", "read": 10, "body": ["<p>Every betting and casino site says it is licensed. Some are telling the truth, some hold a licence that does not cover you, and some are simply copying a regulator's logo. The Malta Gaming Authority has publicly named 15 websites that claimed an MGA licence they did not have, and it warned that such sites often show a slightly altered logo with no working link and no licence number.</p><p>The good news is that checking takes about five minutes, and you do not need to trust anyone's word, including ours. This guide shows you how to check a licence step by step, what to look for on the most common regulators' registers, and what each licence actually means for your money if something goes wrong.</p>", "<h3>Why the licence matters</h3><p>A licence is not a seal of quality. It does not mean the odds are good, the bonuses are fair or the support is fast. It means a regulator has checked who owns the business, requires it to follow rules, and can punish it if it does not. In practice, a good licence gives you four things:</p><ul><li><b>Someone to answer to.</b> A licensed operator must follow rules on fair games, advertising, money laundering and protecting vulnerable customers, and the regulator can fine it or take its licence away.</li><li><b>A dispute route.</b> Strong regulators require operators to offer free, independent dispute resolution if a complaint about your money is not resolved.</li><li><b>Safer-gambling tools.</b> Deposit limits, time-outs and self-exclusion, which in Great Britain must include GAMSTOP.</li><li><b>Tested games.</b> Random number generators and game returns are tested by approved labs.</li></ul><p>A weak licence, or none, gives you far less of all four. And a licence that does not cover <i>your</i> country gives you very little at all, because you are not the customer the regulator is protecting.</p>", "<h3>Step 1: find the licence details on the site</h3><p>Scroll to the footer of the site, or open its terms and conditions. A properly licensed operator will name:</p><ul><li>the <b>legal company</b> that runs the site, which is often different from the brand name;</li><li>the <b>regulator</b>;</li><li>a <b>licence or account number</b>.</li></ul><p>If the site shows a regulator's logo but no company name and no licence number, treat that as a warning sign. If the logo is meant to be clickable, as the MGA's is, click it: a genuine MGA seal links to a verification page on the MGA's own website, authorisation.mga.org.mt. Then go to the regulator's website yourself, rather than following a link from the site you are checking, and look it up on the official register.</p>", "<h3>Step 2: check the UK Gambling Commission register</h3><p>For anyone gambling in Great Britain, this is the one that matters. The Gambling Commission's public register is free and searchable by business name or account number. When you find the operator, check four things:</p><ul><li><b>Status.</b> The licence should be active. A licence can be suspended, revoked or surrendered, and an old entry may still appear.</li><li><b>Activities.</b> A remote betting licence covers online sports betting, and a remote casino licence covers online casino games. A sportsbook licence does not cover the casino, and the reverse.</li><li><b>Domain names.</b> The register lists the website addresses the licence covers. The site you are using should be on that list. This is the quickest way to catch a fake: a copycat site can claim a real company's licence, but it cannot add itself to the register.</li><li><b>Regulatory actions.</b> The register shows fines, settlements and warnings. A large operator with one old action is not unusual, but repeated failures on money laundering or customer protection tell you how the business is run.</li></ul><p>Two things often confuse people. First, a Gambling Commission licence covers <b>Great Britain</b>: England, Scotland and Wales. Northern Ireland has its own gambling law. Second, many brands share one licence. A single company can run several sites, and some brands run on a 'white label' basis under another company's licence. That is allowed, but the company on the register is the one legally responsible, and it is worth knowing who it is.</p>", "<h3>Step 3: check the Malta Gaming Authority register</h3><p>Malta is the biggest licensing hub for European online casinos. The MGA's <b>Licensee Register</b> is on its website and lists each licensed company, its licence number and the websites it operates. The checks are the same as for the UK: the company name should match the site's footer, the licence should be active, and the website should be listed.</p><p>An MGA licence is a serious licence with real supervision, testing and dispute routes. It is not a licence to serve every country, though. An MGA-licensed operator taking bets from Great Britain also needs a UK Gambling Commission licence, and many European countries now require their own national licence. If a site is MGA-licensed but you live in a country with its own licensing system, check whether the operator holds that country's licence too.</p>", "<h3>Step 4: Curaçao and the new LOK regime</h3><p>For years Curaçao was the cheapest and loosest place to get a gambling licence. A handful of 'master licence' holders issued sublicences to thousands of sites, and players had very little protection if something went wrong. That system has been replaced.</p><p>Curaçao's new gambling law, the National Ordinance on Games of Chance (known by its Dutch initials, LOK), came into force on <b>24 December 2024</b>. The old Gaming Control Board became the <b>Curaçao Gaming Authority (CGA)</b>, an independent public body with wider powers. By December 2025 the CGA said it had granted 604 provisional licences, 551 to operators and 53 to suppliers, and rejected 139 applications. It was also reviewing provisional licences to decide whether they qualify for a full licence, certifying game-testing labs and processing applications from dispute-resolution providers.</p><p>What this means for you: a Curaçao licence today should be a direct licence from the CGA, not a sublicence under an old master licence. Check the licence on the CGA's own website, check it is current and not just 'provisional' indefinitely, and check what complaint route the operator offers. A Curaçao licence is still a lighter form of protection than a UK or Malta licence, and many countries regard Curaçao-licensed sites that serve their residents as unlicensed.</p>", "<h3>Step 5: other regulators you will see</h3><ul><li><b>Gibraltar</b> (Gambling Commissioner, under the Government of Gibraltar) and the <b>Isle of Man</b> (Gambling Supervision Commission) license several large betting and casino groups. Both keep public lists of licensees.</li><li><b>Ireland</b> set up a new regulator, the Gambling Regulatory Authority of Ireland, under the Gambling Regulation Act 2024, replacing the old patchwork of betting and gaming laws.</li><li><b>Canada:</b> in Ontario, online operators are registered by the Alcohol and Gaming Commission of Ontario (AGCO) and contracted with iGaming Ontario, and the AGCO publishes the list of registered operators. Other provinces mostly run their own government sites.</li><li><b>United States:</b> online sports betting and casino gaming are licensed state by state, for example by the New Jersey Division of Gaming Enforcement. A licence in one state does not cover another. 'Sweepstakes' casinos generally operate without a gambling licence, under sweepstakes law, and a growing number of states are banning them.</li><li><b>Kahnawake</b>, Anjouan and other small licensing bodies license many international sites. Their protection is limited, and in many countries these sites are not legal.</li></ul>", "<h3>A worked example: checking Casumo in five minutes</h3><p>Here is the check we ran for our own Casumo review, step by step, so you can see how quick it is.</p><p><b>The footer.</b> Normally you start here. Our checking server cannot open gambling sites, so we started from the details published for the brand: Recro Limited, Gambling Commission account 61549. A reader would simply read them off the footer.</p><p><b>The register.</b> Searching the Commission's register for 61549 brings up Recro Limited with an active licence.</p><p><b>The domain.</b> On the domain names tab, casumo.com is listed as an active domain on that account. So the site we were looking at is the one the licence covers, and not a copy.</p><p><b>The record.</b> The regulatory actions tab shows no actions for Recro Limited. Casumo's earlier licence holder, a different company, was fined GBP 6 million in 2021. That is useful history, but it falls outside the three-year window we use for scoring.</p><p><b>The complaints route.</b> The terms should name an independent dispute-resolution provider. This is the one step we could not finish from our checking server, so it is on the list for our human reviewer.</p><p>Result: a genuine, active UK licence that covers the site, with a clean recent record. That is why Casumo scores 10 for licence strength and 10 for the domain match. None of it tells you whether the games pay fairly or the terms are reasonable. Those are separate checks.</p>", "<h3>Red flags that a licence claim is fake or weak</h3><ul><li>A regulator logo with <b>no licence number</b> and no company name.</li><li>A logo that does not link to the regulator, or links to a page on the casino's own site.</li><li>The <b>website is not listed</b> on the regulator's register, even though the company is.</li><li>The company in the footer does not match the company on the register.</li><li>The site accepts players from your country but holds no licence there, and says nothing about which countries it may serve.</li><li>Terms that name no dispute-resolution provider.</li><li>Pressure to deposit in crypto only, or to use a VPN. Using a VPN to reach a site that is not allowed in your country breaks the site's own terms and can be used to refuse your winnings.</li></ul>", "<h3>How BetMeisters.news uses licences in our reviews</h3><p>We only review and list operators that hold a genuine licence somewhere, and we show which licence and which countries on every review and every listing. Licence is the heaviest-weighted part of our core score, and we score it on four points: <b>licence strength</b>, whether <b>the licence matches the site</b> (the domain check above), the operator's <b>regulatory record</b> over the last three years, and whether there is an <b>independent complaints route</b>. You can see the evidence for each point, with a link to the register, at the bottom of every review.</p><p>We do this because a review is only as useful as the facts underneath it. A high score for games or bonuses means very little if the licence does not cover you.</p>", "<h3>FAQs</h3><p><b>How do I know if a betting site is licensed in the UK?</b> Search for the operator's company name or account number on the Gambling Commission's public register, check the licence is active, and check the site's web address is listed under its domain names.</p><p><b>Is an MGA licence valid in the UK?</b> No. A site serving customers in Great Britain needs a Gambling Commission licence, whatever other licences it holds.</p><p><b>Is a Curaçao licence safe?</b> Curaçao's new regulator, the CGA, has tightened the rules since the LOK took effect on 24 December 2024, but its protection is still lighter than the UK's or Malta's. Many countries treat Curaçao-licensed sites as unlicensed for their residents.</p><p><b>What if a site has no licence at all?</b> Do not use it. You will have no regulator to complain to and no guaranteed dispute route if it refuses to pay.</p><p><b>Does a licence mean the odds or games are fair?</b> A licence means the games are tested and the operator follows rules. It does not mean the prices are good. That is what our price and RTP checks are for.</p><p class=\"note\">18+ only. Gambling should be fun, not a way to make money. If it stops being fun, stop: support is available at BeGambleAware.org or on 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["Curaçao Chronicle: One year of the LOK, the first year under the new gambling law (24 Dec 2025)", "https://www.curacaochronicle.com/post/unknown/1-year-of-the-lok-an-overview-of-the-first-year-under-the-new-gambling-law"], ["MaltaToday: MGA cracks down on fake licences", "https://www.maltatoday.com.mt/sponsored/sponsored/131281/mga_cracks_down_on_fake_licenses"], ["Malta Gaming Authority", "https://www.mga.org.mt/"], ["UK Gambling Commission public register", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/public-register"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting", "Licensing and regulation", "General news"], "sports": ["Horse racing", "Football"], "id": "bookmakers-limit-winning-accounts", "section": "news", "kicker": "Explainer · Account restrictions", "title": "Why bookmakers limit winning accounts, what the regulator's data shows, and what you can do", "dek": "Hundreds of thousands of UK betting accounts are cut back or closed every year, and winners are far more likely to be hit. Here is how restrictions work, what the rules say and your real options.", "date": "2026-11-16T16:21:00+03:00", "read": 10, "body": ["<p>If you have ever placed a bet and found the bookmaker would only let you stake a few pounds, you have met an <b>account restriction</b>. Punters call it being 'limited', 'stake-factored' or 'gubbed'. It is one of the most common complaints in British betting, and for years the only evidence was anecdote. That changed in July 2025, when the UK Gambling Commission published data on restrictions across almost 15 million betting accounts.</p><p>This guide explains what the data shows, why bookmakers restrict accounts, what the law does and does not say, and what you can sensibly do about it. It does not tell you how to dodge restrictions with extra accounts or someone else's name. That breaks bookmakers' terms, can get winnings withheld, and is something we will never recommend.</p>", "<h3>What is an account restriction?</h3><p>A restriction is any limit a bookmaker puts on a customer for its own commercial reasons. It is not the same as the checks a bookmaker must make for money-laundering or financial risk, or the limits you set yourself as a safer-gambling tool. The Commission's data separates out four main types:</p><ul><li><b>Stake factoring.</b> The bookmaker cuts the maximum stake it will accept from you to a percentage of what an ordinary customer can bet. A stake factor of 10% means that where a normal customer could bet £500, you can bet £50.</li><li><b>Account closure.</b> The bookmaker closes the account for commercial reasons. You get your balance back, but you cannot bet.</li><li><b>Betting withdrawn, account kept open.</b> A stake factor of zero. You can still log in and perhaps use the casino, but you cannot place sports bets.</li><li><b>Market restrictions.</b> You are blocked from particular markets, most often horse racing, but can still bet elsewhere.</li></ul><p>A single account can be hit by more than one of these, and many punters hold several accounts, so the number of restricted accounts is not the same as the number of people affected.</p>", "<h3>What the Gambling Commission's data shows</h3><p>In early 2025 the Commission asked some of the largest online betting operators for data on every active account in 2024. That meant every account that placed at least one bet that year: 14,923,840 accounts in total. The results were published in a blog by its chief executive, Andrew Rhodes, on 23 July 2025.</p><p>The headline: <b>643,779 accounts, or 4.31%, were restricted in some form</b>. Of those restricted accounts:</p><ul><li>62.17% had a stake-factor restriction, the most common type;</li><li>51.69% were closed for commercial reasons;</li><li>19.15% had betting withdrawn while the account stayed open;</li><li>5.72% were restricted from particular markets only.</li></ul><p>The stake factors are severe. Among restricted accounts, 22.41% were cut to 1% or less of the normal maximum stake, 36.22% to between 1% and 9%, and 29.43% to between 10% and 49%. Only around one in seven could still bet half or more of the normal stake. In other words, once a bookmaker decides to limit you, it usually limits you hard.</p><p>The most telling figure is who gets restricted. Across all active accounts, 25.42% were in profit over their lifetime. Among restricted accounts, 46.78% were in profit. <b>Restricted customers were almost twice as likely to be winning.</b> That confirms what punters have long said: restrictions are aimed mainly at people who win, or who bet in a way that looks likely to win.</p>", "<h3>Is 4% the real figure?</h3><p>Many regular punters say the official figure feels low. The Racing Post's Big Punting Survey, answered by almost 10,000 bettors and published in February 2025, found that 31.9% had been restricted on one or more accounts in the previous 12 months, up from 25.6% in the survey's first edition in 2023. Including older restrictions, the figure rose to 43.6%.</p><p>The two numbers measure different things, and both can be true. The Commission counts accounts, including millions of casual accounts that place a few small bets a year and would never be restricted. The survey counts committed punters who chose to answer a betting survey: exactly the group most likely to bet in a way bookmakers watch. For the casual customer, restrictions are rare. For the regular, price-aware punter, they are common.</p>", "<h3>Why bookmakers restrict accounts</h3><p>A traditional bookmaker makes money from the margin built into its prices. Across a whole market, the prices add up to more than 100%, and that extra is the bookmaker's edge. Our own price data shows how big it is: on 16 November 2026 the average margin on main markets ranged from about 8.5% at bet365 to about 10.2% at Paddy Power, across the four big UK firms we track.</p><p>That model works when most customers bet at a disadvantage. It breaks when a customer consistently beats the price, for example:</p><ul><li><b>Taking early prices</b> that later shorten, a sign the punter knows something the price did not reflect;</li><li><b>Betting only when one bookmaker is out of line</b> with the rest of the market, often called value betting or arbing;</li><li><b>Using offers systematically</b>, such as matched betting, so the bookmaker pays out offer money without making margin back;</li><li><b>Following sharp information</b> in small markets where the bookmaker's prices are weaker.</li></ul><p>The Commission's own explanation is blunt: operators act in their commercial interests and manage their liabilities, and restriction rates vary with each operator's risk appetite, size and customer base. A large bookmaker that takes the market on prices it sets itself will act differently from one that copies others' prices.</p>", "<h3>Is it legal?</h3><p>Yes, in Great Britain, as long as it is done fairly and openly. The Commission has said it is <b>not in its remit to dictate how operators manage their commercial liabilities</b>, and that its data request did not change that position. A business can choose its customers, provided it does not discriminate on protected characteristics such as race or sex. Being a successful bettor is not a protected characteristic.</p><p>There is also no 'minimum bet' rule in Britain. Some Australian states require bookmakers to accept bets on racing up to a set amount to win. The 2023 white paper on reforming the Gambling Act did not propose anything similar, and nothing has been introduced since.</p><p>The Commission has, however, set out what it expects. Operators should be transparent at the start and throughout the relationship about how, when and why an account might be restricted. Customers should know about restrictions before they deposit or bet, and an operator whose business model involves severe restrictions should say so. The Commission has also said it is concerned that restrictions can push customers towards illegal, unlicensed sites, or into opening accounts in other people's names. Both undermine the protections that come with a licence.</p>", "<h3>When a restriction turns into a dispute</h3><p>A restriction on future bets is a commercial decision. A refusal to pay a bet that has already been accepted is a different matter, and that is where you have more protection.</p><p>One case reported in the UK press shows the line. A winning racing customer said bet365 limited her to stakes of £1 and froze a balance of about £54,000 while a dispute over her account was resolved. The case went to IBAS, the independent dispute service bet365 uses, and bet365 said it would abide by the ruling. The point is not who was right. It is that frozen winnings go through a formal complaints process with an independent body at the end, while a stake factor on future bets usually does not.</p><p>Under UK licence conditions, every licensed operator must have a complaints process and must name an approved alternative dispute resolution (ADR) provider, such as IBAS or eCOGRA. If your complaint about a settled bet, a voided bet or withheld winnings is not resolved by the operator, normally within eight weeks, you can take it to that ADR provider free of charge.</p>", "<h3>What you can actually do</h3><p>There is no trick that makes restrictions go away, but there are honest ways to protect yourself.</p><ul><li><b>Read the terms before you deposit.</b> Look for the sections on maximum stakes, maximum winnings, 'irregular betting' and the right to void bets. If a bookmaker gives itself wide powers to cancel winning bets, that matters more than its welcome offer. It is why every BetMeisters.news review has a Terms section, written good or bad.</li><li><b>Use betting exchanges.</b> On an exchange such as Betfair Exchange, Smarkets or Matchbook you bet against other customers, and the exchange earns commission on winnings instead of margin on prices. Exchanges do not restrict winners for winning, although very high earners can face extra charges, and prices in small markets can be thin.</li><li><b>Keep records.</b> Screenshots of bet slips, stakes offered and any messages about your account make a complaint far easier if winnings are ever withheld.</li><li><b>Complain properly when money is at stake.</b> Use the operator's complaints process first, in writing, then the ADR provider named in its terms.</li><li><b>Do not open accounts in other people's names</b> or use VPNs to get round limits. It breaks the terms, it is the most common reason for confiscated winnings, and it can put you outside the protection of the licence.</li><li><b>Watch your own limits.</b> A restriction is not a challenge to win your money back elsewhere. If being limited makes you chase, set deposit limits or take a break: support is available 24/7 from the National Gambling Helpline on 0808 8020 133.</li></ul>", "<h3>How we score this in our reviews</h3><p>Because restrictions matter so much to regular punters, our bookmaker reviews score <b>treatment of winning players</b> as part of the Terms module. We look at the bookmaker's own wording, at independent rulings, and at what complaint bodies and the press have reported, and we write about it plainly whether it is good or bad.</p><p>We also score <b>price fairness against the sharp market</b>, using our own daily price data. A bookmaker with low margins and fair prices gives every customer better value, whether or not they are ever restricted. A bookmaker with high margins that also restricts winners quickly is the worst of both. We think readers deserve to know which is which before they sign up.</p><p>For the record: BetMeisters.news earns commission and fees from some operators, and we say so on every page. No operator can buy a score or a ranking, and nothing in this article changes because of a commercial deal.</p>", "<h3>FAQs</h3><p><b>Why has my betting account been limited?</b> Usually because the bookmaker thinks you are likely to win over time, for example by taking early prices, betting when its price is out of line, or using offers systematically. Bookmakers rarely give a specific reason.</p><p><b>Can a bookmaker legally limit my stakes?</b> Yes. In Great Britain the Gambling Commission does not control how operators manage their commercial risk, as long as they do not discriminate on protected characteristics and are open about their policies.</p><p><b>Will I get my balance back if my account is closed?</b> A licensed operator should return your balance. If it withholds money, complain in writing and then take the dispute to the ADR provider named in its terms.</p><p><b>Do betting exchanges restrict winners?</b> Exchanges make money from commission, not from customers losing, so they do not restrict customers for winning. Some charge extra fees to their most profitable customers.</p><p><b>How many accounts get restricted?</b> In the Commission's 2024 data, 643,779 of 14.9 million active accounts, or 4.31%. Among regular punters answering a Racing Post survey, nearly a third said they had been restricted in the previous year.</p><p class=\"note\">18+ only. Gambling should be fun, not a way to make money. If it stops being fun, stop: support is available at BeGambleAware.org or on 0808 8020 133.</p>"], "sources": [["Gambling Commission: Commercial restrictions by betting operators (Andrew Rhodes, 23 Jul 2025)", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/blog/post/commercial-restrictions-by-betting-operators"], ["Racing Post: Staking restrictions and account closures, inside the Commission's data (23 Jul 2025)", "https://www.racingpost.com/news/britain/staking-restrictions-and-account-closures-inside-the-gambling-commissions-latest-betting-data-a5WCz2R9nPyW/"], ["Racing Post: Big Punting Survey, account restrictions on the rise (26 Feb 2025)", "https://www.racingpost.com/news/the-big-punting-survey/betting-account-restrictions-on-the-rise-as-two-in-five-report-being-limited-asfxC5q9xYDr"], ["inkl / The Guardian: bet365 and the £54,000 account dispute", "https://www.inkl.com/news/bookmaker-bet365-admits-mistake-in-wrangle-over-54-000-account"]]}, {"topics": ["Sweepstakes and prediction markets", "Licensing and regulation"], "id": "prediction-markets-court-split", "section": "news", "kicker": "Prediction markets · US law", "title": "Courts split on prediction markets: what the Kalshi rulings mean", "dek": "Three federal appeals courts, two answers. The question of who regulates sports event contracts may be heading to the Supreme Court.", "date": "2026-11-16T11:30:00+03:00", "read": 5, "legal": {"checked": "16 Nov 2026", "text": "Prediction-market sports contracts are offered in many US states under federal (CFTC) oversight, but several states are fighting this in court and rulings differ by circuit. Availability can change quickly. Outside the US these platforms may be restricted or treated as gambling; check your local rules. 18+ (21+ in some places)."}, "body": ["<p>Prediction markets such as Kalshi let users buy contracts that pay out if an event happens, including the result of a sports match. Kalshi argues these are financial contracts regulated only by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC). Several states say they are sports betting and fall under state gambling law.</p>", "<h3>Three courts, two answers</h3><ul><li><b>Third Circuit (April 2026):</b> in a 2-1 preliminary ruling, the court said New Jersey cannot stop Kalshi offering sports event contracts while the case continues, finding federal commodities law likely pre-empts state law. The dissent said the products are sports gambling that states can regulate.</li><li><b>Ninth Circuit (August 2026):</b> held that Kalshi's contracts are subject to Nevada's gambling laws.</li><li><b>Sixth Circuit (25 September 2026):</b> a unanimous panel ruled that Ohio and Tennessee can regulate the contracts under their gambling laws.</li></ul>", "<p>When federal appeals courts disagree like this, the US Supreme Court is more likely to take the case. Until then, whether a platform can offer sports contracts may depend on which circuit a state sits in.</p>", "<h3>Polymarket too</h3><p>New York has also sued Polymarket over alleged illegal gambling; Polymarket has said it will fight for its users.</p>", "<h3>What it means for you</h3><p>If you use these platforms, the rules where you live matter more than the platform's marketing. We will keep a state-by-state table on this page and date every check.</p>"], "sources": [["Al Jazeera: US court rules states can regulate prediction markets (25 Sep 2026)", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/25/us-court-rules-against-kalshi-says-states-can-regulate-prediction-markets"], ["Front Office Sports: Kalshi wins preliminary ruling in New Jersey", "https://frontofficesports.com/article/kalshi-wins-preliminary-ruling-new-jersey-sports-contracts/"]]}, {"topics": ["Sweepstakes and prediction markets", "Licensing and regulation", "Enforcement"], "id": "sweepstakes-crackdown-2026", "section": "news", "kicker": "Sweepstakes · US states", "title": "The 2026 sweepstakes casino crackdown, state by state", "dek": "At least six states passed new laws against dual-currency sweepstakes casinos this year, and some reach promoters and affiliates too.", "date": "2026-11-16T11:30:00+03:00", "read": 5, "legal": {"checked": "16 Nov 2026", "text": "Sweepstakes casinos are banned or restricted in a growing number of US states, including those listed here. Laws in several states also cover promoters and affiliates. BetMeisters.news does not link to sweepstakes casinos for readers in states where they are restricted. 18+ (21+ in some states)."}, "body": ["<p>Sweepstakes casinos use two currencies: one you buy for fun, and a second \"sweeps\" coin you can redeem for cash prizes. States have increasingly decided that this is online gambling by another name.</p>", "<h3>New laws in 2026</h3><ul><li><b>Indiana</b> (HB 1052): bans online dual- or multi-currency sweepstakes that simulate casino or lottery games, from 1 July 2026, with civil penalties up to $100,000.</li><li><b>Iowa</b> (SF 2289): signed 15 May 2026; sweepstakes are prohibited unless licensed or authorised, with cease-and-desist powers for the regulator.</li><li><b>Louisiana</b> (HB 53 and HB 883): from 1 August 2026 dual-currency sweepstakes count as illegal gambling by computer, with fines up to $100,000 and up to five years in prison.</li><li><b>Maine</b> (LD 2007): prohibits online dual-currency sweepstakes that simulate casino games, with fines of $10,000 to $100,000.</li><li><b>Oklahoma</b> (SB 1589): passed over a veto; violations are a felony, effective 1 November 2026.</li><li><b>Tennessee</b> (HB 1885 / SB 2136): signed 22 May 2026; operating, promoting or supporting online sweepstakes games is a consumer-protection violation.</li></ul>", "<h3>Not just operators</h3><p>A clear trend in the new laws is that liability reaches beyond the casinos to suppliers, payment firms, geolocation providers, promoters and media affiliates. That is why our sweepstakes coverage is news and explainers, and why any sweepstakes link we ever show will be checked against the reader's state first.</p>", "<p>Washington, D.C. is weighing a different route: a bill introduced in April would legalise regulated online casino games and ban sweepstakes casinos at the same time.</p>"], "sources": [["InfoLawGroup: Sweepstakes casino laws in 2026", "https://www.infolawgroup.com/insights/2026/7/27/sweepstakes-casino-laws-in-2026-new-bans-proposed-legislation-and-regulatory-trends"], ["Gambling Insider: Sweepstakes casino monthly update, May 2026", "https://www.gamblinginsider.com/news/162789/sweepstakes-casino-monthly-update-may-2026-tennessee-louisiana-oklahoma-iowa"]]}, {"topics": ["Online casino"], "id": "rtp-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Casino · Explainer", "title": "RTP explained: what 96% really means", "dek": "Return to player is the most quoted number in online casinos and the most misunderstood. Here it is in plain numbers.", "date": "2026-11-16T11:30:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p><b>RTP (return to player)</b> is the share of all money staked on a game that is paid back as winnings over a very large number of plays. A slot with 96% RTP keeps, on average, 4p of every £1 staked. That 4% is the house edge.</p>", "<h3>What it does not mean</h3><ul><li>It does not mean you get £96 back from £100. In one session you can lose everything or win big; RTP only shows up over millions of spins.</li><li>It does not tell you how bumpy the ride is. That is <b>volatility</b>: a high-volatility slot pays rarely but big, a low one pays small and often, at the same RTP.</li></ul>", "<h3>The number that matters: cost per hour</h3><p>Expected loss = stake per spin × spins per hour × house edge. At £1 a spin, 500 spins an hour and a 4% edge, the average cost is about <b>£20 an hour</b>. At 94% RTP it is £30. Thinking in cost per hour is the most honest way to compare games.</p>", "<h3>Check the version</h3><p>Many games are released in several RTP versions, and a casino can choose which one to run. A licensed casino must show the RTP in the game's information panel. Our casino reviews will record the RTP the casino actually runs, not the maximum the developer offers.</p>", "<p class=\"rg-note\">Casino games always have a house edge. Set a limit before you play, and stop when you reach it.</p>"], "sources": []}, {"topics": ["Crypto", "Licensing and regulation"], "id": "crypto-casino-licences", "section": "news", "kicker": "Crypto · Explainer", "title": "Crypto casinos: how to check a licence before you play", "dek": "Many crypto casinos hold an offshore licence and are not allowed to take players from the UK or most of the US. Here is how to check.", "date": "2026-11-16T11:30:00+03:00", "read": 4, "legal": {"checked": "16 Nov 2026", "text": "Most crypto casinos are licensed offshore (for example Curaçao or Anjouan) and exclude players from the UK, the US and other countries in their terms. Playing from a restricted country can mean winnings are withheld. BetMeisters.news only lists operators with a licence we can verify, and shows the countries they accept."}, "body": ["<p>A licence is the first thing to check at any casino, and it matters even more with crypto, where deposits are hard to reverse.</p>", "<h3>Three checks</h3><ol><li><b>Find the licence number</b> in the site footer and check it on the regulator's own website, not just the badge on the casino page.</li><li><b>Read the restricted countries</b> in the terms. If your country is listed, do not play: the casino can refuse to pay out.</li><li><b>Check how disputes work.</b> UK and US state regulators have formal complaint routes. Many offshore licences offer far less protection.</li></ol>", "<h3>Crypto-specific risks</h3><p>The value of your coins can change while they sit in a casino balance, withdrawals can be held for checks, and on-chain payments cannot be charged back. Treat a crypto casino deposit as money you can afford to lose entirely.</p>", "<p>Every crypto operator we review will show its licence, the regulator, and the countries it accepts and excludes, in that order, before anything else.</p>"], "sources": []}, {"topics": ["Bonuses, rewards and VIP", "Online casino", "Licensing and regulation"], "id": "uk-bonus-rules-10x-cap", "section": "news", "kicker": "Casino · UK rules", "title": "UK casino bonuses: the 10x wagering cap explained", "dek": "Since 19 January 2026 UK bonuses can ask you to re-stake winnings no more than ten times, and bet-plus-casino offers are banned.", "date": "2026-11-16T13:30:00+03:00", "read": 4, "legal": {"checked": "16 Nov 2026", "text": "These rules apply to operators licensed by the UK Gambling Commission when they offer bonuses to UK customers. Other regulators set their own rules: an offer with higher wagering may be legal elsewhere, but it cannot be offered to UK players."}, "body": ["<p>Wagering requirements are the number of times you must stake a bonus, or bonus winnings, before you can withdraw. For years many UK casino offers asked for 30x, 40x or even 50x. Since <b>19 January 2026</b>, the UK Gambling Commission caps this at <b>10x</b>.</p>", "<h3>What changed</h3><ul><li><b>Wagering capped at 10x.</b> A GBP 10 bonus at the old 50x meant GBP 500 of play before a withdrawal; at 10x it is GBP 100.</li><li><b>No mixed-product offers.</b> Operators can no longer offer promotions that need you to use more than one product, such as betting and slots, to unlock a bonus.</li></ul>", "<p>The Commission's stated aims are less harm, less complexity and clearer terms, while keeping bonuses available.</p>", "<h3>What it means for you</h3><p>A UK offer showing more than 10x wagering is either out of date or not aimed at UK players. That is a quick test of whether a comparison site keeps its offers current. On BetMeisters.news we only show an offer where it is legal for the reader, with its key terms beside it, and we re-check every offer's date.</p>", "<h3>What has not changed</h3><p>The cap does not make a bonus free money. Maximum bet rules, expiry dates and game contribution still matter, and the house edge still applies to every spin.</p>", "<p class=\"rg-note\">A bonus is not a reason to gamble more than you planned. Set a limit before you play.</p>"], "sources": [["UK Gambling Commission: Gambling promotions to be safer and simpler", "https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/gambling-promotions-to-be-safer-and-simpler"]]}, {"topics": ["Sports betting", "General news"], "sports": ["Football", "Tennis", "Basketball"], "id": "uk-bookie-prices-snapshot-1611", "section": "news", "kicker": "Our data · Bookmaker prices", "title": "bet365 v Paddy Power v William Hill v Ladbrokes: who had the best prices today?", "dek": "A one-day snapshot from BetMeisters' own price data: 336 main-market selections across football, tennis and basketball.", "date": "2026-11-16T13:30:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>Every hour BetMeisters compares UK bookmaker prices with the sharpest market in the world, Pinnacle, after taking out its margin. Here is what one scan on Monday 16 November showed for four big UK bookmakers on main markets (match result in football, winner in tennis and basketball).</p>", "<table class=\"weights\"><thead><tr><th>Bookmaker</th><th>Average margin</th><th>Best UK price</th><th>Price v fair</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>bet365</b></td><td>8.5%</td><td>56% of selections</td><td>-10.7%</td></tr><tr><td>Ladbrokes</td><td>9.0%</td><td>37%</td><td>-12.3%</td></tr><tr><td>William Hill</td><td>9.4%</td><td>34%</td><td>-12.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Paddy Power</td><td>10.2%</td><td>27%</td><td>-12.1%</td></tr></tbody></table>", "<p><b>Margin</b> is the bookmaker's built-in cut on a market: lower is better for you. <b>Best UK price</b> is how often each one had the top price of the four (several can share it). <b>Price v fair</b> is the average price against the fair price: every bookmaker is below fair on average, which is how they make money.</p>", "<h3>Read it with care</h3><ul><li>This is <b>one day</b>, during an international break, so most games were internationals and lower leagues, where margins are higher.</li><li>We compare only these four plus the exchange. Other bookmakers are checked by hand in our reviews.</li><li>An average hides a lot: the best price on any one bet can come from any of them. Comparing before every bet matters more than loyalty to one bookie.</li></ul>", "<p>We will publish this table monthly on 30 days of data, and use it in our bookie reviews' value scores.</p>"], "sources": []}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Football"], "id": "value-betting-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Betting basics", "title": "Value betting explained: chance, price and edge", "dek": "The idea behind every, in three numbers you can check yourself.", "date": "2026-11-16T11:50:00+03:00", "read": 5, "body": ["<p>Most betting advice asks <i>who will win?</i> Value betting asks a different question: <b>is the price bigger than the chance deserves?</b></p>", "<h3>1. The price says what the bookmaker thinks</h3><p>Decimal odds of 2.00 imply a 50% chance (1 ÷ 2.00). Odds of 4.00 imply 25%. This is the <b>implied chance</b>. Add up the implied chances for every outcome in a market and you get more than 100%: the extra is the bookmaker's margin.</p>", "<h3>3. The edge: chance × price − 1</h3><p>If a UK bookmaker offers 2.00 on that 55% team, the edge is 0.55 × 2.00 − 1 = <b>+10%</b>. On average, over many bets like it, you would expect to make about 10p for every £1 staked. At 1.70 the edge is 0.55 × 1.70 − 1 = −6.5%: the same team, a bad bet.</p>", "<h3>Why value bets still lose</h3><p>A +10% edge on a 55% chance still loses 45% of the time. Edge only shows over hundreds of bets, which is why BetMeisters publishes every tip and every result, win or lose, rather than a highlights reel.</p>", "<h3>Three habits that help</h3><ul><li>Compare prices: the same bet is often 5–10% bigger at one bookmaker than another.</li><li>Use level stakes, so one bad week does not wipe you out.</li><li>Keep records. If you do not know your own results, you cannot tell luck from skill.</li></ul>", "<p class=\"rg-note\">Even a real edge is no guarantee. Only bet what you can afford to lose.</p>"], "sources": []}, {"topics": ["Sports betting"], "sports": ["Horse racing"], "id": "each-way-explained", "section": "guides", "kicker": "Guide · Racing", "title": "Each-way betting explained, with the maths", "dek": "An each-way bet is two bets in one. Here is how places, fractions and payouts actually work.", "date": "2026-11-16T11:50:00+03:00", "read": 4, "body": ["<p>An <b>each-way</b> bet is two equal bets: one on the horse to <b>win</b>, and one on it to <b>place</b> (finish in the top few). A £5 each-way bet costs £10.</p>", "<h3>Places and fractions</h3><p>The bookmaker sets how many places pay and at what fraction of the win odds. Common UK terms are 1/4 or 1/5 of the odds for the first 2, 3 or 4 places, depending on the number of runners and the type of race. Some bookmakers offer extra places on big races.</p>", "<h3>Worked example</h3><p>£5 each-way at 9.00 (8/1), 1/5 odds for 3 places:</p><ul><li><b>Wins:</b> win part £5 × 9.00 = £45; place part pays at 1 + (8 × 1/5) = 2.60, so £13. Total back £58 from £10 staked.</li><li><b>Finishes 2nd or 3rd:</b> win part loses; place part returns £13. Total back £13 from £10.</li><li><b>Unplaced:</b> both parts lose. −£10.</li></ul>", "<h3>When each-way makes sense</h3><p>Each-way is best value when the place part is generous: bigger fields where the terms pay more places than the race really deserves, or when a bookmaker adds an extra place. It is usually poor value on short-priced favourites, where the place part returns very little.</p>", "<p class=\"rg-note\">Each-way doubles your stake. Decide your total stake first, then split it.</p>"], "sources": []}], "operators": [], "static": true};
